      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>Illtttexti to flit (fbitor.</head>
        <p>Illtttexti to flit (fbitor.</p>
        <p>• NOTICE TO CI Tax EDITOR of The Examiner" invites corn spondence on all subjects of public interest. LErrisas should be brief and pointed. Their object must be to impart or seek information, not abuse.</p>
        <p>RRESPONDENTS. LETTEBB must in all cases be accompanied by the name and address of the writer. CommcmcAnoNs received early in the week— except urgent matters--can always be given the best attention.</p>
        <p>that and talks about manufacturers, he gets iomewhat incoherent. There may be works in Warrington which have trebled their output, hut not by running night and day, and no works in this town runs anything like the whole of its alant night and day, as Mr. Smith would have as believe. One or two may run a small por- tion, but even in these isolated cases it is not generally possible to turn out anything like as .nuch from that particular section during the .tight as may be turned out during the day. In 'act, there ig one firm supposed to be running part of their works night and day, but the men have frequently to leave at 2.30 to 4 o'clock in the morning. This, I suppose, Mr. Smith would say is trebling the output. Anyway, ha urtly tells me to look round myself. I have 43oked, and, failing to find; havt, asked for his assistance, as he seems to be so much better iu- formed. As to the Chancellor's name, I made no ex- cuses for using it, and need make none. He, at any rate, has seen the anomalies of the present system of rating, which Mr. Smith, in his own I mind, knows well are many and great, and instead of asking the manufacturers to per- petuate these, as Mr. Smith does, by paying higher.wages in order-that higher rents may be charged without question, he has made it cleat that he will strike at the root of the evil, and devise some means to pot matters on a more equitable basis. He might do this on the line of site values, but this would involve ground rents, and then I suppose we should be told of another "injustice to Ireland." Again, by judiciously withholding grants in aid, he might compel Corporations to exercise their powers in the way of providing funds for building pur- poses, which would do away with those incon- siderate mortgagees whom Mr. Smith is $o tender with, so I live in hopes of even Mr. Smith coming to a better frame of, mind regarding Government legislation. In conclusion, I did not overlook Mr. Smith's several reasons for increased rents, but I was more impressed with his methods of obtaining them. Let him make a stand against the tenant having to pay .£3 lie. 6d. to enable him to collect £1 Bs. 6d., which result can be obtained by a system of graduated composition, and then we can all wish him "more power to his elbow." A. HORNBY RATHBONE. Latchford Withont, January 15th, 1914.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>INCREASING RENTS.</head>
        <p>INCREASING RENTS.</p>
        <p>Sir,-1 think your readers will agree that Mr. Smith ought not to be allowed to slip out of this correspondence quite so easily as he slipped out of some of the arguments presented. The wily correspondent is no respecter of name Or "nom de plume." Both are equally at a disadvantage in this respect, consequently it has required the ability of a Scotland Yard expert rather than a rating expert to extract evidence anyways relative to this discussion. Writing as a landlord, his one grievance seems to be his inability to raise rents; whether tenants can afford to pay them or not is, a secondary consideration, but knowing so much about the difficulties of rent charges, Sze., why does he expect to succeed? Starting with an altogether ulterior motive, Mr. Smith has suc- ceeded in convincing most of your readers that cottage rents are higher than they ought to be, owing to the inability of landlords to recover paving charges by a more equitable and just method than that which now obtains. And seeing that not one tenant in a hundred would ever guess how he was being exploited, the thanks of all tenants are due to Mr. Smith for his exposure of the method by which it is being done. We are also indebted to him for the knowledge that. when once the paving charge is put on the rental, it is never taken off, but remains a permanent charge for ever. At the risk of repeating myself I will try to explain this method, and as briefly as possible. The average proportion for each cottage being .618, the landlord proceeds to levy a sum of 18s. per year upon the tenant—the said 18s. being the interest at five per cent, on the .tlB. That would be all very well but for one thing. The paving charges are no more rateable than the gas bill, therefore they cannot be recovere4 through the rates in the same way that in- creased rates are recovered. Let me say here, most emphatically, that to attempt to recover the tenant's share of the paving charges through the rating, and without rebate in full, is certainly unjust. The rent is raised high enough to carry the net rental 18s. higher, and, as everybody knows, that assessment rises with the gross rental, your readers will see that the tenant not only pays his rates oa a higher assessment, but pays a higher rent, and is levied an extra 18s. on the nk rental. Another way of putting it'is that the tenant must pay 545. per year, so that the landlord may recover 18s. per year extra in net rent. This is not all, for the way the 545. is divided is a tit-bit in itself. As near-as makes no difference, it is 275. to the rating authorities, and 275. to the landlord; this again in itself seems to explain why this method is adopted. The rating authorities get 275. for nothing, and the landlord 275., instead of 188. A very good I return, but for the fact that it all comes out of the poor tenant. I am. told that the. rating authorities have no option in the matter, so far as recovery through the rent is concerned, but that is poor consolation for the tenant; but the landlord gets more by this method than he asked for-71 per cent. -instead of five. For the life of me I cannot see why these charges cannot be' paid in the same way as the gas bill, or any other account the tenant may be responsible for. Surely the tenant who pays 6s. and upwards now would rather pay 180. direct than pay 545. through his rental. The callous indifference displayed by the authorities and landlord com- bined towards this injustice perpetrated on the tenants is not consistent with the common wel- fare of the community, and is not the least deplorable feature of the case. Like the meter rent charges, but of vastly greater importance,' I suppose it will be allowed to go on until the public conscience puts a stop to it.. I have done my best, with the help of Mr. Smith, to expose this scandal, and I have no wish to pursue the subject any further at present. 14th January, 1914.</p>
        <p>6t the .rating 4 'latter, so far</p>
        <p>FAIR RENT.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Mr. Smith's powers of perception are remarkable. They must surely have been stimulated by a visit to one of the Picture Houses, the effects of which had not worn off when he wrote his last letter. He imagines he sees me "ill-beset,"" in a state of high fever," "a rushing, seething mass," and "a sort of soup, a broth or brew." Rather -mixed as metaphor, but exciting enough if he had been describing a picture of the backwoods of Amer- ica, with himself as the Indian brave. How he does delight in setting up his own man of straw, and then valiantly proceeding to knock him down to his own self-satisfaction and glorifica- tion. In other people it might be given another name. Well, I will give him another opPortunity of spending a pleasant half-hour, seeing that-he is somewhat free in offering nie compensation, and ia this way. He has been arguing that he is entitled to a rent of 6s. 4d. a week on a house which has only been returning him ss.,'on the ground that he has. to pay £4 10s. 10d. in rates, plus, of course, 18s. for paving. Now, does he really pay .R. 4 10s. Mi. in rates on. this house which has been rented at 58. per week, even though he charges 6s. 4d. rent, or does he only pay £2 95. 3d. until the next assessment in March, 1915? I know his accountant's' certiff.-'1 eate stated that on a house rented at Os. 4d. a week, the rates amounted to £4. 10s. 10d.. but from when is the latter amount charged? Mr. Smith will no doubt appreciate my point, espe- cially when I suggest that 6s. 4(1. a week would give him a clear 7d. or Bd. a week profit over and above what a fair rent would bring him in, over a period of about 15 months. And are there not builders in Warrington charging a rent of ss. 9d. per week, in streets where houses of exactly the same kind on the other side are being let Id ss. 4d., the tatter returning quite a satisfactory profit to the landlord? And in regard to these speculative. bidlelers. I hazard the statement that they are having a pretty good time at present. Mind you, I have not argued that they are not entitled to some increase, but I do argue that in many cases the increases are excessive, and have put them in clover. Then, again, when they sell, they generally manage to create a ground rent, much on the "something for nothing" principle pro- pounded by Mr. Smith, and if they do happen to sell during the next twelve months or so, they stand a chance of obtaining about £l7O for a house which might be purchased at present for £145, that is to say, before the new assess- ment has been made, still retaining the ground rent as a sort of perquisite. Mr. Smith need not think I am envious. In- deed, I rather admire his acumen as far as pro- perty is concerned, but when he gets away from</p>
        <p>land—as a supporter of Disestablishmenr.. Controversialists ought to be careful to verify their facts (before rushing into print), especially ' when so important, and so easily verified, as the case in point. Every schoolboy knows when "the final sub- jugation of the Welsh" took place, but what has that to do with the establishment, environ- ment and continuity of the Celtic or British Church? And every schoolboy knows also,•that from Ireland, through Man, came a stream of mi*donaries guided by the splendid pioneer work of St. Patrick. Some of these established themselves in Wales, and many more in Eng- land. Both the Celtic and the Anglo-Saxon Church owe much -to St. ,Patrick and the Irish Church. It would give me the greatest pleasure to have a conversation (I will not say "contro- versy ") with Mr. Barker on the ancient Irish Church and St. Patrick. ,</p>
        <p>Mr. Barker now adds exceedingly to the per- formances of Henry VIII. Your readers were informed last week that this monarch had established a Church; they are now inforped that he "assumed Papal authority" " A number of books are quoted to (presumably) prove all this. By appoiatment Mr. Barker and I might go through these "authorities," and many more—to see if such things be so. But, if he is a Liberal and a Government man, per- haps it would be more convincing, as it cer- tainly would be simpler, to quote Mr. Ellis Griffiths, who acknowledges that " disestab- lishment, therefore, is not the repeal of an Act of Parliament, but the fundamental modifica- tion of the status now enjoyed by the Church " i.e., he owns that he cannot find any Act estab- lishing the Church. If Mr. Ellis Griffiths cannot find that Henry VIII. established a Church (or Aid all those other wonderful things) I may be forgiven, I think, if I doubt Mr. Barker's ability to do so_</p>
        <p>Some excellent remarks were made 1n Mr. .Barker's first letter, Which I ackno!sileclge to .be -true; iemy-view,-In respeet to Church Reform. But my_ pleasure seems to. have hardly been taken as I hoped, for now, in reference thereto, he comes-out with a very different note indeed, one can with difficulty understand the two writers being the same ! I now read that "Church Reform, so-called, is merely tinkering with disease which goes to the roots of the Church, the only remedy for which is a com- plete and. ruthless re-organisation from top to bottom." When I read this tremendous sen- tencillEW•lis. impoirsible-te avoid using Dditiltde Sampson's exclamation--"*Prodigious!" Mr, Barker wants to "reform" the Church of Eng- land out of existence. Is lie a Roman Catholic? And, sir, is he "a good, sound Liberal"? At any rate, he now begs me to remember that \he has never advocated that the funds of the Dis- endowed Church should be given to the County Councils for administration. This, however, Is what the Bill intends to do which Mr. Barker started to defend! Further, he tells me, and Your other readem that the speech which pro- voked this correspondence was not intended to express the views of the Liberal party—although it was delivered at a. meeting of the Lymm ' Liberal Association !-- I. thought I was contro- vertin,g statements made by a high Char.-Aim:in. a.. Liberal, and an exponent and supporter of the G especially) co ;tnttel:san(dt'h lastisendow One- m oernert Inment 151ri! mpelseata Bartiej:lsunl me to think that he is, exactly, neither one nor the other! C. STIDSTON-BROADBENT. Lymm, January 14th. 1914.</p>
        <p>25 ONTARIO acres vir- gin soil In CANADA the land of golden opportunities—a week from Liverpool. Improved freehold tarsus for what British farmers . pay in rent. Dairying, cattle raising, fruit growing, gardening. Unlimited market. Grand climate. Thousands a/ acres in peaches. Idea! social and educational conddions. Government guarantees r ' farm hands and domestics siturions. Good wages , Marvellous natural resources await nrestors. , . Writes R. REID. . • Ontario Government Agent. 163. Strand. London,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>DISESTABLISHMENT AND DISENDOW-</head>
        <p>DISESTABLISHMENT AND DISENDOW-</p>
        <p>MENT.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l note that Mr. Reginald Barker ack- nowledges his mistake in so emphatically claiming Lord Halifax-r-the leader of tho ex-</p>
