      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>CROSS COUNTRY.</head>
        <p>CROSS COUNTRY.</p>
        <p>Warrington A.C. had a good muster for an eight miles' run over mixed country. Tho load- ing points in the competitions were distributed as follows :—Novices: Baker 10, Potts 9; inter- mediate Scott 10, Hayes 9, Baker 8, Nurse 7, hunt 6, Potts 5; senior Hanson 10, Vose 9, Scott 8, Antrobus 7, Bolton 6, Parsonage 5.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>To-day's Fixture.</head>
        <p>To-day's Fixture.</p>
        <p>Meet at headquarters,, run out at 3.13 p.m., and the leading feature 'will be the semi-final stage of the senior points competition, in which the first six positions now are: Hanson 29, \rose 25, Scott 22, Holbrook 19, Antrobus 17. an&lt;l Par- sonage 17.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>DISCOUNT</head>
        <p>THE DISCOUNT</p>
        <p>"A CHILDISH POLICY."</p>
        <p>Mr. Jolley Freely Criticises the Board of Guardians.</p>
        <p>QUESTION OF BUILDINGS.</p>
        <p>The Office Sites and Building question was again the subject of a lengthy discussion at the fortnightly meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians on Wednesday. Mr. E. L. Bent presided and moved: "That the resolution of the Board fixing the site for the ne* Imbecile and Epileptic Block at the Workhouse be rescinded." Mr. Bent said he thought it was scarcely necessary for him to explain why he gave notice of that resolution, as many of those present were aware of his views on the site. He thought if they adopted the site behind the Board Room they would disfigure and dismantle the approach to the institution. There were two or three other sites in the grounds which they could use. He thought the matter ought to be further considered. Mr. Furness: The architect (Mr. Segar Owen) is here. Can we not have a statement from him as to whether the change of site would affect the contract, and, it so, to what extent? Mr. Mather: Have the foundations been laid? Mr. Owen The ground has been pegged out. Mr. Jolley: I suggest that the architect be not invited to give information without a decent reason for rescinding the resolution. I venture to say there are many of us who do not know the reason. TheShairman assumes we know his reason. Why this roundabout way should be used to kill a scheme that has been before us for some years, I cannot say. Don't put any respon- sibility on the architect; let the Board settle its own questions after proper discussion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>"WITHOUT RHYME OR REASON."</head>
        <p>"WITHOUT RHYME OR REASON."</p>
        <p>Mr. Furness: It is understood who is to have the contract, and now that it is proposed to alter the site I think merbbers of the Board ought to know before discussion whether this alteration will affect the contract.</p>
        <p>----------- Mr. Jolley :.The architect gave his opinion and all statistics to this Board and the Local Gov- ernment Board, and now, without rhyme or reason, we are asked to undo the work of years. Some reason should be given for that, and we have had none at all. The Chairman: My opinion is that if this building is erected on the present site it will be out of uniformity with the whole of our original resolutions. We have two good sites available besides this. Don't let us stint the scheme for the sake of .400 or £3OO. Let us make it a respectable building. I don't think we are doing that, so let us discuss the matter further. Mr. Jolley: The Board has discussed these matters and settled them. The present pro- cedure is so irregular and so unworthy of the traditions of the Board that I resent it.</p>
        <p>"THE MEDES AND PERSIANS." Mr. Furness: Our laws and resolutions are not like the laws of the Medea and Persians. When we pass a resolution and find that it is not in the interests of the Board surely it is no crime if we try to do something better and make a change. If you put the building on this site we shall make, a 'gigantic blunder. The airing courts are going to come in the central courts, and these being the worse class of patients, it IS the most objectionable site you could utilis&amp; for the purpose. There is no difficulty in finding another site. The site behind the new block is more preferable, because it is near the doctors' , :it'd nurses' quarters, and more " get-at-able" than this. The time will come when you will want an administrative block, and what more I suitable site could you have than this block for that purpose.</p>
        <p>A SEVEN YEARS' DISCUSSION. The Rev. A. M. Mitchell: Whatever you do, don't put your block by the railway, because I think that would be highly dangerous. I think it would be better to consider the amount in- volved, as well as the alternative site, and defer the mat+</p>
        <p>__,Apitstszti r. roo</p>
        <p>-1-;7Bibi qleThaTsciiss7.erthe- question for about seven years, and spent enormous sums of money in getting up-to-date ideas from other institutions. We have got the unanimous ap- proval of the Board and the Local Government Board, and then to throw the' matter back into the melting pot would be quite unreasonable. Mr. Dale: I understand that this site adjacent to the building had been approved to save cost. Mr. Furness: The scheme will go on all the same; it only means a change in site. Mr. Jolley: I would appeal to members not to lower the dignity of the Board by this childish policy. The question has been discussed for a long time, and now when we are within measur- able distance of these things coming about there is an attempt to "scotch" the whole thing— ("No")—and the result must be to defer it for a long period. These conditions ought to have been put right twelve months ago. The Local Government Beard will not settle this question in five minutes; it will probably be another year. I ask you to adhere to the line of action we settled on, after full discussion.</p>
        <p>RESOLUTION CARRIED. Mr. Mitchell: Which site gives the best oppor- tunity for opening out in the future? Mr. Jolley: The railway site. The Clerk: The matter has already been dis- cussed by the Local Government Board archi- tect and Mr. Owen, and Mr. Owen informs me they are already agreeable to having a new site. On a vote being taken the resolution was car- ried by nine•votes to five. Mr. Furness then moved: "That the site for the imbecile block be placed on the west side of the new block." Miss Broadbent seconded and was carried. _ the resolution</p>
        <p>On the proposition of Mr. Jolley, seconded by Mr. Brooks, it was decided "that the archi- tect be instructed to prepare •plans for such additional buildings as are necessary in conse- quence of alteration in the site now agreed upon."</p>
        <p>COUNTY BOUNDARIES. A-letter was read from the I".neal Government Board intimating that an inquiry would be held in regard to the question of fixing the county boundary line. • • Details of the scheme have appeared in "The Examiner" a short time ago. The Clerk explained that the date had not yet been fixed.</p>
        <p>The Chairman of the Assessment C,ommittee, along with the. Clerk, were instructed to repre- sent the 'Board.</p>
        <p>The application of Mr. E. J. Sutton, vaccina- tion officer,- for an increase in salary on account of increased work, was referred to the Finance Committee for .consideration.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. R. Jones wrote resigning his position as Assistant Schoolmaster and Drill Instructor at the_Pacigate Homes.'</p>
        <p>The resignation was accepted, and it was decided to advertise the vacancy. The salary is £6O per annum, and the emoluments amount to £4O per annum.</p>
        <p>HANDS BROKE OUT IN BLISTERS. Chi'worth, Rymee-road, Addiscombe, Surrey, Eng.—" During the frost last winter my hands broke out in small blisters and I thought they were chapped. So I used some ointment I saw advertised and instead of getting better they got so inflamed and the blisters spread all over my arms. The itching and burning were ter- rible. I could not rest dab- or night the first few weeks for the pain and irritation. The small blisters broke and wept very much. They got so had I was under treatment on and off about three months and my hands were bound up in I,was not able to use them. "I had tried so many different things I thought I would give Cuticura Soap and. Oint- ment a trial: I used them at once and the im- provement began. I continued using both Cuti- cura Soap and Ointment and lam free from all ot it now; lam cured:" (Signed) Mrs. E. M. Cutten= den, Aug. 10, 1913. Cuticura Soap and Oint- ment are sold by chemists everywhere. A sample of each with .22-p. book post-free. Address: F. Newbery and Sons, 27, Charter-1 house-square, London, or Potter Drug and Chem. Corp., Boston, U.S.A.</p>
        <p>A Baptist Sunday school is to be built along- side the Mount Olivet Church at Burnley, and the church, which has been used both as church and Sunday school, will be used as a_church building only.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>Dr. MARKEL'S SUGGESTED REMEDIES.</head>
        <p>Dr. MARKEL'S SUGGESTED REMEDIES.</p>
        <p>This article on smoke prevention and fuel economy is an abridgement of a paper con- tributed to " Progress seven years ago by Dr. Markel, and now brought up to date by him for "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>In it the oft-told story of the injury done to the community, the waste caused to manufac- turers, and the methods there are to hand of ridding us of a source of much discomfort and the manufacturers of much needless loss is again related—with the added weight of Dr. Markel' s authority.</p>
        <p>The great problem of the present day is un- doubtedly to reconcile our industrial growth with the higher interests of the workers,, and one of the greatest hindrances to its solution is smoke. The dangers and discomforts arising from the careless use of coal Were centuries ago apprehended by the thoughtful few, but where- as then mere thousands were affected, now, with the huge concentration in our towns, this monster has millions in its grip, taking toll of their health and happiness, of their property, and even of their lives. Only those who have been fortunate enough, to be brought tila in the country 'or who have experience of other lands can appreciate the full extent of the evil. The long-drawn monotony of the streets, dreary and sunless, peopled with sallow faces and stunted forma; the mean, dingy dwellings, dull and dirty without, often cheerless and neglected within; the almost brutal indifference to clean- liness and ventilation—indeed, to all the refine- ments of life—that cornea of Constant discour- agement and a misery in which nothing mat- ters; the disease and decay everywhere to be seen in industrial quarters; the absence of all God-given beauty; the dirty drizzles and dense fogs' of the winter alternating with the choking atmosphere of the dog days; all hese—wholly or largely the effects of smoke—which may be observed in scores of our English towns, repre- sent an enormous detraction from our national wealth, and from that health and vitality which is the nation's greatest asset. t That smoke is one of the great factors in the physical degeneracy of our urban population is a point Irwin which science and statistics leave us no room for doubt, for its nature and effects have been the subject of very careful observa- tion. What is smoke? So far from being, as many imagine, pure carbon, smoke is an aggre- gation of a vast number of impurities, of which carbon is the least important. When it has finally settled in the form of soot its most ob- jectionable constituent is a sticky oil, average ing 15 per cent. of the whole weight, but vary- ing considerably according to the origin of the smoke. By means of this tarry oil -the drier carbon • and ash soot constituents are made to adhere to and discolour brick and stone, fabrics, furniture and clothes. It is this tarry stuff, too, that is mainly responsible for one of the worst effects of smoke. F refer to its unpleasant connection with fogs. ,Not only is it often the direct cause of fog longs them and renders them vastly more dis- agreeable and more dangerous to health. Years ago the Air Ay:Fiji Committee of Man- chester reported:— . s. but it always pro- " Smoke; promoting as it does the formation of fogs, and preventing diffusion into the upper stratum of air,' must be regarded as the prin- cipal calla. ‘,4 the impure state of the atmos- tow-fig. 'At-the futme -time i% The be e was found by actual tests that during anticy- clonic periods the amount of sulphurous acid org impurities sed par passu. increased in the 'worst districta to St times the increa proportion that prevails in clear weather, and that anio effect on nulthofrinsug the such atmosphere may i he eff great London fog, January-February, Pa. when for many successive days the blackness and stillness was continuous, the detith-rate rose in a single fort- night from 27 .l -to . 481. The excess of deaths during three weeks ovcr the average was 2,994. The greatest mortality was from diseases of the air passages and lungs. 248 dying of whooping cough and 1,223 of bronchitis in a single week. The general high death-rate of our great towns is largely due to the baneful effects of smoke and the consequent fogs. The clean fogs of the country and of clean towns' on the other hand, produce no distress or disease, and quickly dis- appear. Attempts have been made to estimate the dainge due to smoke ,' The lion. Rollo Rus- sell sets down the annual loss to London in money alone at 45,200,00' This estimate in- cludes, of course, the coal wasted, which was calculated by Mr. Ernest Hart at 42,600000. a year on the basis of 42 per cent. of the total con- sumption, but takes no account of the losses due to deterioration of health and lowered vitality, or of the "moral and intellectual damage"; nor can it assess the damage done to priceless works of art. This, then, is the smoke evil.</p>
        <p>R'rl/;g,-t-wr-g-e</p>
        <p>As the whole mischief has arisen from an ignorant and wasteful use of our fuel resources, the first condition is to study (1) the nature, relative values, and suitabilities of various fuels; (2) the proper conditions and appliances for utilising them to the full. The more this is done, the more the truth will emerge that prevention of smoke always means economy of fuel. The theme so stated is a tempting one to Ilursue, but all I can do in this brief article is to suggest the possible lines along some or all of which reform may proceed- HOW TO DIMINISH DOMESTIC SMOKE.</p>
        <p>- •-•-• • -of Coke, Advantages First, as regards domestic smoke. Almost all this is occasioned by the careless burning of bituminous coal of the Lantashire type, and could be avoided by the use of coke (or anthra- cite, if available). With regard to coke, Sir Charles Cookson's testimony, given some time ago at a Smoke Conference, is interesting. Liv- ing in an old Chelsea house, with all sorts of grates, he had, he said, • burnt nothing but coke for six or seven ing advantages years, and lie found the follow- sent prices this about half the cost (at pre- would probably somewhat over- state the case); (2) no firewood; (3) can be lit at any moment; (4) gas (used for kinilling) gives (1) " heat from moment of lighting until coke begins to burn; (5) coke being always porous, the whole sort of (l mass glows and gives more heat than any other fire: A • no one-half, soot; (7) cleaning reduced by the adhesively dirty, like soot; (8) no sweeping of chimney necessary- The difficulty of lighting he overcomes by leading a gas pipe with a un Bunsen .burner der the bars. The cost of fine dust from coke not being this he reckons at a halfpenny a week for each fire; but several of my acquaintances who use coke simply start the fire in the ordinary way with wood and a little coal. If the ea" is broken into smallish lumps, and added a little at a time fire. , the result is a continuously glowing</p>
        <p>A large increase of coke constimption in this way would, of course, quickly exhaust the out- put of our gasworks, but the result would simply be an extension of the coking industry, now concerned merely with the needs of the iron trade. This would have the advantage of util- ising all those by-products in which coal tar is so rich, which are worth many millions a year to Germany, but which here serve only to con- taminate the air. This is an ideal way of using coal: the ordinary way will probably be one day regarded as somewhat like making a fire by breaking up the drawing-rrm furniture.' GAS STOVES AND KITCHENERS.</p>
        <p>A great improvement could' again be effected by substituting gas stoves stnd kitcheners, and this would have the additional advantage/of enormously reducing the .sulphur emissions, in- auntie!' as the gas has been treated for such impurities.t The improvement of the air in London (where domestic smoke is the more important) practically keeps step with the replacement of coal firm by. gas fires. The effect of the recent gas exhibition at the White City has been, I am</p>
        <p>—tirner-it+—Tu.ln tlitur Irstii</p>
        <p>told, remarkable, gas stove manufacturers being at present quite unable to cope with the orders. The only objection is the cost—at present con- siderably more than that of coal; but against this must be set the ease with which gas may be turned on and off, and saving in labour, especially that of cleaning. Under present con- ditions of manufacture, however, the cost of gas might be considerably reduced. At Widnes, enriched gas of 18. c.p., and 650 8.T.U., is actuall.) sold at Is. id. for lighting and at 11d, for heating. (Compare with this the corresponding prices in Warrington, 2s. Id. and is. sd. per thousand respectively.) Instead of taxing consumers 1:p. setting aside large profits for the relief of the rates authorities should devote every penny to encouraging the use of gas. If any are to bt mulcted in a tax it should rather be those who, by refusing to conform to proper ordinances, injure their neighbours in health and property, and entail unnecessary expense on the com- munity.:</p>
        <p>But in regard to cost of gas we are probably on the eve of a revolution, for the adoption of the incandescent mantle is making it unnecessary to manufacture a high power illuminant for the bare flame, and when once this is recognised by the law as an accomplished fact, the companies will be in a position to supply a gas at 6d. to 9d. per thousand. Owing to certain relaxations of the law as regards quality of illuminant the first step in this reform has already been taken, and I am rather surpriied that this is not re- flected to a greater extent in the present prices of gas.</p>
        <p>' IMPROVED GRATES AND BETTER STOKING. , Where people must or will nee bitumiiious fuel, they should be compelled to burn it with- out smoke. Fortunately there are several grates that will do this.</p>
        <p>With regard to grates the Coal Smoke Abate- ment Society has done good service by its tests,§ but such work should be done by the public bodies. I would suggest that the Corporation take powers to insist on efficient heating applk- ances just as they insist on proper sanitary accommodation. The proper body for the pur- pose would be an independent committee formed on similar lines to the Education Committee, with power to co-opt experts. Such a committee should have power to initiate prosecutions, which should be head where possible by a stipendiary. As a general measure the words "except in connection with a private dwelling- house" should be expunged from the P.H.A. smoke clause. •</p>
        <p>The great difficulty is that ne plus ultra of stupidity—the English kitchen grate. I sup- pose the English working man must have his one fire an open one, and, of course, his predi- lection for it is intelligible -i it is cheerful, and its genial radiancy being independent of convection currents, there is no antagonism between heating and ventilation, which latter, in fact, it assures, no less than the former; but it is terribly wasteful.</p>
        <p>Nevertheless, something can be done by care- ful stoking. Fuel should be added often in small quantities, and not in large lumps; the ideally economical fire is one aglow on the sur- face, where the emerging fuel gases meet the hir. _</p>
        <p>evolved</p>
        <p>_ by the fuel should meet the air before they are cooled (as by striking a cold object). The effect. 6.g., is often striking, of so poking the fire as to leave an open shaft through the centre for the escaping gases.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>FACTORY SMOKE THE CHIEF NUISANCE.</head>
        <p>FACTORY SMOKE THE CHIEF NUISANCE.</p>
        <p>A Cure Ready to Hand.</p>
        <p>Second, with regard to factory smoke—which is responsible for the bulk of the nuisance—it is more difficult to generalise in view of the variety and complexity of conditions, but it is still true, that, with very few exceptions, there is a cure for every smoky chimney, and that efforts to abate smoke are accompanied by an economy, which, in many eases, dwarfs into int significance the initial expenditure entailed.</p>
        <p>Saving 1,000 Tons of kal /per week. The savings effected some years agcl:by Jos. Crosfield and Sons, as a result of the close atten- tion they gave to the smoke problem, are so well known that it is needless to dwell upon details, but the broad fact is that over one thousand tons less coal per week are now being used than would have been used under former conditions. Obviously such a result—for our case was no better than that of hundreds of other old-estab- lished industries—could only be obtained by em- ploying all the resources of science. It goes without saying that modern appliances have been introduced, and the human equation has been as far as possible eliminated by automatic devices, but the main secret of success was that, grasping boldy the principle that combustion is a chemical re-action, we placed the whole plant under a competent, well-paid chemical staff. The fuel is under scientific observation from its entry into the .works till it escapes from the chimney, or is removed as ash. The coal is carefully sampled and tested for calorific value, percentage of moisture, and amount of ash, con- tracts are made on the basis of these tests, and future deliveries must be to standard. The fine gases are by special contrivances frequently and regularly sampled at various points, and also analysed. • _</p>
        <p>Naturally ,there are many small plants where such arrangements cannot be expected to be adopted in their _entirety. Then the skill and care of the fireman, Ithich always count for a great deal, become all-important, and as in the aggregate such cases form a very big part we cannot too strongly emphasise that one of the chief remedies for industrial smoke is to be found in the provision of instruction to firemen. Competent and -conscientious stoking should be insisted on, and good work should be rewarded :by good wages. Such a policy would pay the ' manufacturer, as well as the community. Lastly, we must work ceaselessly to rouse the public into a wholesome discontent, appeal ceaselessly to the public spirit, as well as to the interests of the manufacturers, stimulate the local authorities to action, and at the same I time educate our legislators, and while awaiting the consummation of all these efforts let each _man in his owil measure, small though it be, do something to lift the horrid pall which now darkens the lives of two-thirds of our fellow- countrymen.</p>
        <p>• Some progress in this reform has been made in this country in the interval since this was written but Germany has still a very big start. Not only are all the tar by-products obtained there now on a very large scale, as well as other valuable by- products, such as ammonia and cyanides. but, what is perhaps still more important, the sulphur com- pounds can now be obtained direct along with the ammonia in the form of ammonium sulphate, a sub- stance simply indispensable to agriculture. No direct process is yet used in England on a large scale, and for the manufacture of ammonium sill- Phate we still buy huge quantities of sulphuric acid, while the sulphur in the coal, that would supply it, is allowed to escape and poison the air. t Thanks to the enlightened policy of many, cor- porations and companies which lend stoves Fre* or at a nominal charge, and to the slot meter system, this reform has already made enormous progress, the number of these appliances in use having in- creased by more than a million in ten years. According to Sir George Livesey, who speaks for three London companies. nearly SO per cent. (about 650,000) of their consumers use cookers, and nearly 40 per cent. have each an average of two gtis fires. "I know of an instance," says Chief Inspec- tor Adams, of Chicago, "where a firm consumed GOO dollars' worth of coal in such a manner as to cause an actual damage to adjacent property ex- ceeding 25,000 dollars,"</p>
        <p>, • § Reports of these may be obtained from the Secretary, 25, Victoria-street, Westminster.</p>
      </div>
