        <p>BOLD-STREET WESLEYAN CHURCH. lie Rev. EL Michilewood, preaching at Bold-</p>
        <p>street on Sunday evening, drew many valuable lessons from the story of Peter's deliverance Am prison, as detailed in Acts all.</p>
        <p>The ease of Peter seemed indeed hopeless, for every precaution had been taken to prevent his escape. These precautions were involuntary acts of homage to the God of Peter, and also. revealed Herod's fear of the Apostle. But in spite of Al human forethought, Peter was aroused from sleep, conducted outside the gaol and set at liberty. The strange .thing about this libe;ation waa the happening of the unex- pected. The iron gate that led to the city opened of its own accord.</p>
        <p>The iron gate is symbolic of many unexpetted things which the year 1914 holds for .every man. The coming year 'will reveal crises, difficulties. trials and surprises. There will also be non- revelations of love and power, and emancipa- tions far beyond our fondest hopes and dreams. The unfoldings of the year will be of a pleas- ing nature, and very possibly there will be an admixture of the painful experiences of life. The iron gate in the gaol was opened by the golden key of prayer, and the grace that has been vouchsafed to believers in the past; will' go with the Chureh through the future. The power that has brought men through two wards of the prison will not leave them at the ices gate. , The eircumatances, perplexities and diffi- culties of the year will be overshaAlowea con- tinually by the Almighty Presence, for the- Church has still:the everlasting promise, "„Ify, presence shall go with thee, and I will giro the rest."</p>
        <p>A sum of /68 48., subscribed by the contract ticket hoklers who travel between Manchester and Southport On the Lancashire and Yorkshire R,stilway, was distributed among drivers, stokers aid guards on Saturday night in amounts eittig‘ ing from £4 to 5*- •</p>
