      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>ONLY.</head>
        <p>ONLY.</p>
        <p>BOROUGH POLICE COURT</p>
        <p>MONDAY.</p>
        <p>Before the Mayor (in the chair), Couneillor Dr. Joseph, Aldermen James Evans and Bolton, Messrs. W. Furness, S. Wright and Ai. Roberts</p>
        <p>iirt3BAND " Ler Dows."—A sad case of neglect was heard when Catherine Farrington, 5, Black- hurst-street, was charged with - having been drunk whilst in charge of a female child under seven years of age, and she, along with her hus- band, William, Was summoned for neglecting their children.—lt was stated that the woman iiad been to a "wake," and was found the pre- vious Sunday morning at 1.30 in a drunken state in Carlisle-street by P.C. Cooke. The husband had been off work for a month, and had only been receiving 13s. 241. per week compensation. —The Chief Constable said the child was in a filthy condition and it had hardly any clothes on. The child was gravely neglected, and the 1' legs were no thicker than two fingers put to- 1 gether.—Dr. Peacock° reported that the children were all in a bad state of filth, and he oidered them to the Workhouse. One of the children was very weak and emaciated, and had only a few rags as covering.—A school attendance officer said the woman would not do anything for the children, lie got nothing but abuse from her. The man seemed very decent.—l. Spector Wainwright saiethe woman had asked for another chance to make amends. She grum- bled about her husband's money, but he said he could not trust her with it one week. Ire was let down by his wife, lie would not go in the public-house- if he had more home comforts.— The man said his sister had offered to give them a fresh start.--The woman vvas sent to prison for two months, and the case against the man. was adjourned for a month. •</p>
        <p>Cacm—An old woman named Catherine Con- nor was very cheeky when sentenced to seven days' imprisonment for begging. Turning to the Bench she said "You dirty dogs," whereat the Mayor called her back and said "You will get 14 days now, and if you say any more you will be given a longer term." She was conveyed " below " grumbling all the while.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>TUESDAY.</head>
        <p>TUESDAY.</p>
        <p>Before Councillor H. Roberts (in the chair), Messrs. C. Jackson Holmes and T. Locker.</p>
        <p>FOLLOWED THE LADlF,S.—William Green, no fixed abode, was charged with being a suspected person loitering in Bridge-street with intent_._ The Chief Constable asked for an adjournment. Sergt. Holland saw prisoner following ladies who had satchels in Sankey-street, Bridge-street. and Winwick-street. He followed one lady a considerable distance.—Prisoner was remanded for seven days.</p>
        <p>KIND-HEARTED SISTER.—IrenTy ShOVOIAOII, 22, Charles-street, Warrington, wa.s charged with neglecting his four children.—Mr. Browne, for the N.S.P.C.C., said prisoner was a well-known fish hawker and the complaints against him were that he would not work and would not provide food or money for the keeping of his children. The children had been starved for want of fire and food. He had been dismissed from one situation because he had come home in.a drunken state with the cart. He had not given his wife much money. His w.ife had told him that the gas meter had not had a penny in since the inspector put one in, and prisoner said, "Ask the inspector for another one."— Mrs. Shovelton said she had had a separation order from her husband. Only for her _sister, Mrs. Margaret Dagnall, 8, Norman-street, the children would have starved.—Mr. Constable, relieving officer, said that the woman had run home from Inspector Wainwright's in order to get half an hour's warmth.—Prisoner said he had a job to go to that morning.—lnspector Wainwright ga.ve full details of the case, which was adjourned for one month, the Chairman saying, "Our difficulty was whether to do that or give you three months."</p>
      </div>
