      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>POULTRY HOUSE.</head>
        <p>THE POULTRY HOUSE.</p>
        <p>La,)ing can be governed in no small measure L "Y the system of feeding followed, and back- ward birds can be hastened into laying in the. t,/nie manner as forward birds can be retarded. the late pullets should be fed very generously upon foods of rather a forcing nature, and a ./r4rY" good mixture for the morning mash con- sists of two parts barley meal, one maize meal, one middlings, and half a part granulated meat. At nearly all other. times maize is a food to be avoided, but it is helpful for backward pullets Immediately they begin laying it should be dis- continued, or it will seriously reduce the egg "P*. In the afternoon, wheat, barley and maize, fed either alternately or mixed in equal Proportions, should be supplied. Stimulants are better entirely avoided just at present, as they are so liable to do the birds more harm n'an good. Later ,in the season, when the weather is cold and wet, they may be given With beneficial results.</p>
        <p>SUSSEX GROUND OATS. Pure Sussex ground oats can always be reeog-iiied I by their appearance, taste, and smell, also • by handling them. In appearance they are - somewhat similar to barley meal, but in the best qualities the meal is of one .fineness throughout, without any of the little bits of husk characteristic of barley meal. The taste is an almost infallible guide, and should closely resemble that of oatmeal, Te clean "nutty" flavour; the smell is also like that of oatmeal. NV;,en handled, Sussex 'ground oats have a dis- tinctive "silky" feeling, and if a small quan- tils be crushed up in the hand, it adheres to- gether slightly. Sussex ground oats, besides I".('`lacing the finest quality flesh on fattening .f(Zilts'err also a splendid toed for rearing Assinar.:: as the finely-grounded husks can be ted, and help t build up a strong and ,01‘a.eillfideereloped fi.ame° ; while the kernel of the e of course, one of the most nourishing, of Jood s, and: eont•tining a high percentage of al- butninoids slk is particularly suitable for growing ee of all classes.</p>
        <p>THE POULTRY HOUSE. An expensive house does not necessarily Mean 1, 1 B.,°°Kl house. Some of the most suitable places nave seen have cost very little indeed; where- , "3, oh the other hand, some of the worst that I have come across have been very expensive. It is obviously quite possible to combine suit, ability with cheapness, and this should ever • he the aim. There is, however.; such a thing as raise economy, and this must be avoided. If the house 4 constructed of poor or insalliciently • stout material it may prove very expensive in the long run, for to achieve the best results good housing is essential. As a matter of ,fact, there is no class of stock which responds more readily to good housing than poultry. The exact form of house does not really Matter at all, .so long 4.45 the general principles receive attention. Portable houses possess several advantages over Permanent ones, and whenever space permits they are to be recommended. 'A portable }lease requires to be strongly made, as otherwise the volistant moving soon pulls it to pieces. "When Ri)ace is limited, the permanent form of house is the better, and for this purpose there is no kind so suitable as the scratching-shed. This 4;onsists of a house and run under the same roof. "rile most convenient arrangement, as a rule, is to have the house and run alongside of one an- other, since otherwise the interior of the roost- ing compartment is bound to be so very dark. It often happens that there is . a barn or shed which, with a very little alteration, can be transformed into a convenient house. and any ,olch place should be taken advantage of to the MICA-extent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>GROWING CANADA.</head>
        <p>GROWING CANADA.</p>
        <p>. ---- ! !Progress Reflected in Christmas P,O. Business. ,- The Canadian Post Office Department, with its army of postmasters, clerks, &amp;c., all over the _ D. °minion, had the heaviest time on record dur- , -lipthe Christmas season of 1913. The heavy ,ish mails were accounted for, of course, by ' le largely increased immigration during recent - Lem's' and the fact that parcels post rates be- the old country and Canada are cheaper itseagvellY than those existing in the Dominion Dar ' On the Saturday preceding Christmas W,,1,,e' ellecial train from Montreal carried the .f;Tern mail to Winnipeg. An interesting • fa--a`nre ef the early Christmas mails was the _retet that a carload for China and Japan was 'ei'ved at Halifax and sent across the conti- nent to Vancouver for transmission by a Pacific liner S ,t 4,0 • ome 1,200 or 1,500 extra clerks were en en to handle the Christmas rush. In m ineatreal about 256 extra clerks were employed, .., Toronto,,th 200, and similar increases were made 4.7 fle staff in other places. The extra clerks ,Are engaged for a month in order to be trained. e Christmas mail f Great Britain alone Milk 'ai ailed • ' eight aud a half million packages.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>FARM AND GARDEN,</head>
        <p>FARM AND GARDEN,</p>
        <p>MANURE AND TUBERCULOSIS</p>
        <p>Winter Management of Young Cattle.</p>
        <p>• Mr. R. H. Green, says the "Times," has fol- lowed up his letters to the Press last autumn on the wintering of young cattle in covered yards by bringing the matter under the notice of the Board of Agriculture. In a letter to Mr. Runciman lie suggested that the Board should conduct inquiries into the danger arising from the practice, and, if the results justify such a course, issue is warning to breeders that the system constitutes a fruitful source of tubereulai• infection. The plan Mr. Green condemns is that of rearing young cattle during the winter months in covered yards into which the manure from the cow and other sheds is collected. I His theory is not only in accordance with icommon sense ideas of hygiene, but he claims that he and others can adduce definite proof that tuberculosis is spread in this way. It is with where reference to heifers intended for ..breeding pur- poses. that Mr. Green is chiefly insistent upon thete manure discontinuance f rom th of f wholewinteringm h e e len e yards homestead ;sd • accum- ulated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>Voluntary Action.</head>
        <p>Voluntary Action.</p>
        <p>There never was a time more opportune than the present for pressing upon the attention of breeders the importance of the point raised by Mr. Green. Tliere is evidence of widespread determination to rot out the disease from herds in which it exists. 'Extensive alterations, structural and other, have been made at home- steads with a view to . improving ventilation, lighting, and other conditions making for purity and cleanliness, but it will be recognised that these efforts will prove unavailing if there re- mains unaltered a system of management of young stock or cows that ,facilitates the spread of infection. If there he a single tuberculous aniinar in the herd the Contamination of the others is made 'certain bt the praCtiee con- demned: The Manure from the iiifected stall is removed with that from the others to the covered yard where the heifers or young bulls are brought into direct contact with it, and are liable 'to inliale the 'gerinS *trial the polluted atmosphere.- Mr. Runciman, though obviously sensible of the danger involved, rightly con- ' siders that the matter must be left to the dis- cretion of breeders themselves. since the ilues- tion was raised by Mr. Green many leading breeders have given the matter serious con- sideration, and have resolved upon important changes in the management of their herds. The vital point has been recognised that housing in accordance with modern conceptions of hygiene is a fundamental condition of success in rooting or keeping out an infectious disease of the nature of tuberculosis.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>ANIMAL DISEASE RESEARCH.</head>
        <p>ANIMAL DISEASE RESEARCH.</p>
        <p>Addressing the Newcastle-on-Tyne Farmers' Club on Saturday, Sir Sydney Olivier, Perma- nent Secretary to the Board of Agriculture, said the Board were working greatly in the dark in respect to foot-and-mouth disease and swine I fever' They were apt to be a little panic-stricken when they had to carry out their restrictive measures. eloped that would not be the cese very much longer, owing to the establishment of the Development Fund, which had enabled them to lay the basis of advance in any direc- tions. One of the ways was by endowing the kingdom with a proper laboratory for patho- logical research in regard to the disease of animals. Mr. Runciman had recently been in- vestigating the question of dealing with swine fever by serum and vaccination treatment, as in Holland and Hungary. It seemed to him that even if the inoculation by serum was not an absolute preventive of future infection, yet it did save life and enabled many animals to be marketed which, under present conditions, would have to be killed. Much was being &amp;tie in the way of co-ordination and bringing to- gether institutions working for agricultural education and improving and strengthening them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>IN THE GARDEN.</head>
        <p>IN THE GARDEN.</p>
        <p>Aralias, aspidistras, palms and india-rubber plants should be frequently sponged to pre- vent dirt accumulating on the leaves. It is just as necessary for the underside of the leaves to be kept clean as _the top. What is known as. Wood's scarlet Pea is a variety of lathgrus rotundifolius. It is not a new plant, but it is, nevertheless, a pretty one. It grows about lift. high, and is a useful little plant for a warm place.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>LILIES.</head>
        <p>LILIES.</p>
        <p>There are very few gardens in which the lily, in some form or another, is not represented. The Madonna lily (lilium candidum) is to be fannd in cottage gardens throughout the land, and no lily is more prized than this chaste and lovely kind. About a couple of decades ago a disease appeared, apparently fungoid, and played havoc with fine groups which had for years been regarded with pride. The Madonna lil does better without much humus in the soil, and a fertiliser may be used with a liberal addi- tion of silver sand, given as a dre.ssing around the clumps. Replanting should be done in late summer, as soon as the old flowering stems show signs of ripening. These lilies should be groxn in .warm, sunny borders. -The orange lily (lilium crocenin) is another good and free-flowering lily. The erect umbels of flowers appear in July. Following close on are the varieties of lilium davurnicum. gcod manure dressing to the soil is advantageoas. The tiger lily (lilium tigrinum) with its varieties grow and flower freely. These flower in August and September. The tiger lilies are very effective grown in masses, and may be planted from September to February. Give a light dressing of hot-bed manure at planting. The Scarlet Turk's Cap lily (lilium chalce- donicum), L. ITansoni, L. testaceun, L. ofloram, L. Brownii and others, which •-mme under the longiflorum section, are fine members of the ly tribe The North American Panther lily (lilirm uirdalinum) is a grand lily. This ITy does well iv peat, and likes moisture. The 'beautiful !ilium auratum does host planted in partial shade, and makes a good elicw planted among shrubs. The raa:EIS f,f L,004-,3 thus protected from the nipping frosts of spring.</p>
        <p>( rS " ZC = = = .sa = = = Mc Dougalls • Self- Raising 0 Flour it MAKES DELICIOUS p •!I. Pancakes • Light and easily Digested. n. NO WORRY. NO WASTE. NO FAILURES. TRY IT TO-DAY, -41104 1." 101 No Always</p>
        <p>101</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <p>THF, EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 17, 1914.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>WHERE</head>
        <p>WHERE</p>
        <p>VOTE.</p>
        <p>Prominence Given to Legislation Affecting Women and Children.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>WASTAGE OF CHILD LIFE.</head>
        <p>WASTAGE OF CHILD LIFE.</p>
        <p>BY A LADY CONTRIBUTOR.</p>
        <p>When considering the general results in our Over-seas Dominions since the granting of the franchise to women, one cannot do better than start with a quotation from the resolution passed unanimously in the Australian Senate on November 17th, 1910, and dwell on the last sentence quoted:—</p>
        <p>"That this Senate is of opinion that the ex- tension of the Suffrage to the Women of Aus- tralia for States and Commonwealth Parlia- ments, on the same terms as men, has had the most beneficent results. It has led to the more orderly conduct of elections, and at the last Federal Election the women's vote in the major- ity of the States showed a greater proportionate increase than that cast by men. IT HAS oxvirx A ,IREATER PROMINENCE TO LEGISLATION, PARTICLTLARLY iFFECTINO WOMEN AND CIIILDRENZ"</p>
        <p>This article is designed to follow the trend of legislation with regard to the women and children of these enfranchised countries and to compare it with legislation in our own country. Tasmania was enfranchised in 1903, and in /907 we find the Bishop of Tasmania writing: "It is possible to trace various subtle modifi- cations in public feeling which I regard as wholly beneficial behalf of children, girls and women will be more speedy and more adequate. Australia is thug reaping the reward of having responded to the unanswerable appeal of justice." Since the enfranchisement of women in Aust.. tralia a minimum age has been fixed under which children may not be employed in fac- tories, for New South Wales, Queensland, Wes- tern Australia and Tasmania it is 14 years, and id South Australia and Victoria 13 years. In England, though the child, owing to climatic conditions, does not develop at so early an age, it has not yet been possible to raise the com- pulsory school age to 14 years, and thus keep the children from the mill or factory until that age, nor has the Factories Act of 1901 done more than forbid the employment of children under 12. In Australia all restrictions imposed On employers of child labour tend to one end,l viz.: That a proper period shall be .loyoted to ' primary education and that the early years of toil shall not exhaust the worker l:efore the attainment of full growth. THE CHILD HALF-TIMERON ENGLAND It is true that the English Poor Law Commis- sioners, a few years ago, recommended the total prohibition of child labour under the age of 15, a recommendation which has not yet become law, though the reply of the Prime Minister on February Bth, 1909, to a deputation headed by the Bishops of Southwark and Hereford led one to suppose that legislation for the further pro- tection of children might be forthcoming., The Prime Al Mister on that occasion quoted the dis- couraging figures of the Lancashire operatives' decision whereupon a poll on the question of raising the "half-timer's" age to 13, barely 34,000 voted in the affirmative and no less than 150,000 voted in the negative. That the majority of parents in Lancashire regard children only as a commercial speculation and resist efforts to raise the age of "half-timers" is a further proof of that lack of. a sense of responsibility ,which' might be awakened and- educated were the women granted the privileges of citizenship. On the other hand, it has been contended that the wages of the 12-year-old " half-timer" are urgently needed, by the family, and that his withdrawal from work would throw an extra strain on the mother of the family. This is not found to be the case in Australia, and it must be realised that the half-timer is almost with- out exception the brightest of scholars, who, haying passed the required standards:goes to the mill, thus losing, for the sake .of and unsatisfactory employment, all chance of scholarship and qualifying for a better position in the future. In Australia there is a general enactment of a 48 hours' limit per week for women and chil- dren, and the maximum period of continuous labour is five hours. In New Zealand the hours are limited to 45.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>FARMERS AND TARIFFS.</head>
        <p>FARMERS AND TARIFFS.</p>
        <p>Progress of the New Organisation.</p>
        <p>GREAT INTEREST IN THE COUNTRY.</p>
        <p>No Tax on Focd, No Tariff Reform.</p>
        <p>(FRO3I TILE " MANCHESTER GUARDIAN" SPECIAL, CORRESPONDENT.) Lincoln, Monday. , Whatever may be the feeling in some of the agricultural district's about the farmers' revolt against Mr. Boner Law's new Tariff "Reform" , Policy, there is no doubt that uncompromising hostility prevails in the minds of many Lin- , colushire farmers, and it is said by the leaders, of the revolt that the movement is rapidly : spreading in many other part ii of the country. Iu the course of an interview, Mr. Bernard Gil- bert, the secretary of the new Farmers' Tariff Union, summarised for me the objects of the new association, and traced the events which led to its formation. .Mr. Gilbert first said that he wished to make it quite clear that the Farmers' Tariff Union is not in any way allied to the Tariff Reform League. "It is predominantly a Conservative body," he said, "but its policy is supported by farmers who are Free Traders as well as by those who are Tariff 'Reformers.' " I asked Mr. Gilbert to trace the events which have led to the present rupture with the official Conservative party. "The trouble began with Ur; Bonar Law's Ashton speech," he replied. "When the Tariff 'Reform' campaign was first started the members of the Farmers' Union in this district considered the question at a meet- ing in Lincoln, and we passed a resolution that we would not declare ourselves officially on either side, but would wait and see how the thing went—with the proviso that we would insist ITO any benefits that a Tariff. 'Reform' policy might. produce. It was not until Mr. :Dollar Law throw the farmers over at Ashton that anything was done: I wrote two letters to the local papers asking what Mr. Chaplin had to say. about it, and later the Lincoln Farmers' Union passed a resolution expressing their determination not to be a party to any change in the fiscal policy which excluded agri- culture from its benefits. That resolution was , passed unanimously, and copies were sent to Mr. Bonar Law and the local members of Parlia- ment so that they know quite well what is igoing on. We also sent copies to all kinds of farmers' organisations in other countries, and it has been adopted by a great number of them, as well as by a representative meeting of the Executive of the National Farmers' Union. It is still being considered and adopted by one agricul- tural body after another, and Mr. Henry Chap- lin has written warmly expressing his approval of it. It was suggested to me that my letters on the subject should be printed in pamphlet form, together with quotations from the speeches of party leaders showing that a system of Tariff 'Reform' from which agriculture was excluded would be disastrous to farmers.</p>
        <p>Keen Interest Everywhere.</p>
        <p>----- - "To show the keen interest which the subject I has aroused it is only necessary to state that in less than six months over a hundred thousand of these pamphlets have been sold, notwith- standing the fact that all but one or two of the Conservative nevhipapers have boycotted it. We , are going to make an effort to get a copy int° the hands of every farmer before the next general election.</p>
        <p>"After the publication of the pamphlet a few of the Members of the Farmers' Union and others who were interested met in Lincoln, and we had to consider the fact that the Farmers' Union, because of its constitution and varied membership, could not be expected to carry on I an active campaign of the kind we desired. We therefore decided not to press the Farmers' Union further than the point to which the Executive had . gone in definitely declaring in favour of our resolution, and so the Farmers' Ttliff Union was formed to carry on a vigorous fight for fair treatment for farmers,, and to combat the present fiscal proposals as laid down by Mr. Bonar Law at Ashton and Edinburgh. Our motto is 'All or nothing.' If a tariff is imposed there must be fair play for farmers under it. The movement has extended to all parts of the country, and I have received thousands of letters from Conservative farmers expressing satisfaction that a determined oppo- sition is to be offered to the new Tariff `Reform . policy. I have also received a number of anonymous letters of approval, some from the House of Commons, and one or two from the House of Lords, while a well-known baronet has sent me a cheque for ten pounds in support . of the movement."</p>
        <p>A Fight to the Death. "Do you think that the farmers will press their hostility to the point of opposing candi- dates who support the present policy of the party?".</p>
        <p>"They will indeed," Mr. Gilbert replied. "No compromise is possible, and it will be a fight to the death. We estimate that there are over eighty 'safe' Conservative seats which are swayed by the agricultural vote, and that vote we must either share the benefits of a Tariff ' Reform ' system or remain under Free Trade. We are told that Ulster mu-st come first, and our reply to that is that if we are called upon to wait for Tariff Reform' benefits, manu- facturers must also be asked to wait."</p>
        <p>Mr. Gilbert ascribed the thoroughness of the farmers' revolt—in Lincolnshire at any rate— to a decline amongst them of the spirit of poli- tical partisanship. "A feeling has grown up in their minds," he said, "especially during the past five years, that their interests have not been properly considered by either party, and there is an almost universal feeling that the time has come for them to join themselves to- gether and insist on fair consideration. This movement may be regarded as a crystallisation of that feeling, and it is one of the most signi- ficant factors in the present situation." In reply to a further question, Mr. Gilbert again emphatically declared that the new union was not in any sense a Tariff "Reform" organi- sation. "What we say," he added, "is that rather than have a tariff scheme applied only to imported manufactured goods we would re- main under Free Trade."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>WOMEN'S QUESTIONS</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S QUESTIONS</p>
        <p>Will Good Looks be Imperilled to Hold a Monocle?</p>
        <p>ATTACK ON DEAFNESS.</p>
        <p>Miss Keller has recently been on a somewhat in- terest in her excessive delicacy of touch, and lengthy visit to Mr. Edison, who was deeply in- devoted some weeks to an invention which he believes will enable the deaf to hear. By means of his recent investigations, be claims that he can convert sound waves into electric vibrations, and thus create sound perceptions, which the deaf will be able to feel. Everyone will con- gratulate this lady, if Mr. Edison succeede, for despite the fact that she was born blind, deaf, and dumb, she is more highly educated .than many eminent women possessing the advantages she lacks. The King and Queen of Spain con- sulted her on the matter °froth° education of their second son, Don Jaime, who is unfortu- nately quite deaf, and it is feared may become duinb•</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>Monocles for Women.</head>
        <p>Monocles for Women.</p>
        <p>t. lie wears his eyeglass like a gentleman," said Mr. Disraeli, when commenting on Mr. Joseph Chamberlain's first speech in the House of Commons, but it would not sound very appro- priate to hear this habit referred to as a lady- like accomplishment. Nevertheless the monocle is the latest caprice of society women. in America. At one time it was much affected by 1 men of fashion, and po4sibly it is only a feminine revival. Learned professors are'fond of telling us that there are no such things as new ft;shions. Hobble- skirts, false teeth, false hair, and all the rest of it, we are tom, were familiar to people who lived before the Christian era, and it seems probable that there is nothing new in this latest menace from America—the femin- ine- eyeglass. At the same timo it is not very likely to become popular. There are Women who -will do almost anything to gain notoriety in the forefront of fashion, but they will 'not imperil their good looks b'y serw,ving up and dis- torting their faces in order to retain the monocle in its place.</p>
        <p>Dress Allowances. Pfga husband accom- The law with respect to the responsibility of the husband and father for Personal debts i incurred by members of his family is in a loose sptraettee,d atinidd'iistfecroeinisttaconutir) differently inter- t-stre. i panies his wife into a shop he is responsible for theall she m time onlyay purc kickingliase, his.l h t lio heels he may be all s n the waiting 'room. The latest decision is to the effect that where a daughter resides, with her father the latter is prima-facie liable for any tradesmen's Ile can escape debts she may .incur. this re- sponsibility by making his daughter an allow- ance for all reasonable necessities, but it is the exception rather than the rule in middle-class households for the domestic exchequer to be so apportioned that each member of the family has a stated share of dress, travel and amusement. Such an arrangement relieves parents, and teaches young people the value of money, and how to lay it out to the best advantage. It is therefore clearly' to the best interests of all con't. corned to have suchuM understanding, if circum- stances permit. . Yet we are ex- pecting to enjoy the seasonable heat of early when in Australia An Australian lee Carnival. To people in this country the idea of a resort for winter a ltusljelofia. July, the little tOwnshiP of Riandra, some two hundred miles from Sydney, New South Wales, is enjoying an ice carnival, in which every tts_foe n tol icee d and yt ' member of the community, from the tiny tod- dler to the oldest . inhabitant 44, ,whether yellow, or white, takes part. Visitors from surround- ing centres attend the festival, filling the cen e two hotels to overflowing., The carnival has earned for itself more than a local reputation, more especially for ski-ing—the terraces of the hills on which the little town is situated being specially adapted to this particular sport. The festival owes its inception to some Norwegian settlers in Kiandra,,,aod the competitions are on lines precisely similar to those in vogue in Norway.</p>
        <p>Swiss and Austrian winter greater proportions resortsg e xaa 0: sddi exodus rsla Sportingt 6ci, every vh iee . wise year beforehand. Sports in point of numbers, allot h ise People book rooms S kTiliile appears to be more fascinating than of every kind Possible on ice and snow are pro- in the hotels some m 0 on vided by the management of the various resorts, any other. but ski-ing It enables its devotees to cover dis- tances iMpossible to accomplish on foot, or in any kind of convey-ance, and at the same time to enjoy the most exhilarating of sports. Nor is it—compared with skatin —,i; g -.4ficult to learn, ' and the inevitable falls on the soft snow are amusing as well as harmless. The sports cos- tume is now an important item of women's dress and the equipment for ski-ing is rather expen- sive: A coat and skirt of a smooth, bard, snow- resisting material is the best for the purpose. The latter must be short, and built in such a way that it gives the limbs Perfect freedom of action. Magpie and Filmy Effects,</p>
        <p>Leaders of fashion continue to show a marked preference for magpie effects hi the most beauti- ful laces of the finest makes, such as Cluny and Chantilly, which they combine cleverly and artistically. The most beautiful and effective lace dresses shown this winter are made of the latter, and have skirts a alternate whiie and black flounces, the corsages being a combina- tion of the two tones cleverly mingled. A little lace coatee of the Remono type over such a dress sounds superfluous, but in reality is an effective addition to a charming toilette. An ancient shawl or a or a short length of soft mb on, or tulle, suffices to esrcoirdfer oefd lace, n make one of these useful coatees, that are worn with all light afternoon and tivening dresses. A band of marabout, feather trimming, pleated net, or tulle, makes a suitable bordering for these little garments, that are quite as fre- quently fashioned of white as of black lace. Filmy effects are a feature of this season's modes and the richest brocades in wonderful colour- ings and shot with gold and silver threads, are draped with lace of cobwebby texture. The touch of contrasting fur serves to enhance the filminess of the veiling.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>0449311AWNAPM1.</head>
        <p>0449311AWNAPM1.</p>
        <p>4t,ir.btAl, Of *Et', ty 4act [deemed</p>
        <p>In England it is possible for a girl of 14 to work 12 hours a day, with an interval of 11 to 2 hours for meals.</p>
        <p>In Victoria, the Factories and Shops Act, passed in 1910, limits the weights to be lifted by girls under 18 to 251 h. It is interesting here to recall the fact that on May Ist, 1913, Mr. Asquith_received a deputation representing the Women's Trade Union League and the National Organisation of Girls' Clubs, whose leader spoke on this subject. Mrs. Lowin, of the Trade ! Union League, said she knew that girls between the ages of 15 and 18 had to carry loads of V, cwt. to 2 cwt. Miss Gertrude T•uckwell further urged that there should be a maximum weight for children, and that only certified children who were really strong and normal should be allowed to carry weights. She thought such a proposal should be extended to women. The Prime Minister so far agreed that he considered it should be extended to men also, and he thought it 'possible, by making more stringent conditions, to apply great protection in that respect to children and young people, lie could not, however, " hold out any confident prospect of legislation during the present year."</p>
        <p>ND HALF-TIMERS IN NEW ZEALAND. In New Zealand there are no "half-timers." The Women's Societies, and the Press are urging the discontinuation of child labour on farms before and after school hours. Since 1898 all young persons in New Zealand are opititled to a minimum wage of ss: a week, with the annual increase of 3s. per week,. until £1 is reached. In order to get a minimum wage for one branch of women's industry at the present date in England endless labour is entailed before the demand for a Wages Board is possible, which, when granted, comes into operation six months later still. • The Trades Boards Act of 1909 only affected the industry of chain-making, lace finishing, cardboard box making, and tailoring, to which, in 1912, have been added the trades of sugar confectionery, food preserving, shirt mak- ing, hollow-ware making, and linen and cotton embroidery. A factory in New Zealand is defined as any room or place where two or more persons are engaged, the employer being counted as one. Result—the smallest workroom is under factory regulation as to sanitary conditions and hours of work.</p>
        <p>INDUSTRIAL LEGISLATION IN AMERICA.</p>
        <p>The Best Child's Law in the World.</p>
        <p>In Wyoming, U.S.A., there is practically no factory work employing children, and since the enfranchisement of women an Act has been passed forbidding the employment of boys under 14, or girls of any age, in mines, or of children under 14 in public exhibitions. Colerado obtained Women's Suffrage in 1893. Colerado was in the past less advanced than the Eastern States in the question of the protection of children in industry, primarily due to the fact that the manufacturing industries were not extensively developed, but before 19y8 we find children of 16 and under forbidden to work more than 6 hours a day in any mill, factory, re, or other occupations that may he ed unhealthful. And in 1911 Colerado passed one of the very best laws in the world in the interests of the children; it is known as "The Child Labour Law," which, added to the compulsory education and other chilli protecting devices previously adopted, complßes a most wonderful group of laws for ensuring the health and safety of the growing generation. Largely instrumental in procuring the passage</p>
        <p>of this Bill we find Mrs. Harriet G. It. Wright, a former member of the Legislature, and now for -many years the State President of the Colerado Equal Suffrage Association. her indefatigable efforts in this direction deserve the highest commendation. While contemplating child legislation in America in contrast to the large wastage of child life in other civilised countries, it is specially interesting to read the report of the Federal Children's Bureau (a branch of the United States Department of Labour), over which a woman presides, who was appointed by ex-President Taft, largely through the influence of the Women's Clubs in America, and' to con- trast its constitution with the new Division of the Home Office in England, recently devised to deal with questions relating to children. E. WI</p>
        <p>REGINALD - GOODWIN. FOR SILKS OF MERIT. 401 n. Cymberline, 2s. 6W. 40in. Roman Satin, 3. 6d. 40in. Enjoleuse, 4s. 11d. 40in. Printed and Plain Ninons, is. 11W. ss. 11d. 40in. Soft Broches, 2s. 11W. to 105. 6d. Post Free. 'Phone 5889 City. Saturdays, 1 o'clock. IL RIDGEFIELD, KING-STREET, MANCHESTER</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>FUR IN MILLINERY.</head>
        <p>FUR IN MILLINERY.</p>
        <p>The fibre aigrette mount is frequently seen in the middle of an elaborate ribbon bow, on the newest hats. Artificial flowers are also used, but are less universal than bows and feathers of all kinds. Fur is the chief feature of the new millinery, and a becoming motor bonnet, which could be worn on other occasions than motoring, is of moire and silk, and has a distinctive quaintness due to a band of soft fur that framed the face. The frill of upstanding lace or silk encircles the crowns of toques and many other shapes. These frills are often bordered with fur. The new accordion-pleated ninon, edged with a narrow band of far, makes an effective frill of this description. Time proves that the favour bestowed on velvet and moire is by no means as fleeting as some authorities predicted. for both materials figure prominently in all the new millinery. Moire is extensively used as drapery on the brims of the smaller hats.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>ONTARIO'S FISHERIES.</head>
        <p>ONTARIO'S FISHERIES.</p>
        <p>A recent cable despatch which states that a ten-days' catch of fish realised over ...{20,000 for the fishermen at .Port Stanley, one of the Ontario fishing ports on Lake Erie, serves as an indication of the extent and value of the fish- eries of the province. Of course, the Great Lakes are the largest fresh-water fisheries in the workl, and the white-fish, trout, herring, pickerel, stur- geon, and other fish taken from these waters are of an exceptionally choice variety. Many in- quiries have been received by London dealers. for caviare made from the Canadian sturgeon. This is an expensive delicacy made from the roe or eggs of the large fish when caught before spaWning. The raw material for the caviare is worth over 4s. a pound, and a large size fish is worth £lO for caviare alone. Ontario caviare is also shipped to Germany and Russia. The yield and value of the fisheries of the province are flout two and a half million dollars. Apart from the commercial fisheries, there are splen- did attractions in Ontario waters for the sports- man.</p>
      </div>
