      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 17, 1914. THE PASSING WEEK.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 17, 1914. THE PASSING WEEK.</p>
        <p>BIRTHS. MARRIAGES. DEATHS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>OUR FOOTBALL TEAM MAKES HISTORY.</head>
        <p>OUR FOOTBALL TEAM MAKES HISTORY.</p>
        <p>The Learn that was selected last week to repre- sent the Warrington Football Club in the North- ern Union match against Hull Kingston Boyers, at hull, made history. On Friday night, right on the eve of their prospective departure for tlie East Yorkshire town, the players struck, and have the Unique distinction of being the first body of men to, apply strike tactics. to the foot- ball field. The casus belli, as stated by the dis- affected players, was that the Huddersfield men had been offered a bonus if they beat Warring- ton the previous Saturday, and as the War- rington men proved victors they thought they were entitled to similar treatment. They also , elle&amp; that they had not been adequately treated in the matter of a Christmas gift. So far as Bar latter phase of the dispute is con- cerned we have no information, but there is definite news fromAhe Huddersfield Club deny- ing the bonusa allegation. Mr. Hirst, the secre- tary of IludTersfield, writes to Mr. Bradshaw, secretary of the Warrington Club, denying that his players were offered a bonus to beat NVar- rington. He goes further and say it is not their custom to offer bonuses in League matches. A rumour that the' Wigan men were offered a be Dorms to at Warm% `ton was also inquired into, and an emphatic oilicial denial has been s given, as-in the HunderSfield case. We think it is well to have these disclaimers, for it would oe disastrous to sport if it became a regular practice to offer- bonuses for a win. We need not labour thapoint a$ to what Would, happen. If men can play so Well as to command pay- ment for playing a game, that Payment should be a properly understood and fixed amount.. There are special occasions *such as critical cup-</p>
        <p>tic malcheB', where extra reward may be safely given, not as an extra inducement, but as a vent to that :extraordinary enthuS i lasm which s roused by success in great events. But in the interests of sport, looking nt it in the broad sense, no less than in the interests of individual clubs and playdrs,, there ought to be some arrangement under which it would be impos- sible for such an event as last week's to happen, involving, as it did, disappointment and loss to innocent outside parties, the Boyers and their 'l3ectators being deprived of their " gate " and game. The situation. is capitally summed up by Mr. Houghton, the League secretary, in a speech which we report in another column.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>MAYOR AND THE CHILDREN.</head>
        <p>THE MAYOR AND THE CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>set by the'Nlayor, Councillor Peter Peacock, by Pleasing character was A precedent of a more his entertainment of the elementary school children on Thurgday evening, it may be taken for granted that never was a ball held that provided more unalloyed joy than this one, at which the chosen representatives Of the town's ,scholars disported themselves in all kinds of costumes, and with facilities at hand for amusing themselves to their 'Nuts' con- tent. Pen' Hall provides an extensive floor area, but not one square inch was wasted on this delightful occasion. It was, like the majestic Thames, as described by Sir John Den- ham, " without o'er flowing, full." Ills Worship and the Mayoress were active participants in the feast of gaiety, and the 'band of workers they had attracted to their side, including the Director of Education, the Deputy Town Clerk, and the teachers and Town Hall officials gener- ally, were palpably happiest when they were giving most happiness to their young charges. One of the most reniarkable features of the, ball Was that every one could dance, and every- one did dance almost every-time. The-secret of this was that the tea(,•;hers, whose co-operation from the first was wisely senght, had initiated the children into the mysteries of every move- ment from the graceful waltzing to the /more rugged rag-time, with the mutt that the 'danc- ing in such numbers went along through all the merry hours withoUt halt, crush or accident.</p>
        <p>- - -</p>
        <p>His :W.S OR.IIIP. Ills Worship must have been a proud man, when, at the subsequent gupper to which he entertained the workers in the Council Cham- ber, he was told by Mr. Upson, who voiced the , opinion of the .teachers of the town, that his well-known sympathy for the children, by. tak- ing the practical shape it had, would have a the authorities, teachers lasting effect upon the welfare of the whole town. The work of preparation has brought living touch---a casting waters that is bound to produce the best re- and intoap on close the stilts. The supper to these workers was ti revelation of what can be done at the Town Hall oafndtheehibldrere in the way of catering, for Mr. and Mrs. Mitchell placed before the Mayor's guests a: menu that could not have been excelled. , His Worship, the night before the ball, at- tended the Cripples, Guild and announced the: fact that in the coming sompier he will,-give a garden party in the Town Hall grounds to the cripples of fhe town. - Mr. Frank Ilickman, who was Present, aptly referred to the fact that , London had a famous Cripples' Lord Mayor; : and Councillor Peacock is clearly intent on de- : serving a local-reputation along the same dines: - The prophecies that he would be a popular Mayor are being early justified. Ile and Mrs. Peacock certainly planted themselves in the hearts Of the children of the borough for all time 'by Their kindly act of l'hursdaY.</p>
        <p>it Was a very impressive " lesson " the late Vicar of ThelwalL read when he left instructions :NE LtsT "LESSON.' as to the method that might be employed at his burial. Ile hinted that a handcart might'super- sede the conventional hearse, as being better to his way of thinking. It was a practical corn- ment on the vanity aright, should lead those who prize their " treasure ' } -v- of worldly things, and, read yond all else, to remember the terribly frank rendering of Job by St. Paul, when he said, " We brought nothing into this world, and it Is certain we can carry nothing out." There are many who would benefit in lowliness irthey would ponder upon the inner meaning of the Rev. T. Pym Williamson's "last lesson."</p>
        <p>, SAVINO SOCIETY. ' For some proceeding in the columns of "The Examiner " weeks a correspondence has been upon the relative merits of strikes and politics as means of settling social and industrial pro- blems. A. correspondent representing the "Friends of Labour" has intervened this week, to define the ict —lles, holding that those issues nal are likely to be clouded under the verbzsity Of one of the origi correspondents, and the sorrowful Pessimism of the other. The func- tions of industrial action and Political action are stated, and an appeal is made to us to allow the issues to be thrashed out in these columns. We have pleasure in inviting correspondence on the lines suggested by "The Friends of Labour" in the article "Strikes and Politics."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>_COUNCILLOR DR. FOX.</head>
        <p>_COUNCILLOR DR. FOX.</p>
        <p>-- Dr. Fox has been returned unopposed to repre- sent Town flail Ward in the Warrington Town Council. We congratulate Dr. Fox upon his deserved honour, and the town may be con- gratulated upon having secured the services of so capable and useful a successor to the late Councillor Birties. We shall now have three medical gentlemen on the Council, and if they could only spare the time from their exacting professional duties, that would be a satisfac- tory representation on the medical side. Of course, Dr. Joseph has been exceedingly lavish</p>
        <p>CLOTHS PRAISED BY RUSKIN. The St. George's Guild, of which Ruskin, was master, founded at Laxey, in the Isle of Man, a mill for the production of good honest cloths. Any- -me needing Dress Lengths, Suitings, Rugs, etc., is recently supplied to H.M. the King, of unim- peachable quality, and beautiful design, is invited rØ send for patterns to THE RUSKIN HOMESPUN, 110, St. George's Mill, Laxey, Isle of Man; or G. HOLYROYD &amp; SON. 110, Station-street. Huddersfield.</p>
        <p>Pnvenn, FIRE GRATES never fail to please.— ..4e]. eaent. .r. S GARI,AVT), Ironmonger, 51 to 55. Bridgeldreet, Prarringtea.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <p>in giving of his time and talents—he has been the wonder of the._Council in this respect, espe- cially during the important years of his .Mayor- ally. In view of the many questions looming ahead affecting the health of the borough, we may welcome Dr. Fox as a decided acquisition.</p>
        <p>To WRITE AND ANSWER ADVERTISE- MENTS., Last November a paragraph appeared in " The Examiner" recording the fact that Mr. Chrimes had been lecturing on how to write and answer advertisements. It was pointed out how carelessly both these occupations were performed to the detriment of the best inter- ests of both sides. Since then a correspondence has taken place in one of the great halfpenny dailies, following upon the lines laid down by Mr. Chrimes—which is certainly a feather in his cap.</p>
        <p>ASSESSMENT SECRETS AND THE PRESS. It would be well, we think, if the Warring- ton Assessment Committee drew up a definite scheme dealing with the reporting or non-re- porting of their meetings. Contrary to the usual practice of such authorities, the War- rington Committee 'admit reporters, which, so Ear as it goes, may seem to place Warrington well in advance of places that settle their assess- ment questions behind closed doors. But there are occasions even at Warrington when the points raised and_ the questions. invoKea are of so .delicate a nature that, it .nlaY not be right that they should be published. Then the com- mittee close down upon the reporters, who are forbidden to publish, perhaps, the choicest morsel of the year. • At last Friday's meeting the agenda con- tained an exceedingly interesting item. Nl'. Gunson submitted his valuation of the Corpora- -1 I Lion undertakings and Mr. W. H. Woodcock, assisthnt overseer, made some interesting ob- servations on the report and objected to certain figures. But at the conclirsidn .of an hour's fight the committee resolved that the Press should not have Mr. Gunson's figures and should .not report on what Mr. Woodcock had said. Mr. Gunson was of the opinion that reporters should not be allowed in the meetings at all, and Mr. Woodcock held the view that his remarks should not appear in the 'news- papers. The Clerk, Mr. Bottomley, explained that the Warrington Committee were the only committee in Lancashire who allowed reporters to be present. So far as we are concerned, IA think it would be better for Warrington to follow the usual 'ustom and exclude reporters rather than that the hesitating, picking and choosing policy should prevail.</p>
        <p>THE SHADOWLAND OF DREAMS. In the shadowland of dreams, dear, Within its mystic veil, Unseen, unknown to all but me, Thy form in lustre pale Appears, and in appearing gives To conjured phantasy The semblance of an angel's mould— In dreams I dream of thee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>FIRST REFRAIN:</head>
        <p>FIRST REFRAIN:</p>
        <p>Beneath the veil of sleep thy form, Like an angel's image, seems A-nigh to me in shadowland— The shadowland of dreams.</p>
        <p>In the shadowland of dreams, dear, Thy voice, sa soft and sweet, Comes floating o'er the realm of space My list'ning ear to greet, And in the greeting murmurs low, To bless and comfort me, A melody of truth and love— In dreams I dream of thee.</p>
        <p>SECOND REPEATS teneall the—v—eirOrslee Thy voice, Like an angel's whisper, seems</p>
        <p>Aznigh to mein shadowland— The shadowland of dreams. R. OSSULSTON-RICTIE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>PLATELAYER TROUBLES.</head>
        <p>PLATELAYER TROUBLES.</p>
        <p>Will there be Another Strike at Warrington ?</p>
        <p>OFFICIAL ACIION RESENTED.</p>
        <p>BY "A WORKER."</p>
        <p>Fresh trouble seems brewing amongst the ' platelayers on the Cheshire Lines, and War- rington may become the centre of another strike. The feeling amongst the men is that the surprise intervention by the officials from Unity , House during the strike in December led to a hurried settlement, which was a virtual defeat for the strikers, and this action by Unity House is now keenly resented. The platelayers state that they were virtually "jockeyed" into a settlement by their own officials. Now they have asked that immediate, application be made for a special meeting of the Conciliation Board for the purpose of giving an advance of wages. The average wage, the men state, works out at about ,Cl Os. 6d. per week. Unless the request is granted there is a grave danger of the men striking again. A Pointed Resolution. • The feeling of the platelayers is shown by the following resolution, adopted at a meeting of the Vigilance Committee h6d in Warrington on Sunday: "That this meeting of the Joint 'Vigi- lance Committee repudiates the statement con- tained in Mr. T. E. Williams's letter of the 29th ult., charging members of the organisation with misleading the platelayers. We desire to express our appreciation to members• of other grades, who, at considerable inconvenience to them- selves, gave us their assistance. We call upon our general secretary to withdraw such charges and furnish us with an explanation of this intervention, and the name of the individual, 'if any, who asked for same. We desire to add that the men do not appreciate the efforts of those who led them back to working conditions from which they revolted, and that such inter- vention coming when it did left no other course open to this committee but to settle the dispute as speedily as possible." Railway Policemen.</p>
        <p>Should policemen be trade unionists? At least one railway company has decided that their police must not join the National Union of Railwaymen, and members who have done so have been given notice to quit the company's service. Evidently this particular concern seem to think that for the " lordly" pay of 255. per week they own the policeman, body and soul. There are also a few Chief Constables in different parts of the country who have forbidden their constables becoming trade unionists. Their plea is that if a constable is a trade unionist he might be too sympathetic with others in times of strike. Possibly these chiefs would like the officers to he of the type of the Dublin police, about which we have heard so much recently. But there is not a Chief Constable who is not a member of the Chief Constables' Association, which is a trade union in every- thing but name. Why should not the lower officer have as much right to combine as has the higher officer—the Chief Constable? The old fable about fish and fowl is once- more illus- trated in this instance.</p>
        <p>Different Tents. Mrs. flightone: "Yes, my husband comes of a noble family. His ancestors won great renown on the tented field." Mrs. Wibbles : "110 w odd! My husband's people were in the circus business, too."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <p>DEMAND FOR AN</p>
        <p>ELECTION.</p>
        <p>It_ is at well to inquire what are the real facts of the 'ca.se in view of the stock demand of the Conservative party for an election. Three elections inlet) ' yore vithfl</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <p>result, ought to be sufficient for ordinary 'appe- tites, but when a party's policy has been reduced merely to asking for election after election—presumably on the off-chance that something may "turn up "—we are in the presence of abnormal circumstances. • True, the contention was advanced in the House of Lords, after the General Election of January, 1910, that the verdict of the electors gave a mandate for the passing of the Budget, but not for any legislation to curb the veto of the House of Lords. But the General Election of</p>
        <p>December, 1910, gave the constituencies yet another oppOrtunity to declare whether they wished the veto of the House of Lords to be curbed. The result of that General Election was to re-affirm the 'desire of the electors to curb the Lords' powers of obstruction.</p>
        <p>The</p>
        <p>mandate which the Liberal Government re- ceived was given with the text of the Parliament Bill in the hands of the electors. But, the Opposition object, the electors had no suspicion that the Parliament Act would be used to pass a Home Bute Bill, and the electors only approved the Parliament Bill because they understood that the Liberal Government was pledged to reconstitute the Second Chamber. On LORD LAisrsoowNE's own showing, the Prime Minister had made it perfectly clear that, amongst other legislation which it was intended to pass under a 'Parliament Act, an Irish self-government measure would be one of the most important. And the electors also knew, from the preamble of the Parliament Bill, that %the Government's intention was to curb the veto of the Lords immediately, and to enter upon the task of rec. onstituting the Second Chamber afterwards.</p>
        <p>Under the Parliament Act, the _Opposition say the Constitution is suspended, and the Home Rule Bill must not be passed until another General Election has been held. If the Con- stitution is suspended, the Opposition is cow- ardly and remiss in merely demanding another General Election on Home Rule. They should demand another General Election on the still more pressing issue of rescuing the Constitution from duress. But, at any rate, the electorate in December, 1910, did not vote for any more power to be given to the Second Chamber than it enjoys under" the Parliament Act. The Opposition, it is more than probable, hoped to be able, under colourable terms, to re-establish the permanent veto of the Tory party, through a Second Chamber, on progressive legislation. But the electorate voted, in approving the pre- amble to the Parliament Bill, for the creation of a Second Chamber which would not, like the existing House of Lords, oppose with a blank , negative progressive legislation; and the elec- torate's support for the main body of the Par- liament Bill was given in order to allow long over-due reforms—including Home Rule—to make their way to the Statute Book.</p>
        <p>To what end were the two General Elections of 1910 fought and won? For this end in an especial degree, that it should no longer be possible for the Tory party, when in Opposition, to force a General Election on any and every issue on which they chose to invoke the veto of I the House of Lords. The- British democracy is resolute against surrendering to any manmuvre or any clap-trap the rights which it asserted and won at two General* Elections.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <p>t</p>
        <p>STRIKES AND POLIIICS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Charles Dukes and a Delicate Question. NOUGHT DOING.</p>
        <p>TO VIE EDITOR OF "THE EXAMINER.'</p>
        <p>Sir,—l concluded my last letter by asking "Labor Omnia Vincit " if he was prepared defi- nitely to oppose either the strike weapon or political action as a means of working class emancipation, but from the letter in your issue of January 10th it appears that he is more anxious to hold me up for public examination than to do either. He makes reference to the important position I hold, and continues to ask questions in the interests he claims to serve. Now, if I am to make any public declaration of policy, or offer any explanation for my sup- posed change of view, I shall first of all require to know who desires the information. If any body that I servo care to make that claim, I shall be only too pleased to oblige them, but salirk individuals have interests to serve, as "Labor Omnia Vincit " states, I think such individuals should at least name the interests at stake. •</p>
        <p>"Labor Omnia Vincit " asks what I should think if our positions were 'reversed. Well, judging from past events, I should think a miracle had happened, and I have ceased to believe in miracles. The ways and means adopted to organise the Men and women in the Gas Workers' and General Labourers' Union have proved the right ones by results; why, even employers of labour have objected to them. But. never mind, "Labor Omnia Vincit." We are starting another .campaign—more street corner meetings," and then, perhaps, " Labor Omnia Vincit " will be able to detect 'another change of view. I know what he says is true, that scores are sick of hearing it, but we take a de- light in niaking-that type of people vomit; the emetic often contains what we are fighting for, the lucre. There has been a wonderful illustra- tion of this towards Bank Quay. Hush! But what about those who " rat " after it has been done? Don't they make "Labor Omnia Vin- cit " feel sick also?</p>
        <p>Yes, I yet believe the fight is in the work- shop. What is more, I know "Labor Omnia Vincit does not, and I further know that it is more convenient to believe the latter than the former. It has caused me quite a lot of trouble, but it's true. Let me again state that the solution to the problem is how to monopo- lise labour power. When all men and women hold control of their labour, it will puzzle any form of parasite to exploit it. I am sorry I did not make myself clear at the Freedom Fellowship meeting, at any rate to "Labor Omnia Vincit," and the friend he quotes as supporting his statement. Quite a number seem to have got hold of it all right, and one can't hope to convince everybody, even when agreed on first principles. However, that's a pleasing reminder about me whispering in his simple ear that I believed there was some- thing in the "big man" theory. I muse have been wonderfully susceptible to his overtures on that occasion. Perhaps I wanted to agree with him. What a terrible nightmare he Must 'lave had when he pictured my change of cir- cumstances. "Labor Omnia Vincit " must be- lieve me when I say it is a common malady with many persons tour auditors not included). Those hallucinations must have cost him dear. I should try a change of diet. He says my change is quite natural, which certainly consoles me somewhat; when things occur according to nature they may be taken as correct. Now, when he says I have changed and the working class has not, the logical de- duction is that we have got apart. When people get outside tire working class they usually enter the middle or upper class, but even "Labor Omnia Vincit " must see the humour of this.</p>
        <p>There is one factor I-am extremely obliged to "Labor Omnia Vincit" for, and that is the audit of my services during the last three years. I wonder what any respectable firm would have given for it "reversed." Suppose, instead oi shouting from the housetops, as he reports me having said, "To h—l with capitalism," I had shouted "Boy Scouts," "National Service," "Co-partnership," and "Three cheers for free labour," I wonder if he would have as readily taken up his 'pen? And yet these things are being:done effectively, and instead of friendly Pen wags turning their ability to account. they appear content to wait for loopholes in their own knitting.</p>
        <p>Suppose I do change. What matters? My little influence will not stop progress. There are too many straight fighters in the field to allow the fall of one man to count, but I sup- pose these doubts are the common lot of men at the front. The pity is that changes in the ranks are not taken into consideration; there have been some changes since the time to which "Labor Oninia Vincit" refers, especially in the school from which he now seeks a tacking. faialnedd I remember another occasion when he attended a meeting addressed by myself, at which he then claimed to knowbettAelil persuasion what I meant than I did myself. to convince him that what I meant was not what he thought, so we had better agree to cliffer, as there is a lot more useful work to do than making useless attempts at reconciling the obstinate,</p>
        <p>--li"Vfth regard to his delicate question, suppose tell him that the proper place to ask these questions is through those organisations who would have to accept the responsibility of such a venture. If he asked the Duke of Clough Hole whether he intended throwing the bomb into the next Cabinet meeting, he would politely tell him to .go to the d—l. However, since he is desirous of knowing, I suggest he inserts it in the queries column of "The Examiner." "Nought doing."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>CIIAS. DUKES.</head>
        <p>CIIAS. DUKES.</p>
        <p>DEFINING THE ISSUES. T 6 TIIE EDITOR OF "THE EXAMINER." Sir,—Behind the disoussion at present going forward in your columns there are important issues at stake, which are liable to be clouded under the verbosity of Mr. Dukes and the sor- rowful Pessimism of "Labor Omnia Vincit"; and perhaps it may not be to the disadvantage of the general public, and especially of the is sues clearly community, to have these clearly defined.</p>
        <p>After all, the real matter of interest is not a conflict between personalities, but a conflict between policies, and neither of the combatants at present waging wordy war in your Paper seems very definite about this all-important difference. Supposing we assume that we are all agreed that the working classes need some sort of betterment in their position, or that society, as a whole, requires readjustment and the question is then asked how this is to be brought about, how are we to proceed to work? In answering this question three different atti- tudes may be adopted. In the first place we may possibly declare with "Labor Omnia Vincit" that it cannot be done, "No, not nohow," as the fishwife might say. This atti- tude of pessimistic despair we may dismiss as unfruitful. In the second place, we may, per-, haps, agree with Mr. Dukes that the right , method is political; or, in the third place, we may contend, as many are now contending, that the true and only way is the way of what is called industrial action. It is between these two that the real issue lies, and it is here that clearness is necessary.</p>
        <p>Political and Industrial Action. The political method can be easily defined; it consists and subsists in urging the workers to make such use of their voting power at the polls as to return in every possible case a "Labour Member" to Parliament, and in this way to establish a Labour majority in the Government of the country, which shall by legislation bring the desired changes to pass. We do not intend at the moment- to criticise this method; we shall simply define and set side by side with it the method of industrial action. It happens that industrial action is as easy to define as political</p>
        <p>(Conitintted in next cot? in )</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>JOLLEY NOT 10 PLAY.</head>
        <p>JOLLEY NOT 10 PLAY.</p>
        <p>The Full-back Decides Not to Travel to Hull. BROOKES ALSO AN ABSENTEE..</p>
        <p>Jolley, about whose attitude to the strike there was some doubt, has apparently thrown in -his lot with the rest of the first team on strike. "The Examiner" representative was officially informed at a meeting of We" Team Committee last night "That Jolley was not turning out with the team which travels to Hull."</p>
        <p>The players on strike yesterday issued, under the heading of "Warrington Football Club." the following notice: • "The Playing Members of the above club. not having access to the forthcoming General' Meeting, request all Members attending the "A"' team match on Saturday next. January 17th (to-day) to Stay Behind a few minutes after the conclusion of the game to hear their ex- planation as to the dispute." It was officially announced later in the even- ing that Brookes will not accompany the team.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>STRIKERS' MANIFESTO.</head>
        <p>STRIKERS' MANIFESTO.</p>
        <p>The strikers last night issued a manifesto, in: which they maintain that the dispute was. over a Christmas present and not a bonus. The committee decided to give them a good present for the Wigan match, and to increase this if they kept fit during the holiday matches,whether they won or lost, and the players thought that by gaining seven points out of a possible. ten, ..E1 was `not sufficient.</p>
        <p>The committee refused to see them after the Huddersfield match, and they sent their captain, back.,to tell the secretary that if the committee did not meet them on Friday night the players would not turn out on the Saturday. . They met seven committee men wile.' refused to have anything to do with it whatever, and told the players that they would crmd a team to Hull, under any consideration. They,came out and decided not to go to Hull The committee had promised £lOO bonus if the team got in the first four in the League. The players promised, if they achieved that, they would agree to the extra gift being deducted. They received .tl. which worked out at 4s. each holiday match.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>ECHOES OF CHRIS'MAS</head>
        <p>ECHOES OF CHRIS'MAS</p>
        <p>What is it that makes the Christmas fare so •acceptable,' and causes it to stand out in such hold and pleasant contrast to the food generally consumed throughout the year Of course it is such grand diet as the Christmas pudding anti the mince-pie. Well, to get to the prime cause we must certainly see that the little Greek Cur- rant is the motive power•of Yuletide enjoyment. None of the famous Christmas dishes would ha possible without the Currant, which, by reason of its savouriness, nutritive power, and easy digestibility, so nourishes and satisfies the body, and induces health, that all who eat it enjoy the radiance of feeling "fit." Obviously, the USEA of the Currant ought not, to be restricted to Christmas time. The fruit should form part of the daily menu. There are countless ways of using Currants, all of which are delightful. Here is an excellent recipe, which, once _tried, wil'. surely be repeated. CURRANT LEMON PUDDING. ozs. Currants, 3 ozs. suet, 3 ozs. re 'I crumbs, 2 ozs. flour, 4 ozs. sugar, 1 egg. yin.l and juice of I lemon. IIIrrrtoo: Mix all the ingredi- ents together; put in a greased basin, and cover with greased paper. Steam 2 hours.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>MILITARY tON‘httT. The Local. Army Band Draw Record Crowd to Parr Hall.</head>
        <p>MILITARY tON‘httT. The Local. Army Band Draw Record Crowd to Parr Hall.</p>
        <p>A " BUMPER ' ATTENDANCE.</p>
        <p>any respectable firm would have .. was 'occupied. In t or it "rewersed." Suppose, instead</p>
        <p>The Parr Hall has been the scene of numerous functions, which, in varying degrees, have acted like a Magnet upon the townspeople and drawn audiences large and small, but it speaks well for the town's love of music, or of its admiration of the 2nd Battalion South Lancashire Regi- mental Band, that never before has the acconi- modation of the Hall been so greatly taxed as it was on Sunday. Every seat was "claimed a considerable time before the programme com-. menced, and all the standing room inside the Hall was occupied In addition, the corridors verezdsooely,- racked' with people. - The first-class programme includcd : Saint Saen's "Marche Ileroique," Sibelius' valse " Triste," LeoncaVallo's selection "1 Pagliacci," "Prelude," Rackmaninoff Woodforde Finden' "A lover in Damascus," composed of a suite of six Oriental songs; Handel's "Largo," awl Wagner's overture from " Tannhauser." There was evidence of much enthusiasm amongst the audience, and this was most marked at the conclusion of the overture to " Tann- hauser." Mr. IL E. Cahill was an tittle con- ductor. --</p>
        <p>MAYOR GIVES THANKS. The MAYou, "in the name of the inliabitant:4 of the borough," proposed a rote of thanks to the band,•to Major Green for the organisation of the concert, and Mr. Cahill for conducting. It Was a pleasure, he said, to find that there</p>
        <p>were some'Warringtonians in the band. As a result of the concert the previous Sunday, ..t4* had been handed over to the Infirmary. Enter- tainments of that class mu:st make for a better understanding between the civilians and the Army. It was to be hoped that the townsmen would join either the Territorial or the Regulars. The vote was heartily carried.' BAND AT PARISH CHURCH.</p>
        <p>_ . As is customary on visits to the town, the band accompanied' the service at the Parish Church on Sunday, when the services of the organ were dispensed with. The instruments gave a beautifuiDorgan-like tone. The congre- gation was large. As an opening voluntary, "Judea," from Gonnod's "Mors et Vita," wa43 given, and at the close.of the service the "Boat- ing Song" from Mendelssohn's "Songs without words," and Gounod's "Marche wera rendered. The Rev. Cation Willis preached: BEATING UP RECRUITS.</p>
        <p>_ _ The idea behind the visit of the band is to bring to the mind of the youth of South Lan- cashire that-theY have a regiment 'of their own, and to induce those who have any inclination for a military life to join their local regiment. With this end in view the band, accompanied by a number of officers and men, left the Orford Barracks for a tour through St. Helens, Widnes and the surrounding district. They are ex- pected to return to Warrington this morning.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>STRIKES AND POLlTlCS—continued.</head>
        <p>STRIKES AND POLlTlCS—continued.</p>
        <p>action. Those who believe in industrial action hold strongly that the main difficulties besetting. the social organism are economic and industrial difficulties, and that the way towards a solution of industrial problems must be purely an indus- trial wan- political action, legislation from above, will not achieve the end. On .the con- trary, yen must begin from below, from the heart of the industrial field itself, leaving, for the time being, politics to take care of them- selves. This conviction dictates a clear, prac- tical policy. It demands utter concentration on the following things: (1) Complete organisa- tion of the workers into effectkve groups, with consequent complete elimination of the "black- leg"; (2) federation of such groups into an efficient and easily manageable whole; _(3) the strategic use of the one explicit and peculiarly workers of a genuine class Me-IY. the strike; and (4) ti nh de u cs rt re aiitdi mu na consciousness and a true realisation of their- economic and personal worth. In this line of action there is nothing political in the ordinary sense of that word, and the distinction is per- fectly clear. The believer in industrial action I says in effect that the work of saving society must be done not at the polling booths, tut in the branch meetings and general meetings of trade unions, representing organised labour, nsay also that any worker who has the- future adtheyat heart should be giving his whole energy to the labour of organisation, to the preaching of solidarity, and to the transforma- tion of the proletariate from a chaotic mob int(*) a thoroughly disciplined army. We beg, sir, to offer these clear definitions anti. distinctions to your readers in the hope flat- you will permit a fair discussion of" this radi- cally important issue, and that, in this lay, something worth while may eventuate from the difference of opinion now being thrashed out by Tour main combatants.—We %re, ynu,s • faithfully,</p>
        <p>'TILE FRIENDS OF LABOUR.</p>
      </div>
