      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>LOCAL INTELLIGENCE.</head>
        <p>LOCAL INTELLIGENCE.</p>
        <p>Interesting Engagement. The engagement of Mr. Holt Waring, Warings- t".n, Co. Down, captain in the North Irish Horse, and Miss Margaret Alicia Parr, youngest daughter of Mr. J. C. Parr, of Grappenhall Reyes, Warrington, and Staunton Park, Here- fordshire, is announced.</p>
        <p>Peace in Ireland. At the Anglican Churches in the town a solemn service of intercession for the preserva- tion of peace in Ireland was gone through on Sunday. The prayer had been authorised by the Bishop.</p>
        <p>A New Warrington Curate. The Rev. E. Downham, the new curate at St. Paul's Church, preached his first sermon at that church on Sunday morning, when he made a very good impression. Mr. Downham was for- merly curate of St. Catherine's, Wigan, and until lately curate-in-charge of Lowton Church. Brewers and a Licensee. At the Borough Police Court on Monday Mr. Jenkins made an application on behalf of Messrs. Greenall, Whitley and Co. Ile asked permission for Henry Hankinson, of the King's Arms, Buttermarket-street, to take charge of the Crown and Sceptre. It would be, he said, within the recollection of the Bench that East. wood .was convicted at that Court a few days age, and the firm were anxious to have the house conducted in a impel —manner until the recon- struction.—The application was granted .</p>
        <p>Ilesketh and Son, Painters and Decorators, Warrington, This old-established firm, of some 70 years' standing, which has up to recently been trading a 8 a limited company, announces that it has ain become a private concern. It will con- , Italie under the same management as hitherto _!•0 execute all painting and decorating work !?xtrusted to it, with the economy and in that ("rough and workmanlike manner for which the firm has been noted.</p>
        <p>Settled Upon Terms. In the Official Receiver's Court in London on Monday it was announced that a settlement had been arrived at in the case between William Joseph -Parkyn,_trading as David Adamson and Co., Dukinfield, plaintiffs, and Messrs. Joseph _Cr f osfioeld and Sons, Ltd., of Warrington. The local firm had been sued in respect of machinery de ivered at their works, whilst they counter- claimed for a large sum, alleging breach of con- tract. Mr. Pollock, the referee, was informed that the case had been settled upon terms. Thirty Years a Coroner. Mr. Sam Brighouse yesterday completed 30 years' service as Coroner for the South-West Lancashire district, which includes Southport Bootle, St. Helens, Warrington, Widnes, and 79 townships. He was the last Coroner to be appointed by the freeholders of the county, and the election cost Mr. Brighouse £1,419. Up to the present he and his deputy have held about 15,000 inquests, among them the inquiry following the Maypole Colliery disaster. The proceedings in connection with this extended from August 20, 1908, to July 8, 1909, while the jury were also called together on several subsequent occasions when bodies were found. Mr. Brighouse, who is 63, once declared that he should stick to his Position as Coroner as long as he could.</p>
        <p>Lady Health Visitors. The Health Committee 'Tuesday, out of 21 aPplicants, appointed two lady health visitors The ladies appointed were Miss Ruth Redfern, Willis-street, Warrington, and Miss Emily Han ilngten, Crewe Green, Crewe. Miss Redfern is Zpresent engaged in private nursing and has. Nas held the h rved 118 a district nurse at Burton-on-Trent. nzellospital, appointments of ward sister at the Ashten-under-Lyne, and charge She holdat the Runcorn Union Hospital, Dutton- tificates s the general training and C.M.8.8. cer• tan Herewe Miss arrington is rturSe on the IlEstate. In addition to the general raining he and C M.B. certificates she has gained t -,. diploma of. the Sanitary Training College London. Her experience has been obtained in kondon, and at the Queen,Sictoria., Jubilee ntsing Home, Wolverhampton.</p>
        <p>Captain Crosfield Entertains. , With a view to bringing about a pleasant tiersion from the ordinary routine of daily Captain Crosfield, who is well- known foi the keen interest he takes in the working men of the town, invited the members of the Men's Bible Class associated with the Working Men's Misgdork, Bank Quay, and of which he is Presi- dent, to accompany him on a visit to Manchester on Saturday evening. Leaving the Central Station by the 6.55 train in special reserved car- riages, the party arrived at their destination lust in time to proceed to the New Theatre, in Quay-street, for the raising .of the curtain on What proved to be a highly interesting historical I?..atriotic pageant, under the name of "Drake.' A splendid building, with a magnificent stage, well adapted for the production of such plays, served to give the men a vivid idea of the hap- Pnings in the Elizabethan period. The scenic effects were very much :admired, and the per- formance was greatly enjoyed. On arriving hack in Warrington, Captain Crosfield was thanked by the men for the evening's entertain-, tuent he had provided.</p>
        <p>Not Rival Leagues. - At the annual meeting of the Working Mert's Mission League of Help Society on Tuesday evening, Mr. G. Dale, the chairman, in making a review of the work for the past year, said there was no truth in the statement that rivalry existed between the League and the Citizens' Guild of Help. Many of their workers were linked up with the central Guild; and in dealing with important cases the co-operation of both societies had produced some good result&amp; in fact the Citizen's Guild of Help would heartily welcome the assistance to be rendered by -any other society of a like nature associated with the churches of the town. In passing, he wished to assure them that the work of the Citizens' Guild of Help, although quiet and unobtrusive, ' Ir„ais Proving of vital importance to the well- being of the town. As regarded their on w League, a large amount of good had been done, !Intl although their expenditure had not been ?ugh, on account of better conditions prevailing at Present, yet the efforts put forward compared favourably with previous years. During the l'ast five years t.g164 had been expended in cash Woods, which, when considered on a yearly, of tilgO of about £3l, showed that the existence e League had been fully justified.—Mr. ltworth, as secretary, gave a detailed report orifin_the Year's work, after which a discussion took ' "44' ou future efforts.</p>
        <p>The Modern Laundry. The laundry is among the institutions of the "nntrY that reveal to us at a glance how we „ have advanced in recent years and left old cus- toms behind. In the Latchford steam Laundry ' ,We Bee enterprise and efficiency going hand in nand in as enlightened a manner as in any of the long-established industries: we might go further and say that the laundry methods would Put those of some older concerns in a very bad light. Not only is your washing done for you in favourable conditions as to light and air in Thelwall-lane, but it is collected and delivered in a manner that has speed and the care of the goods as chief objectives. The laundry has just placed on the road a very smart motor van of the best make and type, Which has a specially constructed body suitable to the laundry business. The van is painted a Royal blue, with gilded letters, and is fitted With Michelin twin-wheel tyres. Realising that motor traction is the power of the future, and that it will to a large extent eventually super- sede horse traction, and wishing to he always abreast of the times, the company intend later to introduce lighter motor, vehicles for town and more local work. The area covered by the laundry has so extended that the work of col- lection and delivery is now beyond the capacity of horse traction. Besides, linen sent by train gets tossed about, to the annoyance and incon- venience of customers. To avoid this and to ensure that the linen is delivered in a fit and .satisfactory condition, it is the' intention of the laundry in future, where possible, to use their sawn vans.</p>
        <p>Dow/ Look OLD Heap tberaysges of time at bay by rtkelling ills grey crtreakß with LOCKYEIV SSULPHIIR Hem eQs,_ToTwambieh deepens to finatnraleolourin a few days. _caYer's maintains a vonthfnl appearance. Large bots `Yez7Where.Post free it Bedford Laboratory,Landon.S.</p>
        <p>Lord Acton Better.</p>
        <p>So much improvement has been made in Lord Acton's health that he was able to leave Apple- ton Hall on Monday for Darmstadt. He was accompanied by Lady Acton and children.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>Life and Death.</head>
        <p>Life and Death.</p>
        <p>The births in Warrington last week were 40, giving a rate of 28.1. The deaths numbered 26, giving a rate of 18.2, compared with 12.0 in the same week last year. There were 17 new cases of scarlet fever and 54 cases were in hospital.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>Cab Drivel's Sudden Death.</head>
        <p>Cab Drivel's Sudden Death.</p>
        <p>Whilst at the Police Station the other day, Frank Wilson, a cab driver, employed by Maginn Bros. and who lived at 314, Knutsford- road, died suddenly. Dr. Manson was sum- moned, but found life extinct. Death was due to heart failure.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>Saved the Market Hall.</head>
        <p>Saved the Market Hall.</p>
        <p>The promptitude of the local Fire Brigade alone prevented a fire, which might have re- sulted very seriously in the Market Hall on Sunday evening. A large hamper was in flames, but a few buckets of water sufficed to extinguish the fire.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>New Irish League President.</head>
        <p>New Irish League President.</p>
        <p>Owing to Mr. D. Currie, the excellent and ever- courteous President of the local branch of the United, Irish League, leaving the town for the much larger district of Birmingham, Mr. D. P. lfahony was on Tuesday evening appointed a,s his successor.</p>
        <p>Musical Success. Miss May Pollitt, of 24, Froghall-lane, has been successful in obtaining the diploma of Associate of .Trinity College, London, in piano- forte playing, art of teaching and theory of music.. Miss Pollitt is a pupil of Mr. W. 11. Payton, Mi. Bac.</p>
        <p>Enamel Works' Fire. A fire" broke out at the enamelling works of Messrs. Richmond's, Ltd., Dallam-lane, on Monday night. The brigade were soon on the spot with the new engine, and it was found that one of the drying stoves had become overheated and the gable-end of the building was in flames, which were quickly subdued.</p>
        <p>Warrington Market. Prices on Wednesday were: Wheat, 46. 44. to 4s. exl. per 701 b.; oats, 2s. 6d. to N. Bd. per 451 b.; wheat straw, 295. per ton; oat straw, 31s. to 325. per ton; XX flour, 235. per 2401 b.; bran, is, 3d. Per 201 b. Wheat is slightly better sold, and corn has an -upward tendency. Oats remain firm, and the prospects are better.</p>
        <p>Generous Pleasure Seekers. The collections made at the various places of amusement in town on behalf of the Old People's Treat realised -e4B Bs. 7d., contributed as follows: At the Hippodrome, 423 ss. 6d.; Royal Court Theatre, .tll 17s. 3d.; New Picture House, £5 Bs. 5d.; Park Picture Palace, „t 2 14s. 9d.; Picturedrorne, „t 2 Bs. 5d.; Andrews', £24s. ad.; Theatre Royal, 9s, 6(1.</p>
        <p>Warrington Philomathic Society. The fortnightly meeting of the Warrington Philomathic Society was held on Wednesday evening in the Old Parlour, Cairo-street. The President (Mr. James Walsh) occupied the chair. The programme for the evening con- sisted of a selection of humorous readings by members, organised by Mr. li. Dutton, in which Messrs. H. M. Ashton, H. Dutton, W. B. For- -0 J. Mason, N. Nield, and Taylor.Jolso intoon R. , took part</p>
        <p>Wood's Tutorial College. A representative gathering of students and their friends assembled at Atkinson's Cafe on Wednesday evening to participate in a whist drive, games and dancing. After supper Mr. Travis presented the certificates and prizes to the students successful in the recent Pitman examination. Presentations were made to Mr.l F. W. Wood (principal) and his assistant, Miss C. Butler, on behalf of the students, by Miss E. Wadsworth.</p>
        <p>, • Success of the Missionary Exhibition: Satisfactory Results. The Treasurer of the Warrington and District Missionary Exhibition, Mr. C. A. Littler, re- ports a net balance of .8318 4s. Bd. to be handed over to the Central Missionaries Societies .in London.</p>
        <p>As the primary object of the exhibition was not to make money, but to arouse and sustain interest in Missionary work, the finances may be said to have worked out in a highly satis- factory manner. The enthusiasm displayed, and the all-round success of the exhibition. may be taken as evidence that many people are con- vine-d that the one effective solution of the world's problems lies in the growth and power of the Church.</p>
        <p>Bold-street Cripples' Guild: The Mayor's Kindness. The annual Christmas tree and concert were held in the:Bold-street Schoolroom on Wednes_ day evening. About 130 cripples sat down to tea, after which an enjoyable entertainment was provided by the members of the Sunday School. Miss Carrie Hastings received a hearty applause for her songs "Annie Laurie" and "Mifanwy"; also Mrs. Owen, of Penketh, who kindly, gave a reading; Mr. Brobbin4 two concertina solos; and the Misses 'Caine, Cross and Knight, in a Jap- anese trio; and Miss Cross in a Japanese song. The Mayor and Mayoress, who were introaced by Mr. Frank Hickman (the chairman) at- tended. The Mayor, in a few kind and inter- esting remarks to the cripples, surprised them by saying he and Mrs. Peacock had 3ezided to give them a garden party in the sumnor.</p>
        <p>Golhorne-street Baptist Scholars. The Sunday School annual party was held on Thtireday, when some 140 scholars and friends sat down to an excellent tea. This over, games were indulged in for a while, after which a mis- cellaneous entertainment was given. The Rev. H. Ogden presided, and duets, songs and recita- tions Secretary gave eta a-ere rendered a report,nderedby the scholars. The which showed that numerically and otherwise the school was in a very flourishing condition, and urged all to do what they could to still further the cause of the Master in this corner of the vineyard. Mr. A. Still gave a report of the work on behalf of Foreign Missions, which showed that the amount raised was increasing year by year. He then . presented medals to Ada , Hamer and Florrie Jones for; collecting on behalf of the society's Native Preachers' Fund. During a break in the proceedings prizes for regular attendance werepresentTedhe Pastor's prizes for to of the chil- dren by Mr. Ogden. those who introduced the most newscholars during the year were presented to _ MissA . Pennington and Master Edward Michael</p>
        <p>Prosperous Parr's. The 49th ordinary general meeting of Parr's Bank, Ltd., will take place in London on the 29th inst., when a resolution authorising an increase of capital to £15,000,000 will be sub- mitted. In their report for the past year the directors say that the position and prospects of the busi- ness continue to be in every way satisfactory. Including .8185,446 14s. brought forward at 31st December, 1912, the net balance of profits for the year after writing down investments to below market value, providing for all bad and doubtful debts, contributing to the Staff Widows' Fund, and rebating the current bills at six per cent. is .1:700,601 6s. Bd.• out of this an interim divi- dend at the rate Of 20 per cent. per annum (less income tax) for the half-year ended 30th June, 1913, was paid on sth August, .8207,616 159. Bd.; leaving .8492,984 lie. From this sum the direc- tors recommend: The payment of a half-year's dividend at the rate of 20 per cent. per annum, less income tax, payable on sth February, .8207,616 15s. Bd.; a bonus of one per cent., less income tax, payable on sth February, .820,761 13s. 7d.; the transfer to bank premises account, £30,000; the transfer to officers' pension and provident fund, £25,000; and the transfer to reserve fund (making the Fund .82,100,000), .8100,000; leaving balance to be carried to next account, £109,606 la9d.</p>
        <p>OSRAM and TANTALUM ELECTRIC LAMPS at reduced prices.—Agent, J. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge-street, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 17, 1914.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 17, 1914.</p>
        <p>Dr. Adams Better.</p>
        <p>We are glad to report that Dr. Adams, who has been suffering from pneumonia, is progress- ing favourably.</p>
        <p>Infirmary Ball. A special dancing floor will be laid at the Parr Hall next Monday on the occasion of the annual ball in aid of the Infirmary. Bayley's Band will provide the music.</p>
        <p>"A Message _From Mars " : To tile Park</p>
        <p>Picture Palace. - A special attraction is announced at the Park Picture Palace for next week, when Charles Hawtrey will appear in "A Message from Mars," a sermon on selfishness.</p>
        <p>Well-known Nl.P.'s Visit. A mass meeting will be held on Monday in the Co-operative Hall, under the auspices of the Junior Liberal Association, when Mr. J. Griffiths will preside. Amongst the speakers will be Mr. P. M. Oliver and Mr. A. G. C. Harvey, M.P. for Rochdale.</p>
        <p>P.S.A. Comes of Age. The coming of age of the Warrington JP.S.A. will be celebrated to-morrow, when a meeting will be held in the Park Picture Palace. Mr. E. Lunt is to preside, and the principal speaker will be Mr. E. Wynne Jones. A musical pro- gramme has been arranged.</p>
        <p>"Tragedy of Charles Lamb." At the meeting of the Wycliffe Y.P.S., held on Thursday night, the Rev. T. T. James occupied the chair. Mr. Sydney Jeffery lectured on "The Tragedy of Charles Lamb," illustrating the author's life with passages from his works.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>Dr. E. J. Fox a Councillor.</head>
        <p>Dr. E. J. Fox a Councillor.</p>
        <p>Dr. Edward Joseph Fox, 12, R ylands- street, has been returned unopposed to the seat on the Council for Town llall Ward, vacant by the death of Councillor T. Births. Mr. George Thomas Wilding, an ex-councillor and a house furnisher, 93, Buttermarket-street, was nomin- ated, but ,hitit candidature Was withdrawn on Thursday. ,</p>
        <p>Brick-street Mission. A tea patty was held in the schoolroom on Thursday in connection with the Sunday school, for the distributing of annual prizes to the scholars. After tea a concert was held, and a very enjoyable entertainment was gone through. Solos were admirably sung by Miss Alice Furni- val, and Mr. Law, police court missioner, gave an address to the children and afterwards pre- sented the prizes. A humorous song was ren- dered by Mr. J. H. Bibby, and this took well with the audience.</p>
        <p>Stop That Smoke! Much interest has been aroused in the smoke crusade in Warrington, and in order to give the public additional facts the Warrington and Dis- trict Smoke Abatement League have arranged for Professor Vivian B. Lewes, an expert upon the subject; to give a public lecture in the Co- operative Hall on Wednesday evening. "The Atmosphere and Smoke" will be the subject, which will be illustrated by experiments. The lecture should be largely attended, as it deals with a theme of vital importance to Warring- ton. Mr. C. E. Parker will take the chair at 8 p.m.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>Fire Engine in Collision.</head>
        <p>Fire Engine in Collision.</p>
        <p>'Warrington's ne7,v motor fire engine was in collision on Thursday night, and five of the brigade had to be treated at the ,Infirmary. Shortly before midnight they were proceeding to a fire at the Lion Hotel garage, and when passing Market Gate the engine came into colli- sion with a heavy motor waggon. The five men were thrown off the engine and received slight injuries. The engine was damaged, but was able to proceed to the scene of the fire, where it was found that a taxicab was ,on fire in the Lion Hotel yard. The flames were extinguished with a few buckets of sand.</p>
        <p>Women's Co-operative. Guild. A successful social was held in the Co-opera- tive Hall, Cairo-street, on Wednesday evening, over 200 being present. The Guild F:resident presided. Mr. Billinge, the district secretary, gave a stirring address on the aims and objects of the Guild. A fine musical programme was provided by Mr. Tomkinson, Miss Kershaw, Miss Davies, Mrs. Haviour, Mrs. Sefton, Mrs. Pye. Madame j4ly . Fairhurst, Miss Johnson. Miss E. Sherburn acted as accompanist. Whist and dancing were indulged in. Mr. Lowe and Mr. Eden Op M.C.'s for dancing and Mr. H. Sherburn. M.C.' for the whist drive. Whist result: Lady, Mrs. Higham; Gent., Mr.. Par- kinson; Lady as Gent., Mrs. Farrar. Mrs. J. H. Jones presented the prizes. Preachers for To-morrow. Bewsey-road Wesleyan—Morning, Mr. J. Jar- man; evening, Rev. W. A. Beckett. Legh-street Primitive Methodist—Morning and evening, Rev. A. J. Chessum. Golborne-street Baptist—Morning and even- ing—Rev. H. Ogden. Bold-street Wesleyan—Morning, Mr. S. Moss: evening, Rev. H. Williamson. Christadelphians—Evening, Mr. B. Lake, Manchester.</p>
        <p>Cairo-street Chapel—Morning and evening, Dr. Mellor. Latchford Baptist—Morning and evening, Rev. J. S. Cliedburn.</p>
        <p>_ , St. John's Rresbyterian—Morningjind even- ing. Rev. W. Reid.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>NEW. GRAND MASTER. Oddfellowat Appointment for a Well-known</head>
        <p>A NEW. GRAND MASTER. Oddfellowat Appointment for a Well-known</p>
        <p>Warringtonian. MAYOR TO BE AN ODDFELLOW.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Warrington Dis- trict of the Independent Order of Oddfellows, Manchester Unity, took place on Saturday at the Lodge Room, Pelican Hotel. Amongst those present were P.G.M. George Highara, P.P.G.M. Harry Lawrence, P.P.G.M.'s Whitelegg (Lymm). William Holbrook, Alban Boyle, T. Rigby, G. F. Freeman, Peter Lester; Prov.-C.S. Frank Webster, P.G. Thomas Wads- worth (treasurer), P.P.G.M. Massey (grand tyler), and delegates from the various lodges. At the close of a long business agenda the election of Provincial Grand Master for the ensuing year took place, and Prov. Deputy G.M. Harry Lawrence, of the "Prince of Wales" Lodge, was unanimously elected to the position. Brother Lawrence, who is a well-known War- ringtonian, was initiated a member of his lodge 18 years ago, and at once began to take an active interest in Oddfellowship. He was soon nominated for office, going through the various chairs of the lodo.es with considerable ability and zeal.</p>
        <p>He has, held the offices of lodge and district book examiner, district auditor, lodge auditor, and is a member of the Committee of Manage- ment.</p>
        <p>In 1912 Bro. Lawrence, along with the imme- diate Past Prov, G. Master Ingham, repre- sented the Warrington district at the important annual moveable eonierence of the Order, held at Nottingham during Whitweek, and has several times been elected a delegate to the Lan- cashire and adjacent counties conferences. His considerable knowledge of the National Insur- ance Act has been of great assistance to his lodge during the past two years, and the corn- plimentatf remarks of the various delegates on Saturday last were well merited.</p>
        <p>THE GRAND MASTER'S THANKS. The newly-elected Grand Master, in returning thanks, said he greatly appreciated the honour the brethren had bestowed upon him that day. Ile had looked forward with considerable pleas- ure to the time when he would he elected Pro- vincial Grand Master of the Warrington dis- trict. In reviewing the past year he thought they would be able to congratulate themselves upon being practically in as good a financial position now as they were 12 months ago, notwithstand- ing the very heavy sickness experience of some of the lodges during the first six months' admin- ist ration of the National Insurance Act. Tie asked for the loyal support of the mem- bers and officers of the lodges during his term of office, and he could assure them that he would use every endeavour to help forward the cause of Oddfellowship in Warrington. In conclusion Bro. Lawrenee announced that the Mayor, Councillor Peter Peacock. would he initiated a member of the "Prince of Wales" Lodge to-day, and he extended a hearty invita- tion to the delegates to be present.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>"JOY NIGHT" FOR WARRINGTON SCHOLARS.</head>
        <p>"JOY NIGHT" FOR WARRINGTON SCHOLARS.</p>
        <p>The Mayor Entertains Nearly 400 Merry Youngsters :at a</p>
        <p>Fancy Dress Ball.</p>
        <p>--.,.....,..,a4-:•z5t,,,,•-,..-=:...,,x;.-.,..,-, .... ~.,‘ ~:.;,.,.,.,,,, - - ......6-,x,, . -rm,•:. -,..°.3. i , , , , -, • : • 4 ''' s 1 , ..z.' ‘3•• , '' e ,:t ' '' :., *-,, r,... , '1„, 0 ~ ~,,,•-•''' 7,:,:.,,,,,‘ , bitat f .- ......,..". . ... '''''' 'r*:l** 7--v4o‘,•:' • ' 'y' , ' Ps'A ''''''''• ,h.'-* - - - -''''-'-• *•. • • • .. • . N ,•:,,`-.., •,,., ''', ..,Pl-: . -,,,.. •• .. . ~. • '.', „S-, '' ' ''' , ; ~, . --- . .. ~ • ~' . '' ,;--.'. g','Utt, - • - ..,..1...,,- . • 1'...:,•4" . • . - ' t.. - . .t'''''‘• '' r- ----.,,, . -'-',41,‘ ' • .- . ' . ' -‘, ... . • , -,,,,,...„. - ‘• . 0' 'A • 4 . ' '''' ‘` ' ,„„,. " , •- ,' 1' • ''. ‘1 k ;' .. :-,tis'.,'","' :, ~ • - ' ' ' ' ‘,..4., ''.',..,,.,i',. ..,. 41,,,,,•,4,-- ',, '... .. , 1 ... S., ' ....,,,:, ..., ...., . ..., . ,‘.- ( 't ', • -,.. ', . , i ~. , :!, 4 t, '-11IL4 .i.., , ‘,,,, , - ' ‘.'. ...;''l,' ''''! -"4., - - ,- •• . - , -,. .., - •„ ~ -,-;,,,1-'` niipi.4, .. - 1 4.-,ll' in4ttAtii. ~,,,,, i 1,,,,,,.- - ' , , ,--.--, - ~, , ~,,, A i:,,..• .. ~, 1.,..- :. ~,, . /4., :,,,,,„„,,,.‘,„::..,..-,.,,,,,,. , :ik. ..,.% . a . , ~. .fx,isoti,,l , , ,• . ti ~ • ~..- / - it' ~-.;,‘ • ~ ... .:,..'‘;•.4 ' :, :. '''.., g - .. . , Ir. ...,...-,;::./.„‘...,:.,,,, .. '-:;,,,,,., . -..:.: ioi, ,:, ...,-, . ....- • , 3 ix, agir 0-I 4. „.„4„;,0„.._ .....; , .. ,:‘, .iii,.l. -.- .-- mio , , • ' ' ' ' .••. , , , . . --</p>
