      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>Photograph by Burrell.. HIS WORSHIP AND THE MAYORESS WITH GROUP OF YOUNG GUESTS.</head>
        <p>Photograph by Burrell.. HIS WORSHIP AND THE MAYORESS WITH GROUP OF YOUNG GUESTS.</p>
        <p>toilfle a jovial company within its "Joy Night" was celebrated by Warrington scholars on Thursday. It was a merry, spec- youngsters who thronged the guests of the Mayor and doubtful if the Hall ever h walla The guests, whose ages ranged from five years upwards, were ieceived ,by the Mayor and :Pafaaacdrui°barireelfsams ' crowd.le a asand d such t is mayoress, and the latter, early on in the recep- onwas presented with a handsome bouquet recep- tion, Hilda Peacock, niece of the Mayor. After the reception dancing and games took place, and the Mayor congratulated the chil- dren, parents and teachers on the scene. Then did the spectacle unfold itself. The fancy . dresses, thanks to the tasteful arrangement made by the floor stewards, under the charge of e most active M.C. in Mr. S. J. Price, blended most happily. From Polka to Two_step, fr,ln Darn Dance to Musical Arms, the programme sped merrily on, each event working up 'n the grand climax found 5n the "gine A LIN/QUE SPECTACLE.</p>
