      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>PREVENTION OF CRUELTY.</head>
        <p>PREVENTION OF CRUELTY.</p>
        <p>Annual Ball of the Local Branch of the Society.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>FUNDS FOR GOOD CAUSE.</head>
        <p>FUNDS FOR GOOD CAUSE.</p>
        <p>It. is to be regretted that bereavement has recently affected so many families in thiS neigh- bourhood as to interfere materially with the success of meetings or the giving of perform- ances-of various kinds. The annual ball of the Warrington branch of the National Societi.for the Prevention of Cruelty to Children, which was held in Parr Hall on Tuesday night, is...be- lieved to have suffered from this unfortunate cause, for the attendance was not at all eSm- mensurate with the capitol. entertainment that had been provided by the energetic Ball Com- mittee and officials. In the first place the cele- brated string hand of the 2nd Battalion South Lancashire Regiment, now on a visit to War- rington, which has been most pleasing to the inhabitants of the town, especially its music- loving section, supplied the music for the danc- ing, under the conductorship of Mr.. Cahill, and by permission of Colonel Prendergast and officers of the battalion. It is not often that such famous and delightful accompanists are 'available locally, and this .fact alone ought, in tiormal circumstances, to have commanded a large attendance. The music throughout was charmingly ren- dered, and it will be seen from the following ,rogrannne that the taleffted artistes had a fine range over which to display their talent.:-L-- Extra val.se„ Luxembourg; valse, Sheila; valse, " Oh ! Oh! Delphine"; one-step, "On the Mis- sissippi'; Tab* Cri de Cceur; valse, " lin Pen .cP.A.mour "; tango, El Chocici; valse, "Nights of , illadness"; one-step, Robert E. Lee; valse "The Girl in the Taxi"; valse, Brune; one-step. 13iiiitilatid.; vase, Sumurun; vales, Reviens; "The Marriage Market"; one-step, "The 'Wedding Glide"; valse, "Little Grey Home in the West"; valse, Dreaming; tango;, Venus: raise, "Tales of Hoffman"; valse, The Preacher; one-step, "The Gaby Glide"; valse, Bebe; vales, "The Girl on the Film"; pot- pourri.</p>
        <p>I .gxl</p>
        <p>I' The comfort of the dancers was provided for in every possible way. There was a special parquet floor; and sitting out accommodation was arranged in the corridors, while a special drawing-room was fitted up under the centre gallery.</p>
        <p>Much taste had been displayed in the decora- tion of the hall, work that was in the capable hands of Mr. Ralph Johnson. Art muslin of various colours, flags and flowers were judi- ciously arranged, and blended finely and appro- priately with the surroundings and the occa-</p>
        <p>br</p>
        <p>MRS. F. AYLMER FROST, TiON. SEC. AND TREAS. OF THE BALL CONNITTFX.</p>
        <p>sion. Among those who sent the flowers and plants were Lady Greenall, Mrs. Armitage, Mrs. Frost, Mrs. W. Peter Rylands, Mrs. Henry Greensll and Mrs. Vere B. Davies.</p>
        <p>A first-lass supper was served by the Win- marleigh Cafe Co., Sankey-street.</p>
        <p>. Mrs. Frost was 'hon. sec' retary and treasurer to the Ball Committee, which was composed as follows:</p>
        <p>Mrs. Armitage (Walton Lea), Mrs. Henry Greenall, Mrs. W: Peter Rylands, Mrs. Vere B. Davies, Mrs. Broadbent, Mrs. John Coxon, Mrs. J. J. Crosfield (Prodsham), Mrs. Green, Mrs. Watts (Hartford), Major Green. Captain G. R. Crosfield and Mr. Leonard Davies. Major Green was of great assistance to the committee. The Attendance. Those present included : Captain F. M. Appleton, Mr. and Mrs. Ains- - wortn.</p>
        <p>Miss Bisdee, Mrs. Broadbent, Mr. T. Broad- tient, Miss S. .Broadbent, Miss M. Broadbent, Captain Beeman, Miss Blood, Miss Blair. sirs. J. J. Crosfield, Captain G. R. Crosfield, Mrs. Frank Coghill.. Mr. and Mrs. Vere B. Davies, Mr. Leonard G. Davies, Mr. Claude B. Davies, Mr. Vincent V. Davies, Mr. Briant Dixon. Captain and Mrs. Evans. ' Mr. and Mrs. Tracey Forster, Dr. Forster, Mr. and Mrs. Farrant, Mr. and Mrs. F. A. Frost, Miss Anita Frost, Mr. Eric Fairclough. Major and Mrs. Green, Mr. and Mrs. Cyril P. Gough, Miss Helen Gregg, Miss Mary Goma.% Miss Hatt-Cook, Miss Button, Miss Margaret Hutton, Arr. James Harvey, Mr. Legh Holden, Mr. and Mrs. Graham- Hutchings. Mr. Jacob. '‘ Mr. Nigel Kennedy.</p>
        <p>Miss Lister-Kaye, Mies Vera Lloyd, Mr. W. F. Ditzen s.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. J. Monks, Miss E. (4. Maltley. Colonel and Mrs. Norton, Mr. L. Norton, Miss Nicholson.</p>
        <p>Mr. Cecil Parker, PRP. IT. V. and Mrs. Pigot, Mr. Arthur Pigot, Miss Pigot, Miss D. Pigot, Miss M. Pigot, 'Miss de Pass. Major and Mrs. Rooms, Mr. L. R. Rowson, Mr. Thomas Ridgway. s Mr. J. Slieltuerdine, Miss Swallow. Mrs. A. It. Trevithick. Mr. H. M. Warner, Mr. A. Wood, Mr. D. Withers.</p>
        <p>s THE SOCIETY AND ITS WORK. The Warrington branoh of the National Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Chil- dren, with the assistance rendered by the Children's League of Pity, of which Miss Broad- bent, is the hon. secretary, does a very neces- sary and a very useful work in our midst. Dur- ing the last official year 249 cases, involving 706 children, were inquired into, nearly 1,300 visits of supervision being made by the society's inspector, Mr. G. H. Wainwright. That only nineteen prosecutions were found to be needed is proof that it is only necessary to show offen- ders of this particular class the right way, with perhaps a hint of what may happen if they do not keep to that way, to protect the children from neglect and violence. T),is is the general experience of the society nationally as well as</p>
        <p>The officials are: President, Sir Gilbert Greenall; hon. treasurer. Mrs. P. A. Prost: hon. secretary, Mrs. W. W. Dimmelow;- eases hon. secretary. Mr. W. D. Jeans.</p>
