      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>DOAN'S BAlicnaecy"PlLLS.</head>
        <p>DOAN'S BAlicnaecy"PlLLS.</p>
        <p>WORKEES AND THE CHURCH.</p>
        <p>Class Distinction Objectionable to the Working Class.</p>
        <p>ULT SCHOOL SPEAKER' VIEWS.</p>
        <p>The absence of the working class from church is a serious problem associated with religious Life. It was the topic discussed at the meeting of the Academy-street Men's Class of the Adult School Movement on Sunday, when there was a talk on the question—" Why is the working man outside the Church?" The main points laid down by the speaker as to the cause of this were—too much class distinc- tion, and apparent isolation from the more well-to-do membets of the congregation. The Speaker pleaded for a'broader sympathy, a more earnest feeling of brotherhood, and a complete annihilation of class feeling and separation. The man of to-day does not follow so much the creed and belief of church-goers; what he asks for most is the sort of folks they are, whether their religion is the genuine article. It is not what a man sayi he Belftkves -abobt ;the Christ that counts so much, but the belief worked out in the daily rcnitine that counts with the ordinary man. The discussion wi:t most instructive and help- ful.</p>
        <p>SANKEY BRIDGES MEN'S. Mr. Peg. Essenhigh on Sunday morning gave an interesting lecturette and demonstration on "Etchings." By means of drawings and diagrams on the blackboard and a copperplate the lecturer gave a very lucid account of the production of an etching. The Bible lesson was the first of a series on "The School of Christ," the subject being "The Master." It was pointed out that it was not intended to discuss Christ according to any form of dogma, or in a theological sense, but simply in his human attributes as the Master or teacher of a school. With the use of the blackboard the .question was considered from the following headings: What are the qualities necessary for a successful teacher?-l) personality, (2) respect for the personality of others, (3) knowledge of subject. (4) power of communication, and (3) unfailing patience. - CURRENT EVENTS CLASS. A new departure was made at Stuikey Bridges on Sunday afternoon by the first meeting of the " Current Events" Class. This is to meet fort- nightly on the second and last Sundays of the month. The idea is to enable men and women to meet and disdtss some current event, i.e.. anything of local, national or world-wide char- acter which may be occupying the minds of the people, and of such importance, that it must be thoroughly understood in order to form a sound and unbiassed judgment upon it. # ' The subject down for consideration on Sunday was "The Kukuyu Conference, and After," in. ttOdo !ed by the President of the School. A clear and lucid summary was given of the events extending over the last ten years, which led up to the conference, and its resultant federation of mission. churches in British East Africa. The system of Church polity and doctrine of each denomination involved was explained. A brief account of the basis of federation was given, alai of the action taken by the Bishop of Zanzibar. The introducer of the topic merely gave an account of the events and did not pass any judgment upon the action of the parties con- cerned. Afterwards an animated and edifying discussion took place. The gratifying attend- ance and the individual interest taken in the subject amply proved the need for a class of this character, and should do mach to foster a more intelligent interest in public affairs.</p>
        <p>I attention in comparison to the mind, arguing I that the mind which was thoroughly 'alive, active and well 'nourished would in most cases see to the needs of the body being met.</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH MEN'S.</p>
        <p>- - . . .... Mr.-Levi Richardson gave an interestinglec- turette on "Music," on Sunday morning. He dealt with it chiefly from an historical point of view, from the earliest dates that are recorded. References were made of the works of Haydn, Handel, Beethoven, Mozart, Gounod, Ind. Wesley. Of the educational side he gave a</p>
        <p>pulses religiously which came to the minds of the eminent musicians when composing their great works. Reference was also made to Martin Luther and his works.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>BEWSEY MEN'S.</head>
        <p>BEWSEY MEN'S.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. H. Hampson gave a lectnrette on "Strength." He pointed out that physicat strength was the first necessity of life. Physique lay at the basis of life. Without a body in health the mind was hindered in its growth. Members were urged to look well after their bodies. The speaker then spoke of intellectual and moral strength, showing the power and beauty of the latter. This practical talk gave rise to an interesting discussion. Some mem- bers thought that the body received too much</p>
