      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>TWINS CORED OF ECZEMA.</head>
        <p>TWINS CORED OF ECZEMA.</p>
        <p>A Grateful Mother Praises the Wonderful '</p>
        <p>. Zant-Buk Treatment. CUTS, SOBES &amp; PILES.</p>
        <p>The twin babies of Mrs. Mary Ifazelden got in a terrible state of eczema and skin rash, but Zam-I3uk cleared away all their sores and gave them beautiful new skins. To an " Ucklield Weekly" reporter the -grateful -mother, wha lives at Field Cottages, Framfield, Sussex, said: "James and Joseph were only a fortnight old when inflamed spots with mattery heads Came on their faces. The outbreak spread very' quickly to their necks, feet, hands, arms and bodies, some of the sorgs being as big as half- crowns. THEIR SKIN GOT DRY AND CRUSTY AND SCALED AWAY. The spots, too, burst and dis- charged matter. " The irritation must have been very severe, for both babies were constantly crying and got very fretful. A doctor said they had eczema. As they didn't get better under his treatment, 1 tried another doctor.</p>
        <p>"But the rash and sores got worse. We really didn't know what to do, and I feared that neither of the twins would pull through. "1 decided, however, to see what Zam-Buk could do. I had used this herbal balm with great success for piles, cuts. bruises, &amp;c. "I first bathed the twins all over with Zam-Buk MEDICINAL Soap, thoroughly dried their skin, and then dressed the sores with Zain-Bnk: This ZAM-BLIC TREATMENT ACTED LIKE CHARM! The irritation soon died away, and the little fellows took to their bottle with a relish and also slept long and well. _ _ _</p>
        <p>"I kept • up the Zam-Buk treatment and the twins' sores and rashes gradually died away. Beautiful new skin then grew, and to-day there isn't a mark of any kind on either child." Zam-Buk is an unrivalled cure for poisoned sores, eczema, ringworm, scalp sores, ulcers, Piles, bad legs, chapped hands, rheumatism, &amp;e. Sold only in sealed boxes at 1/1} and 2/9. Of all chemists and drug stores. All imitations are worthless!</p>
        <p>Zam-Buk MEDICINAL Soap, sold in large shilling tablets, is very valuable in connection with the treatment of children's skins.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>LIGHTMAN MAKES THE PLAYS.</head>
        <p>LIGHTMAN MAKES THE PLAYS.</p>
        <p>What Electricity Has Dom, for</p>
        <p>Drama.</p>
        <p>ment of lighting, and evidently t e th The modern drama owes mnchhteogtaliseednegvienleoe; p i and the electrician deserves a niche n temple of fame wherein are recorded the evolution of the modern achievements of the rington Literary Society on Monday in Cairo- " The stage.draLmecatujitnog tOhlie vir a r- street School, 3tlr. It S. Townroe said it was ' really the invention of modern forms of light- ing which had produced the modern drama which, in the form of its 'staging, might, be likened to the picture frame. The theatre nau thus become standardised, and that world-wide uniformity had brought about an identity of dramatic form, lie thought the modern music hall had far greater artistic vitality than the theatre. Compare the faces of the audience—in a music hall they would see the people chuck- ling, bubbling and thrilling in closest sympathy with the stage, while the theatre audience was too often bored, listless and lethargic. Some of the best plays of the last few years have been written for the music halls, and the best actors of the world now appear in the music halls in their best parts. In the music hall we found strenuously expressed our native love of pure drama. Side by side, therefore, we would have the serious drama, -inspired with the joy of variety, and the music hall present; ing, more and more, the living,' vital drama which appealed to the people. He thought the Health Committee of Warrington would have reaped some benefit had they seen Bernard Shaw's play "Widowers Houses," which dealt with the housing question.</p>
      </div>
