      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>EXAMINER</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER</p>
        <p>IDLSTRICT ,iJWS</p>
        <p>STOeICTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>MR. H. SMITH'S "STUPID STAGE." Councillor Rollerts Severely Trounces Member for Warrington. - "THE MELODRAMATIC', REBELLION." Stirring Speeches at Liberal Meeting. Mr. Harold Smith, M.P. for Way-rington, and who usually resides at Walton Hall when on a visit to this part, was severely criticised by Councillor H. Roberts at a Liberal meeting held in the Hall Cafe, Stockton Heath, on Tuesday evening. Councillor Roberts was the chairman, and the chief speakers were Sir William P. Byles, the M.P. for one of the Sal- ford Divisions, and Mr. P. M. Oliver, the pros- pective Liberal candidate for the Knutsford Division, of which Stockton Heath is an im- portant part.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>TOWNSHIP TITTLE-TATTLE!</head>
        <p>TOWNSHIP TITTLE-TATTLE!</p>
        <p>"John Bull" has been hoaxed. Who is the "Heathen" to do it? The London journal discovers that There was a "fire" at the Heath not Later than a fortnight ago. We have to go to London for cur news. There was no fire a fortnight ago. Then we are told that the tire bell Could not be rung until a elothee-prop Was secured from a wash-horse to ring it. , This is a second part of a .zroteique yarn. ' Then the Brigade were said to have reached The "fire" in Warburton-street a tortnight ago and could Only smash the windows of the houses. But why quote more of this silly varn? • It is absolutely too grotesque for words. "John Bull" has been beautifully hoaxed. The Garden Society is progressing. There is a balance of £7O in band. This is considered a bit of a recorl. The Swing Bridge threatened a new Oanger. The frost nearly froze its mechanism. Stoves have been kept burning to keep Jack Frost away. Had it become frozen and held up the (anal traffic Many "Heathens" would have no ea :Ise for complaint.</p>
        <p>Daylight Poaching.—At the Petty Sessions on Tuesday, Edward O'Brien and John Aynes, of no settled abode, were charged with trespassing for game behind the Walton Arms. The offence was committed in daylight.—A film of 405., including costs, was imposed. '</p>
        <p>The Garden Society.—The accounts of the Stockton Heath Garden Society have now been made up, and these show that there is a balance in hand of over .270. Three years ago the society was in debt, but in the interval it has thrived, immensely.</p>
        <p>Justices at Petty ..Sessions.—The .magistrates at the Stockton Heath Petty Sessions on Tues- day were: Messrs. A. H. Talbot (in the chair), IL Roberts, T. Baxter., C. Jackson Holnac);, J. ilepherd, F. W. Monks, W. Fletcher, IL G. Roberts, L. Greening, G. Dakin and J. A. Ran. some.</p>
        <p>For Exportation.-1-At the Stockton Heath Petty. Sessions on Tuesday, Herbert Sidney Simpson, teamsman, Stretton, and Alfred Faulk- ner, Stretton, were respectively charged with working and causing a horse to be worked in an unfit condition.—The evidence showed that on the 30th of last month Simpson was in charge of the horse and lurry laden with bales of straw. The animal was very lame.—lnspector H. L. Dee, of the N.S.P.C.A., stopped the driver and asked how it was he was working a horse like that, and the reply given was, "Well, I ' have to obey orders."—Faulkner stated he was not aware there was anything the matter with the horse. The Bench were informed thaf the horse had been sent away for exportation.— Faulkner was fined ,R 1 and Simpson 10s.</p>
        <p>Irishman and the Watch.—At the Petty Ses- 'sionsion Tuesday, Anthony Donoghue, an Irish farm labourer, at Bradley Hall Farm, Apple- ton, was charged with; on December 19, Stealing a silver watch, valued at £2, the property of John Foy, also a farm labourer. A further charge was preferred against accused of stealing a pair of boots, valued at 7s. 6d„ the property of another- fellow labourer, • James Hevery From the evidence it appeared that early on the morning of December 19 prisoner was left alone in. the shanty and on Foy returning some time later both the prisoner and the watch which</p>
        <p>tile wall.)</p>
        <p>to-rw4 day-s prior to this llevery left his boots in the shanty, and on the Saturday they had gone and were later recov- ered from a Warrington pawnshop, whilst the watch .was found at a pawnbroker's shop in Altrincham.—Prisoner was'sent to gaol for one month.</p>
        <p>Public-house Incident. —At the Petty Sessions at Stockton Heath on Tuesday three men ap- peared as the result of an incident at the Wheat- sheaf Hotel, Antrobus. They were Fred Snelson and George Birtles,, of Barnton, and John Daf- fern, of Winnington, and the first two were charged with refusing to quit licensed premises when requested, and the latter with procuring drink for a drunken person. Mr. W. H. J. Jenkins appeared to prosecute on behalf of Mrs. Rachael Bradbury, the licensee.—The evidence showed that •at 8.30 on Christmas evening there was some trouble in one of the rooms through the conduct of the men. Birtles said. he would fight the 'best man in the house. He then called for a drink, but owing to his con- dition he was refused, and subsequently Daffern asked for a small rum, and, upon being served, Ile was seen to hand it to the drunken man.—For his defence Daffern stated that he handed the rum to Riffles to save trouble, for Birtles was very drunk and was threatening to do damage if he was not given drink. His act tended to the peace of the house.—Snelson and Billies were each fined 103. and costs, and Daffern ..£1 and costs.— The Chairman remarked that licensees must be protected.—The advo- cate's fee was allowed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>MEDICINE DAYS LATE.</head>
        <p>MEDICINE DAYS LATE.</p>
        <p>The Proposal for a Depot Where Patients Could Call for Drugs.</p>
        <p>_GUARD' tNS VETO THE SCHEME.</p>
        <p>- The Runcorn Guardians on Tuesday again decided against the wish of several "Heathens"' in regard to the desirability of proper arrange- ments being made for diStributing medicine in the village, so that patients could get it with-: out having to travel to Stratton. The Clerk read the letter from the Parish Council, asking if it would be possible to arrange for a place to which the doctor could send the medicine. 1 The Clerk said he forwarded the letter to Dr. Bowers, who at the present time always sent his medicine down by a cart, and all the Poor Law patients understood that they could get it from one man. The medicine was brought to their doors at the present time.</p>
        <p>Mr. Hallows: They don't always get it to time, it being sometimes three days late:Some definite arrangement is needed. They are wil- ling to pay twopence a bottle. Mr. Morris: I more we support that resolu- tion. The Clerk: Don't let yourselves into expense before you have considered this. Don't you think it is a matter to consider more closely without passing a resolution off-hand? Mr. Morris: Is the statement correct that the people have to pay twopence? The Clerk: That is the first I have heard of it. Mr. Morris: If that is so, we ought to take the matter up,</p>
        <p>The Clerk We know nothing of it yet.</p>
        <p>Afraid of the _Expense.</p>
        <p>• Jr. Hewitt: If we do this it is opening the door for future expense. .</p>
        <p>The general objection was that if anything were done at Stockton Ireath the other districts would have to be treated. the same. Mr. Gibbs: The idea of the twopence is rotten. _ _</p>
        <p>The Chairman: They pay of their own free- will.</p>
        <p>Mr. Gibbs: I propose the Parliamentai7 Com- mittee consider the matter.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: This is hardly a matter for the Parliamentary Committee. _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Hallows: They- don't grumble about the twopence, but they want the medicine at the proper time.</p>
        <p>_ Alderman Norman: We have nothing to do with this twopence. Mr. Hayward: I move we adhere to the sys- tem adopted by Dr. Bowers. • This was seconded and carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>"GIBES,FLODT AND JEERS."</head>
        <p>"GIBES,FLODT AND JEERS."</p>
        <p>Councillor Roberts, referring th the unhappY position of the Tory party, said: We find Lord Derby. with a courage and determination worthy of a much better cause, doing his level best to persuade the Free Trade Unionists on the one hand and the Tariff Reform Unionists on the other to keep their mouths shut upon the important question of Free Trade and Tariff Reform. I venture to predict' Lord 'Derby's valiant efforts will not meet, with the, success 11 hopes, and in all probability there will be a considerable burst up in the Unionist party on that question. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>Corning nearer home, perhaps, you will par- don me if I make a slight reference to tTie speech delivered by the Member for Warring:. ton. I always read Mr. Harold Sinith' speeches. I daresay You will wonder why— (laughter)—l do so for a variety of reasons. I always invariably find some of that rare and refreshing fruit in some phase or other of his speechee 'Which amPly rewards me for the time, energy Mid—shall I say--self-s f acrifice—(laugh- ter)—which the reading °- his speeches involves. s When you have taken out the gibes, flouts and jeers--in regard to the expression of which, at any rate, Mr. Smith is not second to his more illustrious brother, however much he may have - to play second fiddle in other resPects--when you have made full allowance for the remark- able display of want of political information, and for the exaggerated language in which many of his ideas are conveyed, you will occa- sionally find a candid admission of fact which is worth all the rest Put together, and which fully repays you for the study upon which you have entered.</p>
        <p>"No New Information." Mr. Smith is reported to have said that he Supposed he was now in what was called the stupid stage. (Laughter.) A somewhat irrever- ent friend of mine, with whom I was discussing this point, said, "Well, that is no new informa- tion to us." (Laughter.) you might very Properly say what is often stated in a political contest when the same class of candidate as before has been returned—" No change." (Laughter.) It is not long since Mr. Smith told us that, in his opinion, the tiouse of Commons was very much like a theatre, and the members of Par- liament—present company always excepted— (laughter)—were very much like play-actors. They had to take particular care, in playing to the gallery, not to be found out. (Laughter.) I venture to suggest that we are beginning to find out Mr. llarold Smith, and so are the People of Warrington. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>" Political Ostracism."</head>
        <p>" Political Ostracism."</p>
        <p>Whj o I am not a Prophet, or the son of a prophet, I say there is a very strong probability —unless,. as I seriously hope, will not be the fact, the progressive vote is split—and anybody who helps to bring that about, in' my opinion, deserves political and public ostracism for the rest of his natural life—apart from that, there is a strong probability that Warrington, having "ttimeoveved-she ilea-mude a mistake, Mr. Harold :ntittwill be swenittlsio)emerame-hveere else aj)pv to find ati a co that appreciative ofhnis- varied merits. (Applause.) There was another statement which I think fully proves that his confession was justified. Mr. Smith stated, in referring to the present vt de ed wholehey Could Empire keep with Government, about whom he had a great deal to say that was .neither complimentary 'nor pleasant. that they were prepared to give away d and sticktfa.prtoo- their salaries. jobshthaeirr*</p>
        <p>b3r- I dirmovereci-ih,</p>
        <p>Not Sound Logic. I must confess .I pride myself upon being a bit of a logician, but away with or without 1 when the Empire is given a pound of tea, how are I these men going to stick to their jobs. or retain their salaries? (Laughter.) The only explana- lion I urn give you of that extraordinary, state- ment is the fact that Mr. Smith said be was: now in what was sailed the stupid stage. (Laughter.) I am the last man to be personally offensive to Mr. Harold Smith, and I am not going to be. but I take him at his own valuation, which I am quite entitled to do, if he considers he is a play-actor, and considers it is his duty to play to the gallery , out, and if, after the few years which have elapsed since his election, be admits he is in a and endeavour not to be found , stupid condition, I can only say a very great man, some years ago, deserted the party, of which Mr. Harold Smith is supposed to be an ornamental member it the stupid party; and with a stupid party and a representative in a stupid state of mind, Is think we might be for- given if we honestly mid ardently pray for a change. (Loud laughter and applause.)</p>
        <p>"MELODRAMATIC REBELLION." Sir William Ryles and Ulster. Sir William Byles paid a high .tribute to Mr. Oliver's capabilities as a candidate, and hoped that Knutsford Division would revert to L i b e r - a I i s m as in the days of Mr. Alfred King. (Ap- plause.) Referring to . Rome Rule, Sir William said the .Irish, by large majorities, had called for national independence since 1800. For years an English Government had tried by sops and stripes to rule Ireland and failed. Home.. Rule was coming, and they were not to be diverted from it because Sir Edward Carson said he would not have it. (Hear, hear.) Carson would have to take his gruel and submit. . . The march of the Irish nation to freedom and prosperity is not going to be stopped by a few wooden guns or the few deadly rifles of the Ulster Volunteer force. We shall pass the measure and by the end of the year set up an Irish Parliament. Ulster must drop their re- sistance and accept the Irish Parliament, for resistance to the law of the land can only be met by armed resistance. The Tories are fomenting this melodramatic rebellion more</p>
        <p>from hatred of the Government than hatred •)f the Pope—from hatred of Lloyd George finance, hatred of the democracy and alarm at the for- ward movement of labour. (Loud applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>Despair of the Tory Party.</head>
        <p>Despair of the Tory Party.</p>
        <p>I really despair of the Tory party. They do not even make a decent Opposition. The man of greatest 'distinction arid influence in that party is Mr. Balfour. He has come at last out of his armchair, like a celebrated asthetic who emerges from the cloister, and lectures on what? On food taxes? On civil war? On the political crisis or on the weakness of the Little Navy men?</p>
        <p>No! He lectures on national theology, and is lost in the mists of German philosophy and speculations about the nature of the Deity. Is that not very like Nero, who fiddled while Rome was burning? (Laughter and applause.) Mr. Bonar Law had many fiscal policies. He had a new one in every town in which he spoke, and now the farmers had thrown him over, and said they would rather vote for a Liberal than a Tariff Reformer. All Mr. Sonar Law said.in- terested him was how to win the next eleciion. (Laughter.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>REMOVE THE PLURAL VOTER.</head>
        <p>REMOVE THE PLURAL VOTER.</p>
        <p>Mr. P. M. Oliver dealt with electoral reforn. and said the abolition of plural voting would not, in itself, do anything like win the election for the Liberal party in the Knutsford Divi- sion. After a careful calculation he found it would remove only some 300 of the majority which they had against them, leaving 1,100 still to face. Mr. Oliver declared himself a believer not only in "one man, one vote," but in "one vote, one value."</p>
        <p>The Liberal land policy was making great headway. Never before had so much evidence been gathered from the democracy as through the Land Inquiry Committee, appointed for the purpose by Mr. Lloyd George. The committee had got information from 3,000 persons. The Tories said there should 'have been a Royal Commission, instead of a committee. • Well, if • a Royal Commis- sion started in '1914 to examine 3,000 witnesses, it would be 1928 before they finished. The committee's inquiry was notbonly extensive but thoroughly democratic. Mr. Lloyd George went dowit to the people' and had taken the Opinion of the people, and it was the first occa- sion upon which that had been done. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>WESLEYAN PASTOR'S "AT HOME;" A New Departure at Local Church.</head>
        <p>WESLEYAN PASTOR'S "AT HOME;" A New Departure at Local Church.</p>
        <p>An interesting departure in the social life of the Stockton Heath Wesleyan Church took place. in the Schoolroom on Tuesday •evening, and so successful did the function prove that there is a prospect of it becoming an annual event. It took the form of an "At Home," given by the Rev. W. A. Beckett, the Superintendent of the Warrington Wesleyan Circuit, and Mrs. Beckett. The Pastor and his wife welcomed the guests, who numbered ever 100, and every- one was agreed that the ceremony was one of the most pleasant ever experienced in connec- tion with the church life. • Spa-cites were delivered during the evening by the Rev. W. A. Beckett, Messrs. Asher, ffeakin, S. Moss, and Hall, and all spoke hopefully of the welfare of the church. An excellent musical programme was arranged. Songs were admirably renderpd by Miss E. Horton, and Messrs. J. Isherwood ti,nd A. Foster. A very successful duet was sung by the Misses Webb, while Messrs. Isherwood and Foster also contributed a pleasing duet. A feature of the entertainment was the excellently 'executed banjo selections by Mr. Caleb Whitley. The ladies of the congregation were responsible for the dainty refre6hments which were served. St. Thomas' Band of Hope.—The Parish Room was crowded on Thursday evening at the Band Of Hope meeting on the occasion of the chil- dren's night. The whole of the programme was given by the children. The first part consisted of object lessens; various things being brought by the children, who recited a verse of Scripture in explanation, such as Jacob's ladder, a stone, bread, a box of ointment, and a lamp, &amp;c. The second part comprised recitations and singing, intersOrsed with gramophone • selectlians by George Maxfield, one of the boys. Altogether a very pleasant evening was spent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>GRAPPENHALL.</head>
        <p>GRAPPENHALL.</p>
        <p>Street-making Progress.— the Runcorn Rural District Council int_ang on Tuesday, Mr: Wright (surveyor) reported that the street- making in Grappeuhall was proceeding satis- factorily.</p>
        <p>Local Government, Board Jnquiry Fixed.—lt was announced at the Rural District Council meeting on Tu44ay,thg Mr. Shelford Bidwell, Local Government Board Invector, would attend at the' Large Room, Grappenhall; at 10.4,5 a.m. on January 21st, to conduct'aii incittiry in resPeet to powers for the purchase of land in connection with the Grappenhall Sewerage Scheme—lt was also decided that the Clerk obtain the assistance of counsel if necessary to act at the inquiry:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>COUNTY BOUNDARIES.</head>
        <p>COUNTY BOUNDARIES.</p>
        <p>Local Government Board Inquiry To Be</p>
        <p>Held. •</p>
        <p>At the Runcorn Guardians and Rural District Council meetings on Tueschly the Local Govern- ment Board wrote with reference to the repre- sentation of the Lancashire County. Council in favour of the alteration of the boundary between the counties of Lancashire and Cheshire: The Board had decided to hold a local inquiry at a date to be fixed. In making arrangements which may be considered desirable for the representation of their interests at the inquiry the Guardians must understand that if it sho-uld be impracticable to conclude 'the inquiry in 0110 day the hours of sitting will be regulated.by the Inspector. The Clerk explained that a committee had been appointed in the matter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>PA DGATE.</head>
        <p>PA DGATE.</p>
        <p>Wesleyan Scholars' Treat.—The annual treat to the scholars attending the Wesleyan Sunday School took place on Friday evening. A large number sat down to tea, prepared by the teachers, and this was followed by an entertain- ment.' Dialogues, recitations, action songs, &amp;c„ by the scholars were vigorously applauded, a pillow fight causing much excitement and mirth among the youngsters. A large number of book prizes were presented by Mrs. Plinston, who, in 'a short speech, recalled her long and pleasant connection' with Sunday school work. Missionary Meeting.—ln connection with the Junior Wesley Guild a missionary- meeting was held on Monday evening. Mrs. James Bennett presided and expressed per great sympathy with foreign missionary work. A sketch, illustrat- ing the work and methods of a lady missionary in China, was given by the Misses A. Tomlin, A. Sutcliffe, M. Dawson, E. Pryer and A. Mere- dith. These ladies were dressed in Chinese cos- tume. An address, having special reference to missionary work in China, was given by Mrs. Dakin. Miss A. Tomlin contributed a suitable recitation in a very effective manner, and the Sunday school choir, under the able leadership of Mr. W. Bate, surprised the audience by the fine quality of their singing in the three items they rendered.</p>
        <p>Concert at Cottage Homes.—The youngsters at the Padgate Cottage Homes were delighted on Wednesday night, when they were enter- tained to a concert arranged by Mr. W. C. Brooks, the chairman of the Cottage -homes Committee. There were also a number of friends present, amongst whom were the follow- ing: Mrs. Hall, Mrs. Welsby, Miss Groves, Miss Fisher, Mrs. Kilduss, Mr. and Mrs. Constable, Mr. and Mrs. L. Jones, and Messrs. H. I—Part- ridge, Tole, Hilditch, Ridings and Ward. The programme commenced with a pianoforte duet ' by the Misses Kilduss, who also rendered spngs during the evening. The dancing and singing of Miss Doris Welsby were much appreciated, whilst the humorous items given by Mr. John Newns and Mr. R. Scarisbrick were the cause of great amusement. Mr. William Kilduss de- lighted with his songs and recitations. Miss Kilduss acted as accompanist. At the close Mr. C. E. Lawrence, the superintendent, ex- pressed the thanks of the children to the ledies and gentlemen who had so kindly given their services, and also to Mr. Brooks for his arrange- ments. This the children endorsed by a round I of heady cheering: .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>LINES FROM LYAM.</head>
        <p>LINES FROM LYAM.</p>
        <p>" The May Queen' dance don't forget. • It simply promises to be great." So says a "poetical" Lymmite. The Free Church Council have been very, active. Some sensible speeches were delivered At the annual meeting, and the plea , For unity amongst Christian bodies Was as sensible as it was topical.</p>
        <p>"May Queen" Dance.—The May Queen Fes- tival Committee are holding a whist drive and dance at the Cpnservative Club Room, Lymm, on Monday. This might to prove one of the most interesting social functions of the season.</p>
        <p>Temperance Women.—As one of the persons who took part in the formation of the Lymm branch of the National British Women's Tem- perance Association, Miss Cherry, of New Brighton, is a welcome visitor to the local habi- tation, and the address she delivered at the meeting hssld in the Eagle Brow Sclioelroom on Tuesday evening enhanced her popularity. Speaking about the White Ribbon movement, Miss Cherry said that one important way to f.lrther the cause of temperance was first to secure the adhesion of children through such aa ,orgahisation,...and as an example of the feasibility of this quoted the case of one girl who .had personally secured six hundred mem- bers. Her comparison of the temperance cause Df to-day with the position it occupied fifty years ago was illuminating and instructive, and showed the vast strides that temperance has made in the ijiterim. Mrs. G. L. Welford pre- sided over the meeting.</p>
        <p>The Late Mr. F. Holt.—The interment of the late Mr. F. Bolt, whose decease was announced in our last issue, took place at the Parish Churchyard on Saturday afternoon last. For over fifty years Mr. bit had been aFtively con- nected with the Bridgewater Canal Company, and the respectilpvith which he was held was shown by the large number of representatives who attended the funeral., The chief mourners were Mr. George Holt (brother), -Mr. K. N. Tweedale (son-in-law), Mr. Thomas Holt (nephew), Mr. C. Tweedsle. and Mr. L. Tweed-</p>
        <p>„ - a • a v• ale. Amongst others present were Messrs. A. W. Callender and F. E. Evans (churchwardens), Dr. Burrowes, C. E. Cowper, Ewart, T. Griffiths, S. Hinde, W. Hinton, J. H. Holt, H. Tones, T. Ridgway, Swallow, J. D. B. Smith, Warburton, J. Whitlow, and T. Wyatt. The Rector, the Rev. G. B. Thurston, was the officiating clergy- man, and a choral service was held in the Parish Church. Floral tributes were sent by the following: "Annie and Kenneth” (daugh- ter and son-in-law), "Walter and Mabel," "Claude and Dorothy," "Leonard and Ethel," ,nrhyllis and sack" (grandchildren),, Mrs. Ilefakinson, Dr. and Mrs. Burrowes; Mrs. Fazack- erly (Holly Mount), Mr. and Mrs. Hugh Holt, Mr. and Mrs. Taylor, Mr. and -Mrs. Wyatt, Mr. and Mrs. Owen, "Hannah' (maid). C. HealeY, Ship Canal staff, cartage staff, Water-street carters, Chester-road staff, Castlefield staff and workmen; flatmen, ' warehousemen, Water- street Office staff', and the tenants, • Bison, Walker, joynson, Weeks and Smith.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>FREE CHURCH COUNCIL SERVICES.</head>
        <p>FREE CHURCH COUNCIL SERVICES.</p>
        <p>. . ..„ . The Need for Amalgamation AniOngst • - Christian Bodies. . SUCCESSFUL ANNUAL MEETING.</p>
        <p>• The series of united services held in connee: tion ivith the Lymm Free Church Couneil were concluded by a public meeting on Friday. The chair was taken by the President of the Coun- cil, Mr. G. L. Welford, who was supported by the Revs. E. E. Lovell and A. Harvey (secre- tary of the West Lancashire Federation of Fret) Churches), and Mr. J. D. B. Smith. The local hon. secretary (Mr. J. D. B. Smith) in- formed the audience that the Rev. H. Dunn ico was unable to be present through illness, but had sent the Rev. A. Harvey as his deputy. He then gave his annual report, which contained an interesting statement of the Council's achievements during the past twelve months. The speaker supplemented his report with a few remarks on the question of unity among the Free Churches, and declared the time had come for Christians to amalgamate; non-union was responsible for,much of the present lack of interest in Christian work, and the Free Church Council must justifTits existence by the promo- tion of closer fellowship.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>STEP TOWARDS THE IDEAL.</head>
        <p>STEP TOWARDS THE IDEAL.</p>
        <p>The Chairman stated his belief that the. Pres- ent condition of unity among the Free Churches was a Step in the direction of the ideal. There was now a world-wide tendency iof union among the Churches, as instanced n the uniting of the Methodist bodies in Canada, Australia and New Zealand, and the present visit of a deputation from America with a view to the welding together of Protestant Churches. Whilst referring to the Kukuyu controversy, Mr. Welford said that in this opinion the two Bishops had taken the right step, and declared that less division amongst the Churches at home would make for the advance of Christian- ity in the mission fields, lie welcomed the spirit that brings the audience together, and expressed his desire that there might yet come a time when they could work together with the Established Church.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said there was a matter not touched upon, namely, that of Oughtrington (mixed) School. He said that the changing of this school into a Council school should be a matter of great interest and appreciation amongst local Nonconformists. The Rev. A. llarvey, speaking of the Free Church achievements, said: "The more I know of the Free Church movement tho more I am convinced of its potentialities." He claimed that it was the movement which gave the start to the kindly feelings of the present time, as • evinced in the World's Conference on Chris- tian Unity, the Edinburgh Conference, lec. " We did not recognise our united strength until the Free Church Councils were formed, but on corning together, the value of the combination and the dignity whieh accompanied it was clearly manifested." Speaking of the relation- ship of the Free Churches and the Established Church, Mr. Harvey said: "We have been re- buffed time and time again, but we shall only be too glad to meet the Church of England if they will respond to our overtures. We have no enmity towards the Established Church, but we stand for the principles of our Free Churches."</p>
      </div>
