      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>LIIICII.XL COUNCIL IZEPRESENTA-</head>
        <p>LIIICII.XL COUNCIL IZEPRESENTA-</p>
        <p>'llv ES.</p>
        <p>'Jibe Old Members Re-elected.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Lymm Liberal Assopiation was held on Tuesday in Hirst's Pavilion for the purpose of nonainatikg representatives to the Central Council of the Altrincham Division Liberal Association. Last year's representa- tives were practically re-elected en bloc. The representation of Ileatley Ward was reduced to five, to the advantage of Booths Hill, which now llas twelve members on 'the Council. The aggregate number under the aegis of the local association sitting on the Central Council still remains at 34. Mr. J. D. B. Smith, president of the Lymm ti -.krid he was supported by `qrker.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>Association. presided, and tire"--serretary,. mr.'</head>
        <p>Association. presided, and tire"--serretary,. mr.'</p>
        <p>The Secretary read a letter from Mr. E. Llayd Jones, of the Warrington Junior Liberal Asso- ciation, inviting the Ly-mm Association to send twenty or more representatives to a conference on the ”Land Proposals of the Government," to be held in the Parr Hall, Warrington, on February 21. A public meeting is to be held in the evening, when Mr. A. H. Crosfield will take the chair. It was decided to accept the invi- tation, and several members expressed their willingness to be amongst the Lymm delegates. The .following is a list of those nominated for- the. Central Council : Lymm Ward (eight members): Messrs. R. H. Gotobed, W. Gould, A. Thomas, F. A. Morris, C. J. Barnes, H. Drinkwater, T. Wyatt and W. Mclntyre.</p>
        <p>Heat ley Ward (five members): Messrs. J. Webb, jun., W. Seymour; R. H. S. Pawlyn, Joseph Oakes and WI: O. Jones. Booths Hill Ward (twelve members): Messrs. C. Moss, E. Hirst, D. Daniels, 5 Mercer, E. Clare, A. Mercer, R. Percival, T. G. Bate, A. Leah, L. Thomas, J. H. Brazendale and S.</p>
        <p>Statham Ward (eight members): Messrs. T. Waterworth, R. Barker, J. Tylzey, J. D. B. Smith.- A. Appleton, W. Moss, W. 11. Moston and Rigg. . _</p>
        <p>Warburton Ward (one member): J. Rad- cliffe.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>MOORE.</head>
        <p>MOORE.</p>
        <p>Sewerage Schema—At the Runcorn Rural Dis- trkt Council meeting on Tuesday the Clerk said Mr. Beckett reported good Progress in con- nection with the Moore Sewerage Scheme.— Councillor Roylance asked whether the rate- payers or the property owners were going to pay for the laying of the pipes to the houses.—The Clerk: So far as the work has been agreed upon rPay, but I cannot say at te property owners otowntehrs w e fuitlule.</p>
        <p>WssleY Guild.—A social gathering was held in the schoolroom on Wednesday, when the 0 consisted of a short entertainment, which refreshments were served, and atr gam followed. The musical items avpfroegirorua em gin were: Pianoforte solo, Miss DI Done; duets, , Miss A. Lawton and Mr. J. Antrobus, and Miss M. Bustance and Miss A. Millington; songs, Mr. M. Cartwright and Mr. J. Banner; recitation, Miss W. Done. During the evening some goods which were left from the circuit bazaar were sold, and the receipts, which were about Will be given to the chapel organ hind.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>GREAT SANKEY.</head>
        <p>GREAT SANKEY.</p>
        <p>Whist Drive and Dance.—The Sankey Choral Class spent at enjoyable time at the Sankey • Schools, when they held a Whist drive and dance. The whist winners were:—Ladies • 1, Mrs. Forest; 2, Mrs- James Ray. Gentlemen: 1, Mr. A. Canliffe: 2, Mr. E. Darlington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>s RISLEY.</head>
        <p>s RISLEY.</p>
        <p>Independent Methodist Church.—A prize dis- tribution took place on Saturday, when a num- ber of friends gathered and enjoyed a social evening, supper being provided by the young people. The many and varied books which were given spoke of the earnestness with which the scholars had attended to the school in the past year. Mrs. Cook occupied the chair, and in a short address stated that she hoped to see the good attendance kept up. She herself de- cided to attend to her obligations more care- fully. Miss Ashton assisted in giving out the prizes.</p>
        <p>Other District ties on Page 10.</p>
        <p>411 HEALTH AND HAPPINESS II BY TAKING DR. SANDS' LIVER SEEDS FOR INDIGESTION, ETC. Each Bottle In Blue Wrapper. 9d. Of all Chemists. 1 /.1 100 Seeds. 150 Seeds.'</p>
        <p>. A</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD FOOTBALLERS IN A FIX. JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD FOOTBALLERS IN A FIX. JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>Unreasoning Prejudice. One of the sites under consideration by the Warrington Board of Guardians for new Union Offices is adjacent to the Fever Hospital, and I gather that it was favoured by quite a number of members. No doubt with the spread of edu- cation and the increase of intelligence the old Prejudice against such associations is disappear- ing; but one can easily recall a time when the bare suggestion of such proximity would have raised a storm of indignation. I remember the establishment of a certain smallpox hospital in Lancashire nearly 40 years ago. It was in an isolated situation on a hilltop amid moorland, but there happened to be a public road within a little distance, and the matron told me, with mingled amusement and disgust, that passers-by would get to the extreme edge farthest from the hospital and hold their noses until they had Put a good space between themselves and the institution; indeed, some actually behaved in that aggravating way before any patients had been removed thither for treatment!</p>
        <p>Groundless Fear. In the course of last week's discussion re- • sPecting Howard-place, Alderman Bennett ven- tured upon a little prophecy as to the opinion likely to be entertained 500 years hence with regard to the present Warrington Town Council. A„lderman Tinnion interposed the reminder that we shall all be dead by then," and the reflec- tion seemed to afford our representatives a good deal of satisfaction; anyhow, according to the report, they laughed heartily at the prospect. After all, this simple fact of mortality is one that it is apparently possible occasionally to 1 thaiiss- sight of. A boy reading the newspaper tc grandmother picked out an item to the effect tat the coalfields would be practically worked °llt in 9,000 years. "Aye, deary me, exc aimed the startled old dame, "whatever shall we have to do when there's no more coal!"</p>
        <p>i 11' Sa Vanity? What an enduring monument that unmen t;• ;°llahle erection at Howard-place is destined te, Prove if "five centuries after the people wil) wonder at the Council for putting such a thing there." Nixon Waterman had not learned the secret of lasting fame when he wrote: "Oh, life, how very brief your span ! How small the part I play ! This wondrous thing they call a man Is but a piece of clay. how very soon I'll be forgot When I, like all, must die. My "11 great-great-grandchild may not E'en know where I shall lie.</p>
        <p>And shall they thus in memory My life so lightly keel)? Will not my own remember me When in the grave I sleep? The thought is sad, and yet, howe'cr I dare not criticise, Since I don't really know just where My great-great-grandpa lies." A moment's thought sufficeth to convince one of the truth of this, and if any local alderman or councillor imagines that 500 years from Oak Present he will be had in remembrance by the Warringtonians of that period—well, to put it mildly, he's very sanguine. ,As for the nameless abomination which at Howard-place is to per: petuate the memory of the perpetrators over half a millenium, assuredly it will long ere then have ceased to be.</p>
        <p>Divine Healing. " Almost as far back as I can remember there has been in active existence a sect regularly !).I.trising what, in his letter to last week's tiesexaminer," the good Vicar of Burtonwood. spirli3es 48 "the long-neglected function of 'al 1 eq.l' - bee 1-4111 g." I was intimate with mem- -BOf it 1,0, )• • West town Where I .0 ilve(t in a was employed several Years. They werea solicithr and his wife, and when I first went to the place they were prominent Bap- tists. The lady, however, went to the United States on a visit, and„ returning .a,couvert what they always, referred to as divine'healing, wen over first her husband and afterwards a • 11;inibei of other townspeople. They held see- "ices at their own house and I attended one of their ; meetings. The preaching struck me as 14'.111i1ae to that of the Plymouth Brethren, vleePt that ailing folk were always invited tc remain for healing. I gathered that the- sect (who have now a good foothold in this country, with a complete organisation and literature of their. own) not. Only put a literal interpretation ePon, but, believed to be still applicable and operative the passage in the Epistle of James, 14, "Is any among you sick? Let him call for the elders of the church and let. them pray , over lain, anointing him with oil in the name of the Lord; and the prayer of faith shall save him that is sick and the Lord shall raise him up." I take it that the Guild of Health in whose , behalf the Rev. A. M. Mitchell pleads in these e9lumns, works on somewhat similar lines,' though to me it is a new phase of Church of England activity.</p>
        <p>The HolY Well. ' There are other ways, more or less popular and effective. Among the patients at the Royal 4e Institution, Manchester, I inside the acquaintance of a young Irishman who had had , one eye injured while working as a mechanic and had the sad experience of what is known as sympathetic affection, so that he was almost blind. Since be was discharged as incurable 1 have corresponded with him, and he tells .me he is now gging to St. Winefride's well for treat- Inent, and .has faith that he will benefit thereby.</p>
        <p>Poetry. Returning for a moment to the case_of the local Vicar who so often finds hinwelf in the limelight, I note that some fellow member of the Board of Guardians has been -dropping into poetry with the Rev. A. M. Mitchell as the subject. For Christmas week thybarber's lather boy rigged 111) the usual J( cigar box with the slit in the or customere mites, and, besides adorn- ing it artistically with coloured paper and rib- bon, he added this original verse: "I like to hear the church bells, That call us to our pews; 1314 better still to stay in bed And have another snooze:" Of course I felt it my duty to take the little rascal to task about this, but, after I had en- tehred ulion my sermonette yad was clearing my r°.a4t,,f" the solemn atipWation, he chipped ~ • 11, `` Oh, that's nothing; I've done lots 0,"'" 311—•-• One of our customers has taken some away with him, and he says he's going to have it frame," u At all events, while the Vicar of Burt °llwneiliWmust agree with what I told the ljnather boy as to the immorality of the senti- ents he so shamelessly avowed and openly will before the eyes of helpless victims, he 11l hardly resent any adverse criticism of the more personal lines reproduced in last week's i rom Everywhere" column.</p>
        <p>"For Their Much Speaking." • Surely the longest speech ever delivered in ' the American House of Representatives was that of Senator La Follette. who, in an attempt to talk out the Aldrich Currency Bill in 1907, . evoke from noon until seven o'clock the follow- ing ulcirning• There is a familiar humorous ,Song with the refrain —Lucky Jim, how I envy him," and I can fancy certain 'members of the Warrington Town Council (whose last week's Meeting was so unduly protracted) unconsciously humming it as they contemplate the folly of Senator La Follette. Let me respectfully corn- mend to such the more excellent wily of which readers of "'Sub Rosa " were recently reminded --that of the gifted parrot that, Laving brought down upon himself violent punishment for some trifling jest uttehed in the lightness of his heart, summed ur, the situation with the remark, "1 know what's the matter with me; I talk too mach 1"</p>
        <p>(Cantintied in next column.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>Warrington Strike Recalls Interesting Incidents of the Past. DRESS SHIRTS FOR JERSEYS.</head>
        <p>Warrington Strike Recalls Interest- ing Incidents of the Past. DRESS SHIRTS FOR JERSEYS.</p>
        <p>" EXAMINER" SPECIAL.</p>
        <p>A strike amongst the players, such as we have seen in Warrington, is unique in the annals of the football game, but while strikes have hitherto been unknown, many strange experi- ences have befallen footballers and football clubs. Some time before the Northern Union was horn out of the split in the English- Rugby Union, a well-known Yorkshire Rugby club set put to play a match with. a rival some miles away. They drove in a 'bus and the route took ihem over some moorland. Suddenly a blizzard ,Jame on, and in a short time heavy wreaths of snow were encountered. In one of the deepest hie 'bus stuck, and despite the willing efforts of ihe three horses and the fifteen players, the vehicle could not be got through. The for- wards, a burly lot, nevet pushed so hard in a icrum as they did at the 'bus, but wheel the 'bus they could not. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>TEAM LOST.</head>
        <p>THE TEAM LOST.</p>
        <p>Spades and shovels were borrowed from neigh- •)ouring farms, and finally the 'bus was dug out, 'nit by then the time when the game should fiaVe ended had. arrived and the snow-bound team were still miles from their destination without the means of letting their opponents \ know where they were.</p>
        <p>' By a strange coincidence the ground where the match should have been decided was play- able, although sloppy, and the home team offi- :dais could not understand the non-arrival of heir rivals. The telegraph was got. to work, tnd it was discovered the travellers had actually 'MAW, but where they Were no one could tell. Gate' in the evening a cold, wet, dejected team returned to their headquarters, and then the tory of their adventure became known.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>IN THE WRONG TRAIN.</head>
        <p>IN THE WRONG TRAIN.</p>
        <p>The Batley team were on one occasion, I be- ieve, prior to N.U. days, travelling to play a match somewhere in Lancashire. it chanecd :lere was another football team also in a saloon &gt;n the train going into North Staffordshire. l'hrough some freak on the part of the railway ,fficials the saloons got mixed up, and the Batley team found themselves rushing on to Stafford- ;hire, and the other club doubtless were en oute to the place in Lancashire, which :was 7 the 'Batley destination. Fortunately a member of the Batley Corn- nittee noticed that they were. travelling in the vrong direction, and the traik was stopped. ['he company, made amends, and Batley were .tble to take the field almost on time. What een ;ive great consideration to travelling iappened to the other team misear ried history ;ays nothing. This surely must' 1) b 'tnique cxperience, as the railway companies footballers.</p>
        <p>AN EXCITING ADVENTURE. S3me 20 years ago a number of Lancashire fol- lowers went to Edinburgh to witness the Inter- mtional Rugby match between Scotland and 'England. They decided to drive to the ground Inverleith in one of the char-a-bancs that died for hire. The route to the ground is down t very steep street, and in descending, the brake ailed to act with the result that the horses and Tehicle ran away down, hill.</p>
        <p>- At a .bend in the road the vehicle ploughed tcross the pavement and crashed into the wall 4 a house. It was a miracle the heavy char-a- Uanc did -not fall into a deep area space along- tide, but it fortunately remained fixed, although •loNering over the brink. Intense alarm was wcasioned until the travellers were released from their perilous position by aid of ladders— fortunately they escaped unhurt.</p>
        <p>- OLD WOMAN BEAT THEM. The Edinburgh University Rugby team on dno occasion, now a good many years ago, were travelling to play Glasgow University. There .vere no saloons put on the trains for footballers n those days, and .half a . dozen or so of the )layers crowded into a smoking compartment. An old woman insisted upon joining the crowd despite the students' protest, so by way of re- ' -,renge they decided to smoke her out. The stu- -lents puffed hard for some time and then one ifter another gave up. " Mister," said the old roman quietly to one, "If .yer done 'We yer Ade ye might lend it me to get a bit whiff, as pipe at home." The students morelef k t e rnsiy il sick ever.</p>
        <p>PRESTON NORTH END STORY. At one time Preston North End were the crack team in the country, and their only serious rivals were the Queen's Park, Glasgow, with whom they played four or more matches each ieason. One occasion when they arrived in Hasgow, the Preston men discovered that the luggage had been lost en route. Several calls were made at the station, but no trace of the, luggage, and at length the team had to depart ! for the Queen's Park ground at Hampton Park ; without any playing togs. The home team offered a supply of knickers, jerseys—the characteristic white of the North Enders—could not be had. The then ;eeretary was, however, a Man of action, and lashing into a shop devoted to men's wear, dartled the shopkeeper with an order for a dozen of the best and largest dress shirts he had in stock. With the shirts in his possession the secre- tary drove full speed to the football ground, and n the minute fixed for the kick off Preston North End team appeared robed in beautifully ! "torched and finished evening dress shirts. The ground was muddy and soon the sheen and starch vanished from the shirts.</p>
        <p>W.D.B.</p>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST —continued.</p>
        <p>Smith. . In the opening paragraph of "The Vassing, Week" in the last issue, the Editor hool occa- sion to allude with. emphasis to the good old English name " Smith." 1 was somehow re- minded of an inchient of the far-off days when Miss Terry and the late Sir Henry Irving were triumphantly touring in company., An old journalist to whom the former was under especial obligations called one day when the tal- ented pair were together, and the lady was much distressed on finding that she had completely forgotten her newspaper friend's name. How- ever, she had a happy inspiration and promptly acted upon it. Said she, ".Sir Henry and I were just disputing as to the spelling of your name, and have made a little wager. Please write it down and decide it for us, will you?" The un- suspecting scribe produced his card, and the in- genious actress, refreshing her memory with a glance at it, cried out with well-feigned delight, 11" Ah, I've won the bet." That is an old favour- ite of mine, but the trick doesn't always suc- ceed so well. I recollect one trying. it .on in pretty much the same way. "Let me see," said the forgetful lady, "how do you spell your name?" and the •horrid fellow replied, "Same old way—S-m-i-t-li 1" Roughing it Abroad. •</p>
        <p>I bad hoped to add a few comments upon the communications of Warrington and Lyram men who have emigrated to Canada and have been sorely disappointed, but I have already ex- ceeded my allotted space, and must forbear. Suffice it that should the one who avows • a desire to get to Australia manage to work his passage be will find labour conditions there not it whit</p>
        <p>lhave lately interviewed two or three who have returned from that part of world in disgust, and who can some sad tales [unfold from their own experieme.</p>
        <p>.1 -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 17, 1914.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 17, 1914.</p>
        <p>QUAINT CORNER IN LIVERPOOL</p>
        <p>Perpetuates the Memory of Conditions Prevailing Over a Century Ago.</p>
        <p>RICHMOND CLOTH FAIR.</p>
        <p>The antiquarian-minded. visitor to Liverpool may spend an interesting half-hour and find himself transported a full century and a quarter into the past by the simple expedient of turning aside under an archway in Richmond Row, and inspecting the, buildings of the ancient Cloth Fair.</p>
        <p>_ Though crowded now among buildings that shelter a thick population, it is. not long since Richmond Row was a country lane. It sloped upwards towards the then picturesque village of Everton. On the one side was a small lake, through which a,brook flowed merrily from the fields above; in an adjoining field were located the Corporation Hounds, and here, in a rural and pretty spot, assembled the Lords of Sefton or Knowsley merchants and .bucks from Liver- pool, farmers from kheOcountry-side mounted on their Shire horses, to take part in the Coursing. The gateway of the old kennel yet stands and bears two hounds and a horn in sculpture upon the .stone.</p>
        <p>The comments made, if anyone proposed that the Corporation should resume its old function of hound keeping, would be a good indica- tion of the distance we have travelled since these stones were cut.</p>
        <p>Out of Richmond Row, passing through a low archway, we gain access to the Richmond Cloth Fair. This large court, 'hidden away from the throng, is well worth a visit, and we feel that we shall be doing a courtesy to our readers in directing their footsteps towards it. Around a iare, open court are wooden build- ings, two storeys in height; around which runs a gallery; supported .by pillars. A winding stairway leads to the upper storey, and a wooden fence protects the pedestrians who use the terrace. Along the walls are large. wooden chests Used anciently for the storage of goods.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>OUTLIVED THEIR PURPOSE.</head>
        <p>OUTLIVED THEIR PURPOSE.</p>
        <p>This is the old Woollen Hall or Cloth Market, built for the sale of Yorkshire woollen goods by Iwo brothers, Thomas and William Dobb, about</p>
        <p>the year 1785. Upon these extensive verandahs buyers and sellers met and did business, while country horses. and vehicles splashed with long travel, rested in the court below.</p>
        <p>With the passage of years trade moved to the centre of the town, and the business of the market fell away. Sktrangely enough, the Old buildings, with their woollen chests, have out- lived their.. purpose for a full century. Char- women, orange girls q.nd scavengers live in the Small tenements into which the Cloth Pair buildings are broken up. Cloth is still a pro- minent feature; but it is cloth that flutters from numerous clothes-lines. The chests make ad- mirable verandah seats, and bespattered urchins play a muddy game of football where Yorkshire , Pack horses rested in the long ago.</p>
        <p>It may be mentioned. that close by Richmond Row was the Ancient Butts, where the Mersey bowmen, attired in the wood-craft dress of Kendal green, with flat caps and feather, and sheaf and arrows, practised with the long bow.</p>
        <p>_ The Butts exist only in name and memory, and beyond the name there is nothing to recall the weapon that stood the English in so g00..1 stead upon, many a hard-fought field. A.M.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>OLD MOORE AND HIS 'LOYAL</head>
        <p>OLD MOORE AND HIS 'LOYAL</p>
        <p>ALMANACK."</p>
        <p>'I he Gloom of Age</p>
        <p>The opening of the year, with its disasters, turns the mind towards the hardy old man who, year by year, cheers or disturbs us by his_pre- dictions. In his reading of the stars for "the year of human redemption, 1914," Old Moore is unusually gloomy. Perhaps, as the "Manchester City News" says, it is the disillusioning effect of age</p>
        <p>that weighs %on him; _ for this is his 217th year of prophedy, and we- can hardly expect from ' such over-ripeness the cheery outlook of beard- less boys. Whatever the cause, he has little good to promise us for this year. Take his weather forecasts, for example—with a grain of salt, if you like. What are we in for? -January is a rich blend of cold weather, snow or rain, stormy on the fourth, gales on the fifth (a nice distinction), snow on the eighth, and so, with only two days "finer" to. " cold and incle- ment," "gales" again, "wind and rain," and our miserable old friend "variable." February is little better, but March, cheerily opening with "cold and snowy. weather and high winds," goes gleefully on to heavy ralits and high winds, and reluctantly consents to be fine and dry towards the end. April is " a generally fine month with much rain," and May opens with rails. and dull Weather, and finishes unsettled and cold, the middle genially stuffed with a few fine and warm days, followed by fogs, ruin and wind, thunder. "Thunder !" seems an appropriate comment. July is "a variable and changeable month," August "find generally, with storms," and Sep- tember, except when it is windy, rainy, variable, stormy, cold or unsettled,. is "a fair month on the whole." October, to cut it short, seems about the only decent month we shall get. November is unspeakable, but we are to have a bit of mild weather in December in preparation for the "heavy gales, snow, and blizzards," which will make Christmas seasonable. As to social affairs, Saturn is going to make trouble all over the. place; Uranus, also, is to put a mischievous finger in the world's pie. Strikes, rioting, evils in naval affairs, mortality and scandals in high life are to come so thick and fast in the first quarter of the year, with unrest, Socialist and Suffragist outrages in the spring quarter, that we have lacked courage to read any further. There is to be an increase of the birth-rato in the spring, otherwise it seems to be a season hardly worth having. Old Moore proves it all by diagrams, and it is. of course, obvious that when "Jupiter and Uranus are near the cusp of .the ninth house," something is bound to happen. Read Old Moore for "all the 'orrible details," and be happy! .</p>
        <p>. A HUMAN ZOO. A species of human " zoo " is about to be started in Chicago by Mrs. L. Bracket Bishop. Mrs. Bishop intends to become the mother by adoption of fifteen children, each representing a characteristic type, the " Express " says. She has requested her friends, who are touring in Europe to find for her babies representing such races as could not be secured in Chicago. Her family will include a negro baby, an Indian, an Arab, a Japanese, a Malay, a German, a Chinese, a Scandinavian, an American, an Irish infant, and others representing the inhabitants of South America.</p>
