      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>WORLD OF LETTERS.</head>
        <p>THE WORLD OF LETTERS.</p>
        <p>New Books.</p>
        <p>The Weakest Link. By Harold Begbie. London': National Council of Evangelical Free Churches, is. net.</p>
        <p>We live in an age when our thoughts are turned, almost against our will, towards reform. Everywhere, and always, we hear the cry that</p>
        <p>, this custom is old-fashioned, and should be abandoned, that that institution has been out-</p>
        <p>lived and must give place to something more suited to our manifold needs. And there is this difference in the present situation from the past : that we are not divided • into two camps of progressives and reactionaries, but into many parties, who all alike claim to be billy progres- sive. It may be justly said that change is the characteristic of the Present time. " Surely," says Mr. Begbie, " there has never been in the history of this country a period when change gathered so great a momentum, when stagnation so completely vanished, when the present was so full of ringing blows, and the future so rich with promise of victory," And in the midst of all this change, this cease- : less striving afier better conditions and a fuller • life, to the keen observer, Mr. Begbie thinks, there is "something lacking, some soul, some . Stopped dead in the triumphant spirit, some touch of love and divinity missing. One is pped march Of political oPtimism by the miserable</p>
        <p>_ . littleness in prosperous humanity, of all that which had seemed to us in theory the crowning glory of the race." And in face of this seeming bi.0.,1M these things Why is man not dinifetr irony of life, Mr. Begbie asks those questions, " iaropoijdiet over nobler, vi which must trouble all thoughtful people who effort'of huma t ionity to these ' after the immense IT its head and behold the stars? Why is life not more beautiful, more dtgnificd? Wily is vulgarity such a power? sa Why has \ugliness got so tight a hold upon us? Why is respectability a failure, and goodness its questions Mr. Begbie finds in the position of women, and hi the esteem in which wonmn are held, in the world. And we, are disposed in a ' measure to agree with 'him. Bat we think .that Mr. Begbie is inclined to Tellife answer so barren and weak?" exaggerate bne aspect of the - dishonouring atti- Wde towards women, which we think is ' one among many of the evil effects of the position of women. Proatitution—quaintly termed our " soeitd evil "—as if there were no other social eviis_ts, indisputably, one -of the greatest blots on our civilisation, and makes one realise very forcibly that, notwithstand- i„ g our vaunted improvements on past civilisations, the actual distance . we have travelled is relatively small. But Many factors individual and social, are involved, iind the political and economic disabilities of women have more to• do with this problem than most peoPle - recognise, or, at least, are prepared to admit. Mt, Begbie of course, is aware that in a comprehensive study of our social conditions the question with which he deals would be treated proportionately to the other. problems arising out of our national life. And, in 'fact, in a prefatory note he apologises to the reader for the "weakness of insisting so earnestly on one side of a question that the other side seems as if it had no existence in the writer's mind." We are not certain whether this inspiring little book is net likely to have more effect by its very narrowness, by concentrating the reader's atten- tion so exclusivelY on the one grave issue, We must confess to some scepticism when Mr.- Beg- bie expresSes the belief that the evil "will be cured directly it is faithfully attacked," not because we hold a cynical view of human ' nature, but because 'social evils of this kind cannot be cured quiekly, and require, to be attacked at many points and in more Ways than one. But that Mr. Begbie's little book will do a real service towards this end we have no doubt whatever. It jos finely -conceived and finely written, and attests- in every page the intense earnestness and sincerity ,of its author. we ' hope that it, will ho.re:iv, -I,4de circulation._ and I contribute towards the .realiiiition of a concep- tion of social life far removed from that which obtains at present.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>Literary Gleanings.</head>
        <p>Literary Gleanings.</p>
        <p>JANE AUSTEN.</p>
        <p>Jane Austen has had. to wait a long time for a place in the "English Men of Letters" series; but now that her chance has Come she is well treated. Mr. F. Warre- Cornish, well-steeped in the Austen literature and tradition, makes a living picture of his 'subject. It was not a very easy subject, for, like so many feminine characters, it contained a combination of irre- concilables, and was . further overlaid by the 1 reticent conventions of its age. But the ' wonian_who emerges from its Pages is alto- gether loveable and wise. Endowed With sagacity and penetration, and with a coOlness of judgment very rare in the impetuous sex, , Jane Austen was a kihdly satirist, who saw through the foibles of her friends, yet never let her criticism dim the bri 1 gotness of her natural affection. As gr. Cornish says: "Free from illusion about humanity, she did not condemn or despise it with a cynical sneer; nor, on the other hand, make use of sentiment to cloak what she saw to be vile." In her girlish days she was something of a flirt, but it seems likely that an early romance, closed by premature death, separated her finally from affairs of the heart. genceforth, she was con- tent to be a looker-on at the comedy of life. and her estimate of the play was genial and critical at once. Dullness was the one sin that she could not forgive, though perhaps humbug ran it close in her catalogue of the scarcely- pardonable!</p>
        <p>jl aa il 11 de. i 1:el ii, s one of the THE UNION JACK TRIUMPHANT. wonders of the world. surrounded You enter a narrow strait, guarded by a towering conical mountain, many of them with the most rantas- a nd The d i shcaa,beor harbour of ogfi air: :on sd de n on every side by 41'11114 and preclpitous tic outlines. I should imagine,. Says the Hon. mountains, Stephen Coleridge, in " Memories," tbat all the fleets of the world might anchor there in safety. i seventes a couple. . In the earlyof English blue-jackets in search of adventure climbed to the top of the mountain that guards the en- trance, hauled after them a flagstaff and a Union Jack, set up the pole on the summit, : and unfurled the flag to the breeze. . The astounded And indignant Brazilians ' ' awoke one morning to find an alien bunting fly- rfrk over their territory fro_ ta its most con- , ' spicuous eminence. Protests Were lodged with • 1 ! the English Minister, who, ...with the utmost ' politeness, apologised for the thoughtless esca- pade of some entirely unknown persons, and gravely told the Brazilian Government that, of course, he would have no Possible Objection to the prompt removal of the flag and pole. The Emperor, however, could- find no subject in all his .1,,id0 empire who would volunteer to make the ascent to the summit of the mountain, which the people of Rio de Janeiro considered unscalable. In this embarrassing dilemma the Brazilian Government determined to knock over the staff by shooting at it. The Brazilian fleet was ordered to shoot the offending pole off the top of .the mountain. Either they could not train their guns to the 'required angle of elevation, or the target was too narrow for a successful</p>
        <p>A STRIKING EXAMPLE of 1- genuine value is afforded by the fact that Steedman's Powders have had a steadi-ly increasing sale for nearly 100 years, and still stand unrivalled as a safe medicine for children. These powders contain no poison. Observe the uin Steedman.</p>
        <p>shot. At any rate, the Byglish flag, flying over the proud Brazilian city, remained, braving the battle and the breeze, till it rotted away.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>BEHIND THE VEIL AT THE RUSSIAN</head>
        <p>BEHIND THE VEIL AT THE RUSSIAN</p>
        <p>COURT.</p>
        <p>Count Vassili here presents to the reader a running commentary upon the life and man- ners of the-RusSian Court, since the death of Nicholas I. in March, 1855. When that stal- wart autocrat laid down his sceptre his dying woriels to his son, Alexander IL, were these : " A Sovereign has no right to dream. He can only work and endure." His successors have had t 9 endure much, but they have not alto- gether abandoned the lofty priiilege of dream- ing.</p>
        <p>. Everything that can be said against the Tsar and Tsarina seems to be dragged into the fore- ground, and, if half that is said is true, it is no wonder that the Winter Palace looks "un- kempt and forlorn," that Court life is "sad and dull," and that the Royal Family is happiest when it can find 'concealment in the security of the country. The class of mind which takes pleasure in being reminded that crowned heads are not exempt from the bitterest troubles of humanity may, perhaps, derive some sort of satisfaction from the record of the sorrows of the Russian Empress and of the hourly terrors that surimund her home. •</p>
        <p>Unfortunately for the picture which Count l'aul Vassill presents, he is biassed to the point of virulence. Re leaves "not Lancelot brave nor Galahad pure." According to him, Nicholas 11, is a nincom- poop with neither the heart nor' the brain to comprehend the wishes of his people or the mis- fortunes of his reign. It is suggested that it was be who sent the Russian fleet on that hope- less demonstration which ended in the battle of the Straits of Tsushima; and that lie alone of his people, was unmoved by the disaster. Of the Empress, Count Vassili finds as little good to say. Haughty, in temper and in manner, she'has had neither the brains nor the wish to conciliate Russian society; like her husband, she is a prey to the impostures of spiritualists: and of her son, the Tsarevitch. Of the Tsare- vitch the author writes</p>
        <p>A I^t of rubbish has been written about tile illness of the Tsarevitch, and the most stupid tales have been circulated. The 141. who has been very delicate from his birth, suffers from an organic disease of the arteries, which re liable to rupture upon the slightest pro- vocationi, and even without cause. • Already three years ago, he had to undergo an opera- Von, which was performed by Professar suloroff, one of the doctors who treated him in the autumn of 1912. The fact was kept secret from the public.</p>
        <p>If this means anything, it means that the Poor boy is suffering from hwmophilid, of which soveral cases have been known in turopean reigning families. It seems unnecessary to the Point of brutality to. add that he has grown into a "peevish, disagreeable Q.l.lild, who . . . beats his sisters, tyrannises over his Krvants, and whenever anyone attempts to correct him instantly threatens the unfortunate pers:m with all kinds of punishments." The only members of the Imperial Family abut whom nothing actually unkind is said are the Vrincesses. , "Of these, the two eldest ones are already grown up and lead sad liver, never being permitted to enjoy themselves as girls of their age generally do."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>LINKS OF LIFF.</head>
        <p>LINKS OF LIFF.</p>
        <p>I want to draw a picture with words, not artist's brush, But cannot get the shading, and my sentenccs seem rough.; Still, if it be true to Nature, what better can it be Until I. am skilled in painting a picture you can see.. Descriptive is my story of a wee-and, 'dainty- maid, With a. face like waxen moulding and hair of fairy shade; Her eyes were blue as the ocean,. forth many a glance they drew, Graced as a queen, When.in leer teens, with happiness given to 'few.</p>
        <p>We .were chatting round the fire on a stormy winter's night, • • When we heard a gentlelta-Pping, •so we soon turned on the light, ,•• • , Revealing in the doorway this. child of beauty rare, Who said, "I've come to:lite with you, then I'll be in your care; . •My mother she has long been dead, her children they were ties, .. . Father he did quickly wed, then his heart and brain capsized. So I took my little brother fat away to grannie' home, • With him she could not bother, but her •age for that atoned."</p>
        <p>Then we drew her close beside us, and supper it was laid, And, as the time for bed had come, a cot for her waS made. I said "I'll be your mother," and have truly kept my vow, As I kissed her cheeka in slumber—oh, that I could do so now ! I knew the angels sent her : they know how my heart yearned To have companion sister to las• boy, iny one sweet pearl. And through the years which followed they grew up side by side,, They measured heights together and compared their age with pride.</p>
        <p>The clock it .was then chiming time for their Sunday School, As they made a hasty scramble to keep the good old rule; Many a boy her promise pressed, that she would be his own, To each she made rejoinder, " I've given a sailor one." , In my fancy now I see her, in a gown' of peacock blue, A face brimful with pleasure, and such tiny feet which flew. The sailor boy was coming, did he not own her heart? They two would soon be married, in life to make their mark.</p>
        <p>And o'er the sea his bonnie bride and be have sailed together, In sunny lands and stormy breeze, Captain and Mate did weather; , Brightest days won't always last, and two now mourn in sorrow— But not as dead; our loved ones live with us in our bright to-morrow, Freed from the perishing play of earth, a spirit state for ever. - • • 'Tis they who give our thoughts new birth; 'there is no death to sever, And oh! the joy of knowing them true comfort, they do bring: •• This earthly life, so brief its span, when the bravest must give in. E. A. BRIDGWATER..</p>
        <p>- MUSHROOM POISONING CURE. A cure for mushroom poisoning discovered by Dr. Louis Olivier, one of the most distinguished chemists of France, has recently been presented before the -Society of comparative pathology. After a demonstration that mushroom poison- ing destroys the red corpuscles of the blood, he stated that his neutralising agent was a serum from a sheep. The preparation was extremely He drew the blood front a sheep and allowed it to coagulate, the liquid resulting being his ,serum. This he injected into the veins of a- human heing. In the country dis- tricts of France there are annually hundreds of serious cases of mushroom poisoning, which the society now hopes may be easily cured.</p>
