      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>World's Tribute of Interesiing Items of News.</head>
        <p>The World's Tribute of Interesiing Items of News.</p>
        <p>QUAINT AND AMUSIKG.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Dan Crawford mentioned some extra- ordinary Central African dishes in the course of a recent leeturC. They included stewed elephant's trunk, roast rhinoceros foot, boiled hippo tongue (stewed forty-eight hours to make it tender), roast wild donkey, stewed monkey, roast water-rat, head, tail, and all, the luscious morsel; which a chiefs.provided.as a State deli- cac,Y, of a mess of thousands of white"- ants, frizzled in their own fat, like a sort of Central African whitebait. Also there was a special 'dish, much favoured, of starchy boileCgrass, "green and glutinous." .„</p>
        <p>Australian Penny Banks. -One. of the most interesting departments of the Savings Bank of South Australia is that of the penny banks. It is in' this section of the bank's operations that the spiritiaf thrift is encouraged in. young South Austtalians; and the saving, of small coins in youth is frequently the start that leads to wealth in later life. At the close of last year there were 281 schools at which penny banks were in operation in South Australia. The number of depositors was 12,251. The total amount deposited stands at 'X10,146. The work in connection with the palmy 'savings banks at the various schools is performed gratuitously by the teachers.</p>
        <p>Cancer and Coal. Increasing evidence indicates that, chemical irriMnts are often connected with cancer (says " Science Siftings ") and late Observations sug- gest some influence of the sulphur in coal. The disease seems to affect those who use coal as fuel much more than those who burn only- peat. In the coal-burning parts of Nairnshire the death-rate is notably high, but in a section of the county depending on peat there has been but one cancer death in ten years, and the Orkneys show similar differences.- The only exception to the rule that the burners of Coal are the sufferers wa,s found in certain islands where, with high cancer rate, peat is used that is of a peculiar coal-like, sulphurous kind.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>Scot-Fall.</head>
        <p>The Scot-Fall.</p>
        <p>From recent experiments it appears that 654) tons of. soot fall on a square mile in the East End of London in one year; while in the manu- facturing district of Coatbridgo the tests show the enormous amount of 1,939 tons of soot and dust to the square mile. These figures (says the " Sanitary Record ") are .appalling, and indicate the extreme importance of the work of the Smoke,.Abatement Society to the health of the community. We are, as a nation, not yet doing one-hundredth part of our duty as regard's keeping the air of our towns and the water of our rivers free from avoidable contam- ination, but so long as we make some progress,</p>
        <p>however slow, we are on the right road.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>Beans v. Beefsteak.</head>
        <p>Beans v. Beefsteak.</p>
        <p>Statements have lately been published to the. effect that beans, peas, lentils, and peanuts contain twice as much uric acid poison as does ! meat, and that the natives a India suffer greatly because of the amount of uric acid contained in the dahl which they eat. This is an error (says the "Family Doctor"). ,Accord- ing to Professor Hall, beans contain .four grains of uric acid to the pound, whereas .beefsteak contains fourteen grains, liver nineteen grains, and sweetbreads seventy grains to the poUnd. And meat contains nearly three times as much water as beans or other legumes, so it will be readily seen that for a given quantity of food meat contains nearly fifty times as much uric acid as an equal quantity of beaus, peas, or, !</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>Prehistoric Lions.</head>
        <p>Prehistoric Lions.</p>
        <p>Los Angeles, California, has now attained fame as a mine of antique bones. Mr. W. 1L Osgood, curator of the Field Museum in Chicago, was directed to explore in the neigh- bourhood .of Los Angeles, and what he discov- ered there is now astonishing scientists. He found, to begin with, the skull of a prehistoric lion. the skull being eighteen inches long and twelve inches wide across the top. These measurements may not convey much to the ordinary mind, but to the scientist they indi- cate that this was the largest lion that over lived on earth, the skull being twice as large as the largest one heretofore known to science. The explorer also unearthed the fossils of three camels, a drove of horses, and quantity of other interesting bones.</p>
        <p>The Existence of the Soul. The late Dr. Russel Wallace, was once asked if we are " likely to get from spiritualism' proof of the existence of the soul and the per- sistence of consciousness beyond the grave?" Dr. Wallace replied: "I bold that those two points are already established. It is only be- cause the scientific investigations of spiritual- ists are confounded in the popular mind with the .chicanery and impostures of a few char-, latans that the undiscriminating World has not studied the literature of spiritualism. A study of that literature, an honest and unbiassed examination of spiritual investigations, would prove to the world that the soul of malt is a reality, and that death is not the abrudt and unreasoning end of consciousness.</p>
        <p>A Flea Famine in London. The warning of the American Consul at prague against the e:p,orialsatliioisn to that cainty of A mcii,ar, fly-papers: "There are not sufficient folinTryt---(3;elrlys the vain quest of Professor E. A. Minchin recently for fleas in London (says tin. " Westminster Gazette"). Having decided to experiment On the transmission of trypano- somes to rats by means of fleas, Professor Minchill advertised in London for a supply of common .flcas, but was unable to obtain any, althongb he offered a shilling each for them. railing in London, the Professor turned to the provinces, and, In reporting the result to the Quekett 'Microscopical Club, he mentioned that he finally obtained all the fleas he wanted from the 'county of Norfolk.. Possibly Londoners are not so adept at.trapping fleas as the people of Norfolk.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>WHEN YOU ARE GROWN UP.</head>
        <p>WHEN YOU ARE GROWN UP.</p>
        <p>My Dear Children,— I promised to announce a new competition this week, and here we are. I think an inter- esting subject would be what you would like to be when you are grown up. Tell me briefly what you would like to be, and say why you would like to be what you choose.</p>
        <p>There are so many things you can be when you grow up—l hope none of my nieces will be Window-smashing . Suffragetes—that all of you must have already thought about a trade, calling or profession. Tell me what you have chosen, and give the reason why. I remember when I was a little chap of five I *anted to he a sweep, because I thought it must be nice to dispense with the necessity of washing my face. But I changed my mind after and 1 became a journalist. A friend, how- ever, once told me I was, after all, a sweep, ar. I swept in all manner of news into the papers. I do not think he was far Wrong, do you? l'ossibly most of you have already chosen what you are to be, so just put it, and the reason why, in a little letter, and post it to me at "The Examiner" Office; Bewsey-street, War- rington, to reach here not later than 7th February. Be sure, if you post it in an envelope, to put a penny stamp on, as the Postmen fined me quite a number of pennies through you sending into the Christmas Com- petition letters with half-penny stamps on. Please do not forget the date-7th February. Your loving UNCLE JACK.</p>
        <p>.</p>
        <p>"MOTHER'S MAN." Some day when moVier dresses me She puts niy rompers on, Of course I have to ramp an' play, An' keep it going all the day, . An' mother calls me then, you see, Just "Boy" or "Little Son."</p>
        <p>But sometimes if I coax her to, An' coax as bard's I can, She puts my overalls on me, I get my workin' cap, an' see How I can help—there's lots to du; An' then I'm " Mother's Man."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>MUSICAL GAME.</head>
        <p>A MUSICAL GAME.</p>
        <p>A musical search, or searching by music, is not only a pleasant but interesting game, and depends greatly on the skill of the pianist. One of the company retires from the room, and some small article, such as a handkerchief or trin- ket, is hidden. Then someone sits at the piano, and the absent one is called in and told to search for the missing article. The pianist then indi- cates by the strains on the piano when the searcher approaches the bidden object. The further he is away from it, the softer and more mournful becomes the music; as he comes nearer to it, the music becomes louder and more lively, bursting into a triumphant strain as the prize is discovered. A good march makes an excellent final.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>CHARMS OF DUBLIN.</head>
        <p>CHARMS OF DUBLIN.</p>
        <p>So much is being said about Dublin just now, and so aitich is. it under a cloud, that I will attempt: to. show you that even Dublin's cloud has its Silver lining. It is the proud Irishman's boast that within ten miles of Dublin you can catch a trout, slloot a grouse, and hunt a fox, and this I fully believe, although I engage in none of these sports. There are many rivers in the near neighbourhood of Dublin which aro ever being fished b 3 patient citizens.; and not in vain. In the Dublin mountains grouse abound; and it is, not anuncommon sight to see a hunt in county Dublin.</p>
        <p>. The Diver Liffey divides' the city into two parts, the north and the south. In the centre of the city is Nelson's Pillar, from which a splendid view of the city may be had. It is the terminus of all the principal trams. It stands •in the centre of Sackville-street, the widest street in the city. Opposite the Pillar is the General Post Office. O'Connell's Monu- ment and Father Matthew's Statue are in Sack- ville-street also. The Bank of Ireland is on College Green, and Trinity College is on the , other side of the road. The Phenix Park, the second largest park in the world, lies on the banks of the Liffey. In it are the Zoological Gardens, which, although small, contain an ex- tensive and valuable collection of animals. The keepers are very successful in rearing,liou cubs. I believe the new Edinburgh Zoo has a speci- men from Dublin. The Botanic Gardens, at Glasnevin, are very interesting. They contain a great many flowers growing' in The open air, and also a great variety of flowers growing in hot-houses.</p>
        <p>The Dublin mountains, though not high, con- tain many fine beauty spots, and are situated about six miles south of Dublin. From them one can obtain a splendid view of the city and Dublin Bay. Let us imagine that we are stand- ing on one of the Dublin mountains with the bay before us. On a very clear day the Mourne Mountains may be seen. Down beneath us on the left lies Howth, with the Bailey Lighthouse on its farther extremity. We will run our eye along the coast past Sutton and Clontarf, fash- ionable suburbs, till we i:eaclt Dublin. Over the city there hangs a haze of smoke, but we can see quays emerging from the smoke. Two long .piers run out seemingly under Howth, but in reality far from it. Passing Dublin we see the white walls of Merrion and Blackrock Baths. Then, farther south, Kingstown sends its two Piers into the sea. The mailboat te Holyhead has iasi gone, leaving behind it a trail of black smoke.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>USEFUL BOOK FREE.</head>
        <p>A USEFUL BOOK FREE.</p>
        <p>A book almanac which should be in every home has just been published. It can be obtained free from any chemist, and it is really worth possessing, for it is crowded from covey to cover with useful information, valuable advice on health, entertaintng articles and clever pictures. Every copy also carries with it an insurance for £250. It is called "Hollo- way's Almanac and Family Friend for 1914," and it is issued by the proprietors of tbe famous remedies, "Holloway's Pills and Ointment.' You can obtain a c•oPy free from your Chemist's, or by sending a postcard to Thomas Holloway, 113, Southwark-street, S.E.</p>
        <p>The death oecuired at Chatburn on Sunday of Dr. Robinson, for many years medical officer of health for Fleetwood, and a major in the sth Battalion King's Own Lancaster Regiment. He was a native of Blackburn. -</p>
      </div>
