        <p>A BASKET OF POTATOES. What the common potato is to Ireland the sweet potato has been to Satsuma, Japan. To it, declares the author of "In Togo's Country," .11r. If. B. Schwartz, the province undoubtedly owed much of its former power and influence. The introduction of the sweet potato is traced to a basket of the tubers which, in 1698, the King of Loo Choo presented to the Prince of Satsuma. The prince caused them to be planted in the island of Tanegashima, and from there they soon spread all over the pro- vince. Prom Satsuma they have been intro- duced in comparatively recent years to almost- all parts of the empire. Much of Satsuma is too hilly for rice land, but the sweet potato can be grown anywhere. It requires little cultivation and yields a large ^.mount of food from a very small acreage. Boiled with millet, the sweet potato forms the common diet of almost all the farmers and ,00rer people of the province. to fli this timeso b By its aid the rough and not over-fertile than atinilaeen country sustains a vast population, and ill feudal tuber t o other ulitperp:ir;roteavadifnitl famines aieleg. eTI. hialtnimk s o- Itllr which fairest in olden times devastated so many of rest provinces of Japan remained wholly unknown in Satsuma.</p>
