      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>1 I I</head>
        <p>1 I I</p>
        <p>CO</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 17, 1914.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>CHILD CAROL SINGERS.</head>
        <p>CHILD CAROL SINGERS.</p>
        <p>Guardians Object to the Young Inmates' Visit to Runcorn.</p>
        <p>THE YOUNGSTERS "HAWKED" ABOUT.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Board of Guardians was held ;et Dutton on Tuesday, Mr. C. E. Linaker, jun., presiding. On the motion of the Chairman, it was de- cided to send a letter of condolence to the rela- tives of the late Mr. Mawdslcy:whose death we recorded last week. The Chairman said they all knew Mr. Mawdsley well. Everyone thought highly of him. He was always a jolly and genial man, but most sincere and outspoken in all he did and said. Twelve of the members of the District Council and Guardians were at the funeral the previous day, which spoke volumes for their thoughts of the deceased. Mr. T. Wright moved that the Board record the death of Viscount Cross, who passed away at the great age of 92. When the Dutton Workhouse was built 57 years ago Viscount Cross was the first vice-chairman, Sir Richard Brooke being the chairman at that time. Mr. Spruce seconded and the resolution was confirmed.</p>
        <p>Children in Workhouses. The Clerk produced a copy of the Poor Law Institutions Order, 1913, and explained that amongst the new regulations was the one, "That after 1915 no children would be allowed in the workhouse." They had, he said, just made their step in getting the Children's Home in time. On -the proposition, of Mr. Morris, it was d&amp; cided that a copy of the Order be sent to each ,inember.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>Boys and Sea-going Life.</head>
        <p>Boys and Sea-going Life.</p>
        <p>The secretary of the National committee on Sea Training wrote bringing to the notice of the Board the report of the proceedings at the annual conference, and pointing out that it was within the power of Boards of Guardians to forward the cause of sea training and assist the boys under their care to obtain a calling in life by sending those of good character and physique, who desired it, between the ages of eleven and fifteen and a half, to the Exmouth, at Grays, Essex, which had acomplete system of elementary, secondary and technical educa- tion, including a sea-going tender. Poor Law boys from country unions were received at the rate of 9e. per head per week. The Chairman: We will duly consider this when the occasion arises. • It was decided to file Oleo communication for future reference.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>Miscellaneous Matters.</head>
        <p>Miscellaneous Matters.</p>
        <p>The Local Government Board wrote confirm- ing the appointment of Miss F. E. Garlington as assistant nurse at the Workhouse at a salary of £25 per annum, with rations. On the proposition of Mr. Woodward, Mr. W. Harrison and Mr. Booth, along with the Clerk, were appointed delegates to the Central Poor Law Conference to be held in London. Miss-. Potts, of Preston Brook, was appointed sewing mistress and assistant at the Children's Home. There were three applicants.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>Messrs. Pilkington's Offer.</head>
        <p>Messrs. Pilkington's Offer.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Children's Home Com- mittee it was resolved, after full consideration of the offer of Messrs. Pilkington Bros., St. Helens, to accept some of the boys in the glass works: (1) That this committee is unanimously agreed that it is one of the best openings for boys that has ever come before us, and we recommend that such as they deem suitable be sent there; (2) that two boys, Thomas Wilkin- son and John MacLeod, be sent there.</p>
        <p>It was also resolved that the superintendent be allowed to take the children to Frodsharn Picture Palace; that Mr. J. W. Hayward be appointed a member of the committee, and that votes of thanks be accorded the ladies and gen- tlemen who had given presents and treats to the children.</p>
        <p>Mr. Spruce moved the adoption of the min- utes. The committee bad felt it would he a -wwwit-epening -to • send the 'Joys- to -St: -Helens. The conditions were satisfactory and the pros- pects very good. There were 89 lads in the hostel under..the care and supervision of an effi_ cient officer, Mr. J. J. Firth, who was formerly the Police Court missioner at Warrington. The committee were also of the opinion that -the lads clight 'to he questioned as to whether they ' would like to go there or on farms.. They con- sidered it a very wise thing to send 14e lade there. . •</p>
        <p>Mr. Antwiss: At whose expense did the chil- dren go to the Picture Palace?</p>
        <p>The- Chaiiiitan : Not at the ratepayers'.</p>
        <p>. . Mr. Spruce: A gentleman in Frodsl!am in- vited them, and said be would provide convey- ances, &amp;c... Mr. Antwiss: I have a strong objection to IL</p>
        <p>Children Carol Singers. Mr. Mokris raised the question of the children going carol singing. Permission was granted ny the Guardians for them to go out singing, but the idea was that they were to go round the neighbourhpod and not to be hawked about the streets at' all hours of the night. We thought they would be going to the various farms in the neighbourhood, and not to Run- corn. I would like to know on whose authority the children were allowed to go to Runcorn. The Chairman: I thought it was to be only in the district. The Clerk: You simply passed a resolution saying they could go singing, and you did not restrict them at all. You may take action for the future.</p>
        <p>- - ----- . Mr. Wood : Mr. Goddard told me he was com- ing with "leni to Runcorn, and asked me for names of friends. I arranged a short • pro- gramme, and the Runcorn people were ari-de- •lighted to see them, and hoped it would be an annual affair. The way the children were sup- ported proves their singing was appreciated. I second the report. The children who go .to St. Helens will get 98. per week to start; and they have. good prospects. Mr. Gibbs: I have heard it remarked that it was a shame to hawk the children round the streets singing. People noticed that one of the members of this Board was at the head, taking them round, and I had the privilege to subscribe whea they cattle up to me in the street. My feeling was that it was very infra dig to Tiawk , the fact that they were children from the homes. The minutes .werp then confirmed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>WINTER SPORTS IN THE BERNESE OBERLAND.</head>
        <p>WINTER SPORTS IN THE BERNESE OBERLAND.</p>
        <p>The progress which winter sports have made in the Bernese Oberland is exemplified by the fact that the opening of Grindelwald as a sport centre has been followed, in the course of com- paratively few years, by the opening of a dozen other places, which, together, can accommodate between five and six thousand visitors. One of the effects of this development is seen in the fact that the Jungfran Railway Company has decided to run a regular winter service of trains connecting with those of the Bernese Oberland and Wengernalp Railways. Five trains will run in each direction between the Little Soheidegg and the jungfrau Jodi Station (11,300 feet) daily. The first train leaves Wengen at 7.48 a.m., azal arrives at the Joel', the highest station in Europe, amidst eternal snow and ice, at 9.55.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <p>Loss of Appetite. Loss of appetite is usually traceable to some form of stomach or liver trouble, and may be taken as a sign that the digestive system is in Reed of a stimulating tonic. When you. can't cat, or when food is distasteful, just try the effect of taking Motlu.r Seigel's Syrup after your meals every day. This splendid herbal remedy tones up and strengthens the digestive organs' sharpens the appetite, and regulates the whole system. Then you not only eat your fo)cl with a relish, but, more imper- taut still, the food you eat enriches your blood, nomiihes your body, increases your strength and vitality, and makes you look well, feel well, and keep well.</p>
