      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>CORONER AND OLD FEUDAL SYSTEM.</head>
        <p>CORONER AND OLD FEUDAL SYSTEM.</p>
        <p>A soquel followed to the shooting affair which took place in a wood between High Legh and Knutsford a month ago, when Mr. S. Brig- house conducted an inquest On the body of the victim, Joseph Skill, 74, ex-gamekeeper, Hollo- well Brow, High Legh, at Warrington, on Sat- urday morning.</p>
        <p>Details of the accident were exclusively re- ported in "The Examiner" at the time, it being stated that Skitt was going through the wood when his foot caught a wire, Which exploded a hidden trap gun. The old man fell, severely wounded in the -thigh. Ile shouted for help, but none being home. En route he fell into a ditch, and with difficulty got out; The old man managed to crawl within a short to crawl was found latefr9 distance of his home, where lie collapsed, and men passing. They summoned medical aid from Lymm, and after treatment Skitt was removed to the War- rington' Infirmary, where he died on Thursday. On Saturday Mr. Henry Oreenall appeared for the relatives, • and Mr. L. Caldecutt, of Knutsford, for the under-gamekeeper employed by Colonel Legit. The Coroner eiplained the case to the jury, , and ;the first witness was then called. 11:byht:otmwoing:oup:ogeeeded</p>
        <p>The Coroner : Did the deceased say how he received his injuries?</p>
        <p>Witness: He said that he "saw the gun, but saw it .to late." Deceased had been ill for some time, and was far from well.</p>
        <p>Mr. Calaecutt rose to cross-examine the wit- nce.:4 as to deceased's weakness, when</p>
        <p>The Coroner remarked that no doubt Skitt had been ill, but the solicitor could not c 011.., vince the jury, well, ai ally rate, could not con- vince hirn,-that the deceased would have died when he did if it had not been for the accident.</p>
        <p>THE UNDER-KEEPER'S STORY. Robert Henry 'Oreaves said he was the under- keeper, of Northwood, High Legh. He had known Skitt for about two years, and they were on very friendly terms, as Skitt was a retired gamekeeper.. '7' The Coroner: And he could give you wrinkles? Witness: Yes. Referring to the accident, Wit- ness said lie heard the report of the alarm gull, and he knew it had come from that gun in Whitefield Woods. He 'proceeded to the spot, but on the way he was informed by a man named Leigh that Skitt had been removed to his home. There was, no shot in the gun. The Coroner: Did you receive orders from the head keeper to set these guns in October? Witness.: Yes, I set three. .</p>
        <p>REMNANT OF FEUDAL SYSTEM. The Coroner: This is like a remnant of the feudal system—the setting of alarm guns. Witness: They were only to fire an alarm if anyone were In the woods. It was not to hurt anyone. :</p>
        <p>The Coroner : When did you set this gull? Witness: The ,day Worm • The gun was produced, and 'witness said this was partly fixed in the ground, and it was quite visible. Skitt told him the place would be a good one. When I saw him next be said, "I don't know what I was thinking about. I clean forgot the gun." The Coroner. Skitt was allowed to go over the estate?</p>
        <p>- Witness z Yes. Mr. Greenall : This is not a public footpath? Witness:. No, sir. It is a short cut through the wood. lie must have gone off the footpath to avoid the mud. Witness added that it was Possible Ile might not have cleaned all the dirt out of the muzzle of the gun. The piece of slag might have ricochetted off the ground. Skitt was pensioned off by Colonel Legh and lived rent free in a cottage. -</p>
        <p>THE CORONER'S VIEW. In his address 'to the jury, the Coroner said You have to consider three things. First, was • there an accident? Second, if so, do you .think the injuries hastened or accelerated his death? • Although the doctor seemed to have some doubt on the matter, I don't think he has much doubt in his own mind. When the slag goes through to the other Ride, I think your common-sense will tell you that in all reasonable probability he would have lived longer if he, had .not re- ceived the wound, and therefore, if that is so, the wound is the cause of death. You have to come to the conclusion; also, whether it was an accident or an illegal action. , The gamekeeper seems a decent, honest, open- hearted man, who has told the truth. I ,will read you the law on the subject. • The Act says "If 4 person sets a spring' gun calculated to destroy human life or inflict grievous bodily harm with intent, it is illegal." Here . the keeper tells you it was simply- set to give an alarm with a- blank cartridge, so that if a 'per- son trespassed, notice would be given to the keepers. If you think this gun was set under circumstances which were legal, I don't think you will have any doubt in your minds that it was one of those regrettable -Accidents that do occur.' •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>A_ NEPHEW'S STORY.</head>
        <p>A_ NEPHEW'S STORY.</p>
        <p>William Skill&gt; the neiTihew, a colliery man- ager, of 126, London-road, Stockton Heath, said his uncle had been engaged as keeper oh- the High Legh estate for 43 years. He retired some time ago, and resided at Brook Cottage, High Legh. On the lfth December he (witness) heard that his uncle had had an accident. He jour- neyed from Staffordshire, and wlen he arrived at High Legh his uncle said that Greaves, the under-gamekeeper on the estate, had offered to sharpen his razor. His uncle thought he would go for it to Greaves' house, He went by a near road through the wood, thinking that he might meet Greaves on the way. In getting out of some mud he set off the alarm gun. He fell and knew at once what had happened. He said, William, I don't know whatever I was thinking about." Witness visited the Infirmary frequently and his uncle spoke several times of the accident, alwilys bear- , big out his previous statement. On the 3rd January he (witness) returned from Hanley and found his uncle very weak and gradually sinking. ' He stayed until the end. Mr. Caldecutt Did he say he was off the foot- path when he caught the wire? Witness: No; he did not say whether he was or was not.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>MEDICAL EVIDENCE..</head>
        <p>THE MEDICAL EVIDENCE..</p>
        <p>• - - Dr. Law, senior house surgeon at the War- rington Infirmary, said that when deceased was admitted he was suffering from an injury to his left leg as a result of the accident. He lost a great deal of blood. They X4ayed him, and three days afterwards his left leg was opened and a piece of Slag (produced) was extracted. got weaker, refused his feed and gradually sank. skitt then did ver • The Coroner: What was the cause of death? Y well for a short time, but he Dr. Law: The immediate cause of death was heart failure. The Coroner I suppose the injury he re- ceived through this alarm gun accelerated his death? - • ':</p>
        <p>Dr. Law :It hastened it. It was a contribu- tory cause.</p>
        <p>_ The Coroner:. In all Probability would lie injury? if lie had ,• not received this have lived longer</p>
        <p>Dr. Law: Yes, he would.</p>
        <p>MIGHT HAVE DIED ANYWAY.</p>
        <p>Mr. Caldecutt: raCii;un—ii-n—g—he—b-ad gone to bed from any other cause, would he have died? Dr. Law : Highly probable. Mr. Caldecutt:: Ile had recovered from his injury, and if beliad been strong enough he Would have been discharged? Dr. Law: Wei.attempted to get him up, and lie had to be I nit back to bed again, if lie had been well euouglae would haVe.been sent home. , Mr. Caldecntt : It was the lying in bed for suc!i a long time that brought on weakness that caused his death?, ,</p>
        <p>• Dr. Law: Yes. He had also a Serious inflam- matory condition of the Gladder which was a most contributory cause of his. death. Mr. Caldecutt : You don't say he died As the result of the gun shot wound? Dr. Law: No, sir. The Coroner: But you said just now be would have lived longer but for the gun shot wovid. Do you mean to say if he had not received a gun shot wound he would have 'died when he did? Dr. Law: I could not say he would have lived longer.</p>
        <p>COMmON-SENSE rONCLUSION. . The Coroner : Suppose he had not received this wound, would be have died on that date? Dr. Law: I could not possibly say. The Coroner , :.What is the common-sense con- clusion to be drawn. Here is a man who re- ceives a wound on the 11th December and_ion the 15th a small piece of slag is taken out of his thigh. In the face of that', would you say he would have died when he did if this had not haPpened? because one has to take into consideration t Dr. Law: I am sorry I cannot answer that lo Your satisfaction, he physical condition of the man, apart from the accident. lie was an old man, and pretty well broken down with a very in- flammatory condition of the bladder. Anything might have hastened his death. The Coroner: All the more likely that a wound like thid would hasten or accelerate his cleath _ .</p>
        <p>Dr. Law, / spy it did 'hasten it, but I could not sity whether he would have lived longer if he had not received the wound.</p>
        <p>DANGEROUS STATE OF HEALTH. The, Coroner: if I am doomed to die to-night a(l2 Wclock, and someone comes to me and hits me.,80 that I die before, he is guilty of murder. Caldccutt : lie was in such a -daw,°erous state of bealth.that be might have died from natural causes? Law: Yes.</p>
        <p>, Air. Caldecutt: Do you think if he „bail had lo gu to bed for any reason at all he would ever la," recovered? „"r• Law: Ile would not. I understand that Dr. Thorpe had been attending him for some time, and he had said he ,could do nothing for lu'o, and that he .was an old nian commencing senile decay. Mr. Caldecutt: Dr. Thorpe has had to go right i. to a c set it" t" I :. ° under all operation and cannot be here to-day. The Coroner. I shall have to say to the jury would this man have died when he did if he had not received the injuries. If the injuries accel- erated or hastened his death they w—Suld have to inquire into all the circumstances of the (accident. Then the question would arise, "Did his man set the gun?" and if so, "Had he a quite appreciate the legal Position. '</p>
        <p>_ The Coroner: If he had no right to set it he must get out of it the best way he can. If this had been a ease under the Workmen's Compen- sation Act—my wont! If they hurt a finger they g° for something. Caldeeutt: How do you think this slag came into the wound? Dr. Law: It must have been from the muzzle of this alarm gun being slanted somewhat. It bad nearly gone 'through the thigh. It must have gone from the muzzle and ricochetted off smnetbing.</p>
        <p>CLOTHES COVERED WITH BLOOD. Mrs. Bourne said that the deceased resided alone in the cottage next door to her.. She was in the house when she heard a shot, but, thought nothing about it. Later she saw deceased in the roadway opposite her house, and on going out found him lying on the ground near the hedge, three yaixi; from the gateway. His cloth- ing was covered with blood. She obtained assistance from a Mr. Leigh, who was passing, and the deceased was carried into the house.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>'ACCIDENTAL DEATH."</head>
        <p>'ACCIDENTAL DEATH."</p>
        <p>The jury retired and on their return the foreman said : "lie met his death accidentally, and there is no blame attaching to the game- keeper,"</p>
        <p>Recalling Greaves, the Coroner said : "I have had 30 years' experience Of human nature,. and I know what is sometimes Said. The jury and myself are certain that the account you gave was true, and that you were on the best of terms with this man, and perfectly, satisfied that y,ou did no act to which yotr were not entitled. The jury say, and so do I, that there is not the slightest blame attaching to you.</p>
        <p>Commencing on the 19th hist., Mr. and Mrs. L. V. Harcourt are ro pay a series of visits to Most of the Liberal Clubs in. the Rossendale Division.</p>
        <p>On Saturday', in a fog. which extended from the Isle of Mau to 'Liverpool, tlie Manx steam packet Douglas, in charge of Captain Cain, was the only vessel to enter the Mersey at her usual time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>Danger of Influenza.</head>
        <p>The Danger of Influenza.</p>
        <p>A warning is necessary to all who have been attacked by Influenza, .because of the petits of its after-effects, which leave the system dangerously debilitated, the nerves and heart enfeebled, and the blood laden with noxious germs, New strength, with new vitality and steady nerves are recoverable after Influenza only by making the blood pure - and red. This is readily accont- clished by Dr. Williams' Pink Pills, which by their power of renewing the bload,have cured inanycases of Influenza's after-effects. Miss M. Heywood of ii Doeor Street, Burnley, mentions : In the early part of last year I had a had attack of Influenza. I lost much strength, and one doctor said that I was dangerously weak and bloodless. " Then Bronchitis followed, and I had to remain in bed. .But besides the cough and weakness I had pain all over me. • My digestion was too weak to manage anything better than beef-tea or milk. I got little sleep at nights, and my blood seemed like cold water.</p>
      </div>
