        <head>Dr. Williams' Pink Pills.</head>
        <p>Dr. Williams' Pink Pills.</p>
        <p>SOUTH LANCS. ENTERTAINED.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>Getting the Public and t'ae Regimen</head>
        <p>Getting the Public and t'ae Regimen</p>
        <p>into Touch.</p>
        <p>MR. F. A. FROST'S EXAMPLE.</p>
        <p>By the generosity of Mr. F. Aylmer Frost, a substantial hotpot supper was enjoyed in the Drill Hall on Monday night , try the men .at Orford Barracks, the Second South Lancashire bandsmen, and the local •Territorials. It was by no means a " thin red line," for it is esti- mated that over 506 attended. Mr. Frost—pre- sided, and he was supported by Major Green, Capt. ad Adjutant Cotton, Capt. B. Evans. Capt. Anderson, Lieut. and Quartermaster J. Baines, Lieut. R. Withers and Lieut. F. Gebbie, from the Depot, and the following officers of the Territorial Force: Col. B. Fairclough, Major G. R. Crosfield,. Major Appleton, Capt. and Ad- jutant Izod, Capt. and Lieut. Fairclough, Capt TT. G. Roberts, Capt. F. R. Monks, Capt. Smith, Lieut. L. S. Henshall, Lieut. M. Woods, Lieut. Robinson, Lieut. B. T. Dickson, Lieut. A. S Jones, Lieut. A. Skinner and Lieut. Whitley. At an interval Mr. Wilfred Ludlow (Man- chester) gave a pleasing entertainment, consist- ing of songs and sketches. The men were supplied with refreshments, tobacco and cigarettes. The catering was excellently carried out by Atkinsons Cafe, Ltd., Bridge-street.</p>
        <p>TO POPULARISE THE SOUTH LANCS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Frost's Practical Aid.</p>
        <p>On the proposition of the Chairman, the toast of "" The King" was duly honoured. Major GREEN proposed the toast " Otir Host." Explaining why the men had been invited that night, he said that most of them—at any fate those from the Depot—would know that in a good n‘any counties in England there was only one regiment—the county .regiment—and the whole of that county was ibterested in the regi- ment, and had nothilig else, -from tt military point of view, in which to interest itself. ,In Cheshire there were many wealthy people, and they had one regiment—the Cheshire Regiment. Anything that was going to be done by the civil people in Cheshire, or other counties like that, was for their particular regiment, auk there</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>`l4°-"fle'2l) t-R4'l'?7'-telt,""11"</head>
        <p>`l4°-"fle'2l) t-R4'l'?7'-telt,""11"</p>
        <p>'hers. In the ...ncashire Itegiment, of which they had 'representatives of every batta- lion, with the exception of St. Helens Battalion there that night, it was merely one of eight Lancashire regiments of different names, in- cluding ,the Lancashire .Fusiliers; the North Laneashires, the .East .Lancashires, the ilfan ahesters, the Liverpools, the South Lancashires. It etood to reason that in Lancashire, where a regiment could only have a portion of the county from which to get its recruits, and where neople took an interest in it, they were some- what. handicapped. The portion of Lancashire that fell to that regiment embraced Warring, ton,'St. Helens, Widnes, Newton, and the neigh- bourhood. That comprised the whole •of What was called their recruiting area, and though the •neople at the time of the South African war were exceedingly good, to the regiment, yet it was not the same thing as having a whole county to itself.</p>
        <p>An Effort That is Bearing Fruit. Since he came to the Depot it had been his earnest wish and endeavour to get the men under his command more in touch with • the people of the neighbourhood than they had been °Pinion, the people of Warring- I;Oelft°dreid hisi ° know enough of their local regiment. ro begin with, there were two regiments at the, Oepot, and many civilians did not know the difference between one regiment and the other. de did not say that disparagingly, but i was difficult for people not accustomed to military matters to distinguish between one regiment and another in the same barracks. They were. now alone, and he had endeavoured to get the South Lancashire more in touch with the people of the neighbourhood, from which they hoped to draw recruits. That being so, he had spoken to leading civilians in Warrington, and first and foremost lie spoke to Mr. Frost, their host—(applause)—who was entirely with him, and he had promised lie would do whatever he :.:ould to further the object he (the speaker) had in view. \ Ile thought he could say that the I,hings they had tried to do between them were seginning to bear fruit—from a recruiting point of view he spoke with certainty. They had had a great many More recruits in the last year for the Regular Army and the Special Reserve _than had been known before for a good many years. tie found that ten years ago at the Depot they got during one year 1000 recruits, and last year they got 306. They wanted more. Mr. Frost had very kin dly asked -.the, South Lancashire Regiinent to be his guests that evening. There lavere representatives of four of the five batta- lions there that night, and he asked them to drink the health of 11r. Frost very cordially. (A pPlause.) —The toast was received with musical honours.</p>
        <p>MR. FROST AND OUR "FINE REGIMENT." In reply, AiroFSCIST thanked the men for theii cordial reception. He would be getting "swelled head" after such a reception from all the men. fie was pleased to be able to welcome the Band of the 2nd Battalion to their home. (Loud a)plause.) Although Warrington or Southi Lancashire might not be the .birth-place of all the men, it was the birth-place of some, and if they were not born sons they were sons by adoption—(applause)—and they were proud tc. own them as such, congratulated the band upon the most excellent performances they had given; under the able leadership of Mr. Cahill. (Applause.) Through the band the regiment had done a great (teal for its native town, having raised a substantial amount, with the aid of Mr. Macnaghten and Mr. Turpin, who worked very hard for them, and raised a substantial sum for the Infirmary, which needed all the support that could be given. A subscription of .1:40 meant a great deal to those who had the management of the Infirmary in hand, and who were always scratching their heads to know from where the next copper was coming.. Thcro was an empty ward in the Infirmary, I)ecaus they had not got enough funds to run it Ifc would like to see it performing its purpose the band had been in the town much longet that ward would be used. His regret was that they did not see more of the line battalion amongst them. Although the barracks would not hold all of thin, he wished it could be arranged with the War Office that the barracks could be filled with men of the" line, and then recruits would flow in. They were exceedingly proud of that fine reginient. There could be</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <p>nothing better than to be a member " Fighting 40th." (Applause.)</p>
        <p>Superb Ignorance.</p>
        <p>lle thought the lack of recruits was relisztee and apathy6of the general Their 'ignorance was superb. lie heardl man who had enlisted, and he was said 11 rom Ireland. lie joined the Army. what regiment he was in, he said, "The, Asked " Why?' he said "Because I WO 5e near my brother in the 21th." laughter.). That was a sample pf the ign if the public. Some people had the ide going into barracks was like going to de had not tried either, but he did not there was much vf the prism about 01 racks of the British Army. If they wet 331diers would not be there.</p>
        <p>Major Green had said that during th War the people of Warrington and Soot ,ashire were good and kind, but he d hink it ought to be necessary to have a Ind bloody battle going on before the ki If the town and neighbourhood showed de was a man of p6ace, but he thought good for the men to know that when wal iut the eyes of all Warrington and Soot ,ashire were on them. If there were n ators at a football match it would be Efferent game indeed.. Ile hoped tb Notdd feel that the eyes of all were upon mud render a good account of themselves, lid in the Boer War for those at home. hear.)</p>
        <p>, Advice to the Territorials. , He was pleased to see the Territorial' ;hat night, for they were a body of -1 xhich Warrington was proud. If ai *ere in the air to-day he would advise tlu torials to agitate for better opporthnii •make themselves more efficient. Thre( 'and men would carry a great weight vi lovernmeut which happened to be in l'he government, in one sense, did not E -dle people governed themselves. If they a or more training they -would be able !omfortable„ knowing that if the expedil 7orces Were sent away, their places we .':aken by the Territorial forces. He f Colonel Fairelough and the officers of the Force for •lending him that hall 11. yaiters. He was sorry the band had lie town on Monday nest. (Hear, heaA rlop:s.d it would not be nine years befoi !azue again, for they were a credit to '-own„.their country, and the Empire: nlause.) The singing of the National Anthei " Auld Lang Syne" concluded the pror€</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <head>HEREFORDSHIRE TEACHERS' T</head>
        <p>HEREFORDSHIRE TEACHERS' T</p>
        <p>At the meeting of the Herefordshire C.7oancil.:on Saturday, Colonel U. Prescot presiding, the action of the Educatioi ruittee in refusing to grant the teachers A salaries came up for approval.</p>
        <p>The committee .presented a report in ',ley- anted that they had decided by 31 two that it was not desirable to estat TheY'had arrived at the conclusio the.salaries. of head teachers in some case lower :than those paid to teachers in most :ouiri.les; and they had, unanimously deci merease them to tho extent of about „e 1.31 ;nemeses to take effect as from January 1. ')owever,.had not had the desired effect, a etieliers stated that they would only be sa, with an adequate scale of salaries. To e %..ise their determination a number . signed.</p>
