      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0184" n="art0184">
        <head>FIELD OF SPO .</head>
        <p>FIELD OF SPO .</p>
        <p>WARRINGTOJI TEAM TO CO TO HULL</p>
        <p>Despite the Strike the Committee Are Able to Select a Strong Side.</p>
        <p>THE RESERVE MEN TO THE CLUB'S RESCUE.</p>
        <p>The sensation of the week in Viarrington foot- ball circles, alai people not usually concerned about footuail have also been interested, has been the strike on the part of the Warrington Senior team. As was given in detail in last week's " Examiner," the men " downed tools" on Friday night, and it was found impossible to send a team through to Hull to carry out the Imigagement with Hull Kingston Rovers. Had the committee not been taken by surprise at the last moment it might have been possible to have drafted a side, but this was impossible, and SO the Secretary was most reluctantly compelled to cancel the engagement at Hull. The Position of the strikers was set forth in 4, rie last issue of "The Examiner," and briefly heir chief complaint seems to have been that were not given a bonus for winning the liddersfield match. A special meeting of the ci Committee was held on Saturday night, Pd it was decided that no bonus should be grren.</p>
        <p>ton was Bpecially invited to compete for by Hull, a portion of the "gate" going to charity. The teams will be found set out below.</p>
        <p>MATTER SUB JUDICE.</p>
        <p>As the members at the committee meeting are to sit in judgment upon both sides in the dispute the matter may now be considered sub judiee, and therefore it would be unfair to both parties to the dispute to make any comment One comment, however, is permissible, and that is the extremely generous manner in which the committee have offered to meet Hull King- ston Rovers in the hope of recouping them for the loss of thetpateh on Saturday. .</p>
        <p>The committee have offered to give the match , fixed.for Easter Monday at Warrington to Hull, and thus the Kingston Rovers would have a great attraction for that day, and possibly a better gate than that which would have accrued had the match been played last Saturday; The offer is under consideration.,</p>
        <p>THE CHRISTMAS BOX QUESTION. ,tAnother complaint of the players was that 'AU". '4'ere not given a sufficient Christmas box. member of the committee stated that they nad l'eeeived a handsome one, but this the v,'ltYm's deny, and further state that it consisted m a sovereign per man. vll'rY important meeting of the committee was held..at the Pavilion on Monday night; and then it was decided to suspend the players, Pending anY action to be taken by the Northern Union League, who held a committee meeting at Huddersfield on Tuesday night. Prior to that the position of affairs at Warrington was made known officially to the Secretary of the League.</p>
        <p>Councillor Osborne, the chairman of the War- rington Committee, attended the gathering at Huddersfield, and made a statement on the situation, hut as the meeting was private no details are available. It was understood, how- ever, that the League Committee meanwhile left the matter in the hands of the club.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0185" n="art0185">
        <head>SUSPENDED SINE 01E.</head>
        <p>SUSPENDED SINE 01E.</p>
        <p>- There was some confirmation of the belief that . the matter was left in the hands of the Warring- ton Committee by the further action taken at a 1 oleeting held on Wednesday night, over which Councillor Osborne presided. . The official state- nient in regard to this meeting was: At the request of the committee, Bradsbaw, captain of the team, appeared before the eoMmittee and stated that the players were not Prepd are ;_, to play until the committee had re- Tits'eted the question of the Christmas gift. Rpm.lthe committee refused to do, and the dis- Th'liltecim Players were suspended sine die." ne4. aB,-7,e4es that the players will not figure in ' Lu'e Matches to-day, strike will 2 continue. and therefore the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0186" n="art0186">
        <head>MEMBERS MEETING CALLED.</head>
        <p>MEMBERS MEETING CALLED.</p>
        <p>Th _ the -6 members have been much c,oncerned c,- r unhaPpy incident, and at the meeting of the ceromittee on Monday night a requisition for a general meeting of the club signed by 50 mem- bers Was received. Upon that the following °Seial announcement was made: Rule the requisition did not comply with 'ule 13, which says that 'at any time on receiving a requisition and stating the business rd signed by 50 members of the club the ,eratarY shall convene a special meeting to take place within ten days after the requisition 1! been in his hands,' and as this requisition"asl_"a not state the business a meeting could not ue convened!' At the committee meetint, on Wednesday it was officially announced that a requisition in 14ProPer order had been received, and that a .Pecial meeting of members would be convened . for Thursday next, 22nd January.</p>
        <p>TWO TEAMS SELECTED. At the close of the special meeting on Monday it Was officially annoUnced that: It was decided that the fixture with Hull next Saturday (to-day) and the 'A' team fixture with Huddersfield at Warrington will be fulfilled." Again, at the meeting on Wednesday, it was Officially announced that two teams were selected from the Reserve team that from other players on the register. This means that the fixtures, so far as can be foreseen, will duly take place. The first team .travel to Hull this morning, where they play null at the Boulevards in a combined League and Charity Cup match. The trophy is the "Greenbank" Charity cup, and this Warring-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0187" n="art0187">
        <head>TO-DAY'S MATCH AT HULL. A Fairly Representative Team.</head>
        <p>TO-DAY'S MATCH AT HULL. A Fairly Representative Team.</p>
        <p>The committee have selected a fairly repre- sentative team to make the journey to Hull to-day to play the combined League and Charity Cup match; and they are assured of a cordial welcome. Of the old team which have played recently names figure in the list, only two namely, Jolley and Young. There are some, however, who have had an experienee against senior sides and all the others are "triers." null are turning out their strongest side. The teams selected are:— Warrington. Jolley. Way-well, C. Brown, Brookes, Baker. A. Brown, Young. Harmer, T.Tailor. Green, Bevan, Critchlen rtoardman, Herridge. 01(lhairi, Haramill, Once, Taylor, Holder, Anderson, A. Rogers. Batten, Devereux, Gilbert, Francis._ E. Rogers. Hull. Au excursion train is expected to run d, an it leaves Warrington Central Stat. ion at 9.10 this morning. It is anticipat,ed the t ' earn will travel on it.</p>
        <p>NEW "A" TEAM AT WILDERspooL Bramhall To Turn Out Again. The club have built up a new "A" team, and they are to have the pleasure of entertaining Huddersfield "A" at Wilderspool to-day. tinder ordinary circumstances this should have been a line match, but we are passing through extra- ordinary times,-ailt it is hardly likely the new A's" will. overthrow Huddersfield, although one never can tell what may happen. Some of the team are old friends and a few are new- comers. The Warrington side will be: Bramhall. , Aspinall, Shaw, Page, Harold Davies. - Douglas, J. Brown. Worrell, Wright, Bradshaw, Fletcher, Arnold, Jaundrell. • 'The kick-off in the match at Wilderspool this afternoon is at 2.45.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <head>LEAGUE SECRETARY'S VIEWS. "Strike Will Be Soon Over.”</head>
        <p>LEAGUE SECRETARY'S VIEWS. "Strike Will Be Soon Over.”</p>
        <p>What may be accepted as an official view of the strike from the N.U. League standpoint was expressed by Mr. T. H. Houghton, the secretary of the League, at an entertainment given to St. Helens players. Mr. Houghton said there could only be one issue to the matter. It was generally thought that the time had come when the Northern League Committee should step in between the Warrington Club and its players. But it was not any part of their duty to interfere with the management of any club. They felt that War- rington was able to mind its own affairs. He was quite sure that before the week was out the Warrington difficulty would be settled. It would be settled as only it could be settled, and the Warrington Committee must maintain the dignified position which it held, and the players must assume their proper position.</p>
        <p>"No Breach of Agreement." As he understood, there was no breach of agreement, and that being so, there could be no trouble so far as the League Committee was con- cerned. He wished to say to both members of</p>
        <p>the committee and the players present that if the element of true sportsmanship was to be dominated by s. d., then the sooner they put up their shutters and closed down the better for sport generally, lie did not believe that the time had come when the players simply went on the field for the money that was offered; if he felt that, then all his years of labour for football had counted for nothing. He was persuaded that if they asked for vol- unteers to carry through their fixtures, without one penny of financial advantage except in the shape of travelling expenses and erut-of -pocket expenses, they would have more than sufficient players to carry out the full programme, and carry it out well. (Applause.) He was satis- fied of that, but the question of money had crept into their agreements. That being so, he felt that the players who had made those agree- ments at the beginning of the season must carry out their agreements.</p>
        <p>" No Need for Trouble."</p>
        <p>The commirt-te-e- and the players knew exactly, where they stood, and there was no need for trouble. It was not right or fair, sportsmanlike or British, for men to engage to do certain things on certain terms, and then, because they felt they were doing their work exceptionally well, to suddenly demand a tremendous increase on the terms they had agreed to. He hoped that the players and committee of every club would hold to each other, honour and trust and have confidence in each other, then no difficulty could arise. They need have no fear; the Warring- ton Club would go on, and the players would some time, when the Warrington Committee said that the time had come, resume their posi- tions in the team.</p>
        <p>P4O BONUS FOR HUDDERSFIELD OR WIGAN. Official Announcements by Club Secretaries. The Wigan and Huddersfield secretaries have written on the bonus question as follows Wigan Football Club, January 15th. Dear Sir,--With reference to your inquiry as to whether our players were promised a bonus if they beat Warrington at Warrington on Saturelay, December 20th, I beg to inform you that they were never promis.d any such thing, and if your players think differently they are entirely wrong.—Yours faithfully,.</p>
        <p>_ GEORGE -TAYLOR, Secretary, Mr. T. P. Bradshaw, Secretary Warrington P.C.</p>
        <p>• Huddersfield Cricket and Athletic Club, January 14th. Dear Sir,—l laid your letter before my com- mittee to-night, and I am directed to inform you that we did not offer any bonus to our team in our match with you, and it is not our custom to offer bonuses for League matches.—Yours sincerely,</p>
        <p>W. H. HIRST, Secretary.</p>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION CUP. Third Qualifying Round. Rylands' Recs. v. Runcorn White Star.</p>
        <p>The-se old rivals met on Saturday on the Gor- sey-lane enclosure, before a record crowd. The visitors had their strongest team, but Rylands' were short of A. Horrocks, who was injured in the last round. Stretford kicked oft, the ball being returned to the centre. Gradually the visitors worked their way to the railway end, where, from a mark, McAllister kicked a goal for White Star. This put the visitors in a happy mood, but it was short-lived, as from a passing bout among the home backs Morris parted to Scarisbrick at the right moment, and that player showed a clean pair of heels to the visitors, scoring a grand try. G. Warren failed at goal. Half-time arrived with Rylands' lead- ing by 1 try (3 points) to 1 goal (2 points). Hoggets started the second half for the visitors, Broadhurst being tackled in possession. A battle royal then commenced between the forwards, who controlled most of the game in this half. From a kick by Warren the visiting back was slow in returning, and his kick was charged down by Stretford, who dribbled the ball over the line, for Whittaker to Score. Warren added the extra points. From a penalty Warren again added a goal. Rylands' won by 2 goals 2 tries (10 points) to Runcorn White Star 1 goal (2 apints).</p>
        <p>Latest developments in connection with the Football Strike will be found on Page 4.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <head>HOCKEY.</head>
        <p>HOCKEY.</p>
        <p>LATCHFORD JUST BEATEN. Latchford were entertained by South Man- chester at Whalley Range on Saturday. A pleasant though rather vigorous game ensued, and South Manchester won a strenuous game by three goals to two. The second eleven were at home to South Manchester "A," and after a pleasant, hard- fought game, ran out victors by four goals to three.</p>
        <p>Team for To-day. The first eleven have no match. The second eleven meet Southport Carlton, at home, with the following: G. Mynekyme; W. T. Eccles and P. Allanson; J. B. Wilkinson, F. C. Newbold and T. Banks; J. .7; McCauley, W. S. Furness, W. n. Minards, A. Knight and C. Newbold. Bully off 2.30. Umpire, Mr. H. Eccles.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
        <head>BEDDI Gby INSTALMENTS</head>
        <p>BEDDI Gby INSTALMENTS</p>
