        <p>A Large Bale full of High-Class Bedding, value TWO POUNDS,' for ONE GUINEA. Payable at 11- Deposit, and 2/- Monthly, For One Week Only! TILIE NATION L eLeTRING</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0191" n="art0191">
        <head>SURPRISE FOR LATCHFORD.</head>
        <p>A SURPRISE FOR LATCHFORD.</p>
        <p>Changing Goalkeepers Leads to Unusual Incident in Warrington League Match.</p>
        <p>WHY CANNOT DIVEX KEEP ENGAGEMENTS"</p>
        <p>A very interesting match was played at Thel- wall-lane between Latchford and Altrincham Lads, when, owing to the recent good form shown by the former club, everybody got a sur- prise by the excellent play shown by the Lads, and instead of the home club annexing both points they were in some respects very lucky to share the honours. The visitors showed better form in midfield, being quicker on the ball and cleaner tKith their passing, but it was in front of goal where they failed lamentably on two :sWhen Kite left his place the ball t back in front of goal, and not a visiting forward appeared to have the strength to put the ball in the net, and then when, to all appear- one of the Mime defenders just managed to fall back and save the situation. While the home team played more slovenly in the, open, ',they, were always more dangerous with their olac sa spNi uo n shots, and Berry, the Lads' goalkeeper, should be complimented for the good work he did, several of his saves from Smith, and especially one from Fogg, being very good, and he was ably supported by his backs, Hallam and Dunn, the former especially being a tower of strength to the visitors, being very sure and clean 'with his kicking. The game ended in a ealless draw and probably ere the season closes it may prove to be a valuable point lost to the home team.</p>
        <p>lacked sting, and when the dfinal whistle blew they had to retire defeated. Result: Oughtring- ton United Reserve, three goals; Altrincham St. Margaret's, two goals.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT ,LEAGUE. Orford Barracks v. Daresbury. These teams met in the return fixture on the Barracks Ground, when the home club, owing to removals, were not able to put the usual team in the field, and the visitors appeared at the full strength. The visitors immediately got near the home goal, and, easy chances of scoring were missed by their -forwards' erratic shooting. The home team were unable to break down the visitors' defence and had to retire into their own half, when one of their backs had the mis- fortune to kick the ball through his own goal. From the centre-kick •the -Soldiers displayed bet- ter form, and carried play into. the visitors' , area and forced a corner. The flag-kick was got away and the visitors rushed. towards the ' opposite end, but were driven back for infringe- ment of the offside rule. This did not relieve ' the home defence much, as the visitors were playing the smarter game. Houghton increased the visitors' lead. Even play in midfield fol- lowed, and at the interval the visitors were , were leading by two goals to none. On resuming, Daresbury were soon busy attacking the Soldiers' citadel, and Lee-Jones scored their third goal. After this reverse the Soldiers played up with determination, :,nd Acton scored their first goal. The game •t as now more evenly contested, but both teams missed easy chances of adding to their score, and, previous to another. attack by the visitors, the home captain changed goalkeepers without notifying the referee. When the new custodian fisted the ball away a penalty was given against the home team. Cash took the kick and steered the ball safely between the posts. From the centre-kick the home forwards now laid siege on the visitors' goal, and after several smart exchanges Acton scored a second goal for the Soldiers. ,The visitors replied with another goal by Houghton, and just before the ,final whistle blew Sansby added another goal for the Barracks. The result was: Daresbury, five goals: Orford Barracks, three goals.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. Second Division. excelsior v. Wilderspool Brewery.</p>
        <p>_ . These teams met each other for the first time this season on the ground of the former at Bewsey-road. The home team were well repre- sented, but the visitors had to play several reserves. The visitors won the toss and Leah set the ball rolling. The Brewery defence was soon besieged, Clayton, in goal, saving some good shots, but Leah beat him with a beautyj and shortly after Hughes-Cox added a second The visitors could not make much impression on the home defence and they were held chiefly in their own half. Although Clayton distin- guished himpelf very well in the visitors' goal, Hughes-Cox beat him a third time, and when the interval was called the home team were :leading by three clear goals. On resuming, it was soon evident the visitors , were no match for their opponents, and they j were unfortunate in losing the services of Vow- ; land, who got a nasty cut over the eye arly. in the second half, and did not resume play. Further goals were added by Walker, O'Neil, Hunt and Hughes-Cox. Result: Excelsior, nine goals; Wilderspool, Brewery, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0192" n="art0192">
        <head>WARRINGTON LEAGUE NOTES.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON LEAGUE NOTES.</p>
        <p>. -- __.. The Warrington and District League's fixture list was made the Poorer by the non-appearance of the Divex team at Stockton Heath, and also the Crosfield's club had to cancel their match with Great Sankey. cam informed the Divex Club cancelled their match with the "Heathens" by wire, which was delivered at the Secretary's address about half- past eleven. This is not the firet time the Divex have offended in this manner, and to treat clubs in this fashion will soon get both the club and 17 bad name throughout tho With respect to Crosfield's having to cancel ditisstr members a very their match with Great Sankey, great disap- pointment was caused to spectators and players alike,as this was looked upon as a " Derby " i match, . h abu t it i tc appearsso the Recreation Comm itt have dah m ee ehow or other with regard to a fresh playing field at Hood-lane, and at the lest ,moment it was found they could not play on it, and their other ground at Folly-lane *as out of the question, as the playing pitch Was nearly under water. The Daresbui7 team met Orford Barracks in their return fixture on the Barracks Ground, this being their second victory when the visitors were successful in gaining Second Division the Wilderspool Brew- eryiadsoamvisit to the Excelsior an, with the defeated to the extent of nine goals to visitors could only muster six of their dbnreouitr.unhirnntteagThP:dehmthienetse • rregeseunlaL.players and filled the vacancies --..-"-- NORTH CHESHIRE LEAGUE. Latchford V. Altrincharn_ Lads. -</p>
        <p>and</p>
        <p>.......___ _. „.... ---. , At Thelwall-lane, before a moderate number of spectators. Teams: Latchford—Rite; Has- wh tpeerll; asnmditliho. bpi n. sown ; Si,d dLaeel le, ,E. eWggebebn dand Car- ebbAltrincham Lads—Berry; Raster. Dunn; Hallam and Presty, Walker and Barnett ; Boland, Lances- ter, Wallis, Kent and Shaw. After the open- ing exchanges, Webb set his forwards going, . and cen- when Presty repulsed them by good ere Bent tackling , and sent 'the ball out to the left, Dunn to concede beat several opponents and centred across the goalmouth, and finally the leather an open gclweaelreLd hie lines. goal, and Has- ltedhe aunpertihgleirtsse. Smith on the right made ptareflilnlne run, and forced ht taFawcr kom imo 8 ent theirea Has- the goal kick thewent past i.but the ball being baillY Placed, it- a corner, was easily got away, and Kent and Shaw transferred to the home end without Emcees% Even play fol- lowed, each end being visited, and play was very fast and keen. Walker fouled Fogg just outside the penalty area, but nothing came of the free kick, and the ball travelled very quickly to the home lines, where Kite was at fault in leaving his goal. The bal. was sent across the vacant goalmouth, and the visiting forwards missed a very easy chance to open thc score. Flaspell, miskicking, gave a corner ki the Baal lc' el, which was placed outside. This brought reljef. to the home defence, and from , kick Lees made a splendid attempt to get .through, when off- side against P. Webb Vo'lll the effort Directly' after Fogg sent in a gran shot, which Berry The ensuin , i tipped over the bar. proved or no benefit, and the ball - t , v, a.s rane- ferred to the home quar.tnrs. waeri the visitors again missed scoring Wu' til 10 enaPty goal in front of them. The whif! e bsoiv fOr val without any score hein the inter- The made., second half oPeadee very briskly, and ea_ll Berry was, quickly e upon to •save. The homesters gained several corner kicks in- quick failed them, but they liu t° improve upon them, Berry, Hallam an D, Lut defending splen- didly for the visitors, and :Lees was very on- lucky with a grand shot which glanced off Hal- lam for a corner', which was sent .wid . From the goal kick the visitirT forward, e well together, and took the ball i combinednt° the home half, when off-side against Lancaster sent them back, and a grand centre b -&amp;-, Y - egg over the bar by P. Webb. After a was headed spell of mid_ field play the visitors' forwards became dan- gerous; and a long ground shot by Kent Was saved by Rite at the expense of a corner. The ball was placed behind. The homes ters again made a raid on the visitors' goal, where Berry made several smart elprances frorn Smith, on the home right. A .._s the end was approaching both teams made strenuous efforts to score, but the defence on both sides Proved too good for the attack, and when the final whistle blew the game ended in a draw, no goals having been scored.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0193" n="art0193">
        <head>NORTH—CHESHIRE LEAGUE. Second Division.</head>
        <p>NORTH—CHESHIRE LEAGUE. Second Division.</p>
        <p>_•-••• Oughtrington Reserve v. .St Margaret's. From .the start the home team began to press and the visitors' backs and goalkeeper had a very busy time, and, owing to their fine play, they saved their citadel from downfall. From a goal-kick they got relief, their forwards mak- ing several efforts to get near the home goal, but their final shots were very weak and thus were easily repulsed. The homesters continued to have the best of the exchanges this half, but met with a very stubborn defence, and several chances were missed by wild shooting and off- side. However, Burrows was successful in netting the ball twice before half-time, which arrived with the home team maintaining the lead.</p>
        <p>On resuming, the " Saints" now had the wind behind them, and they soori began to make their presence felt on the home defence. The home Players only got over the half-way line very rarely, and through the good marksmanship of Cook,- the visitors equalised the score. It was At this stage of -the game that the home team began to reassert themselves, and during a brief spell of attack on the visitors' goal Bell man- aged to put his side ahead with a very good shot. The visitors from now had the best of play, and were unfortunate ill not adding goals to their total, as the homesters did not seem capable of holding their opponents in midfield. It was with their final efforts that the visitors</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0194" n="art0194">
        <head>FRIENDLY MATCHES.</head>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCHES.</p>
        <p>Grappenhall St. Wilfrid's v. Stockton Heath Reserve.</p>
        <p>On the "Saints" ground, Knutsford-road. The " Heathens " at once made a visit to the home quarters, but were driven back by the backs, and the play was transferred to the visitors' lines, where Harrison was called upon to save. Ford, gaining possession, sent the ball between the uprights and thus drew first blood for the home team. Stung by this early reverse. the " Heathens " played up in dashing style and Rigby equalised the score. Continuing to have the best of the exchanges, Sankey was successful in putting the "Heathens" in front. From the centre-kick even play followed and both ends were visited without further success. At the interval the "Heathens" were leading by two goals to one. . On resuming the home defence was hard , pressed, their backs and goalkeeper defending , very well, but at the end of 15 minutes' play ' 'Rigby scored a third goal for the "Heathens." Not to be denied, the visitors came again, and, the home defenders had their work cut out to save their citadel. Give-and-take play followed in midfield, until Sankey received a nice pass, and, making the best of his opportunity, he registered a fourth goal for the "Heathens." The visitors now relaxed their efforts, and, owing to slackness op the,part of their backs, the ball was handled in the penalty area, and one of the home full-backs took the kick and scored. The "Heathens" now advanced on the home goal, but by good defensive play they e ere driven back. The visitors' bucks were caught napping, and the home centre-forward rushed through and scored with a capital shot, after Harrison had partly cleared. . The following exchanges were evenly contested in midfield and no furthel score was made, the game enCing in favour of the "heathens." Result: Stockton Heath, four goals; Grappenhall St. Wilfrid', three.</p>
        <p>TO-DAY'S. GREAT GAME AT PENKETI4. League Leaders' Stirring Rivalry. There will be. another "Derby Day" in the Warrington League tourney this afternoon at Penketh, where Great Sankey meet Stockton Heath. These two teams and the Adult School team are striving mightily to head the League, and are running one another a good race. Great *ankey and the Adult School have not lost a match, but Great Sankey have the advantage of having only dropped one point for a dfaw, while the Adult School have had four draws. Stockton heath have lost one match and drawn one, and have thus dropped three points. The importance of the game this afternoon will therefore be apparent. Great Sankey will be represented by: Bond; Glover and Stephens; 11. Ditclifield, Gardner and Payne; Darlington, Illidge, J. Ditchfield, Bonlter and Houghton. The following players will represent Stockton lleath Simcock; 11, Sankey and P. Gosling; Glover, Denny and Gifford; Antrobus, Jones, Sholeshaft, Dunn and Stubbs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0195" n="art0195">
