      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0197" n="art0197">
        <head>ANGLERS' MEETINGS.</head>
        <p>ANGLERS' MEETINGS.</p>
        <p>Warrington Workingmen's Angling Association.</p>
        <p>N.A. CENTRE.</p>
        <p>The amalgamated general meeting took place at the Old Music Hall, Market Place, between sixty and seventy members putting in an appearance. This is the first meeting of this kind ever held in Warrington in connection with r the Northern Anglers, and meetings of this uescription are to be held annually in future;ae ' it gives all the affiliated clubs a chance to voicb I their opinion. Mr. T. Melling, of Wigan, pre- sided. The first item was that matches of affiliated clubs be held on separate dates, and I this was carried unanimously. The next was that the centre accepts Post Office weights. This was defeated by a large majority, so the rule regarding weighing specimens stands as I before.</p>
        <p>•• Advantages to Warrington Members. Mr. Melling then rose to show us the advan- tage of being Northern Anglers and the manner he explained matters to is was something to our advantage, and he particularly requested mem- bers to carry it to their fellow-anglers. He informed us that Wigan was stocking seven miles of the Leeds and Liverpool Canal, and they intended to put fish in quicker tharCanglers could take them out with rod and line. They were to be all takeable fish from the start. Wigan has almost 1,200 anglers, to be correct 1,157, and each man is levied Is. for the stock- ing of this canal, and there is more than two- already paid up, and by this time next lyear they are estimating on 1,500 shillings annually to stock the water. At present we note that Wigan has not a single foot of private lwater of their own, but they are taking the ex- [elusive rights off the Northern Anglers. Now considering the nuinber of -waters our society I has, and taking into consideration the sub- s?riptions of the two societies, we must be far better catered for than Wigan. 11r. Melliug went on to say that special facilities will be granted to Warrington members, and not one penny will go into the Wigan funds, but will all be spent in stocking the canal. A. S.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON ANGLING ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>Very Poor Sport Last Year. Mr. W. A. Proud' presided at the 23ril annual meeting of tha Warring-ton Anglers' Association in th.liriide'ali. There was a'fais attend- ance, including Mr. P. Knight, the general</p>
        <p>meetin</p>
        <p>Secretary. Mr. F. J. Emson, the assistant secretary, read the report, which stated that "sport during thc past year has, we, believe, been the worst ex- perienced during our lives, and it is difficult to find a solution to the slump. Not only have local waters been affected, but the same dearth of sport has been experienced throughout the country; our private waters have beegnat tz:ntid- cularly unproductive both in the gre the quality of the fish taken. This result is somewhat of a mystery, as there is no gainsaying that our local canals have been stocked to an enormous extent during past years. Of the 110 fish weighed in, 40 have been recor:ied2 9fsr.o, nilsllhoamvee waters, and 70 from foreign waters; of the 51 prizes of the aggregate value of been won from home waters and 36 from foreign waters. "The number of fish recorded is slightly less than the previous year, 110 against 122, with an aggregate weight of 1431 b. 14oz. 9d.r., an average per fish of 11b. lloz. 131dr., against 11b. toz. 6dr• .last year." The report was adopted.</p>
        <p>-- , The General Secretary read the balance sheet, which showed receipts of £275 16s. hi., including a balance of £27 14s. brought forward. At the end of the year there was a balance in hand of I £2B Bs. 11d.</p>
        <p>The prizes were then distributed by the Chairman to the various winners.</p>
        <p>1 Mr. B. M. Crosfield was re-elected president. The following retiring committee men were re-elected: Messrs. J. Lyon Whittle, H. Kent, W. 11. Gandy, G. Hamblett and 11. Taylor. The Bay Horse Society's annual meeting will be held on Saturday, January 24th, at 7 p.m. The agenda includes the secretary's report of the year's working of the club, the balance sheet for 1913, paying out of prize money, and election of officers:</p>
        <p>The first club night of the year will be held at headquarters, Gladstone Arms, on Monday, January 26th, at 8 p.m., when names of all new members may be handed in and subscriptions received.</p>
