      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0222" n="art0222">
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 17, 1914.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0223" n="art0223">
        <head>CHINA'S "TWO EYES."</head>
        <p>CHINA'S "TWO EYES."</p>
        <p>A Strange Belief Held by the Celestials.</p>
        <p>TRAP SET FOR EVIL SPIRITS.</p>
        <p>1 The Chinese have a strange religion, at least strange to we Occidentals. ' They have "eyes' in their shoes so that the feet can see where tc, ) walk, "eyes" in their boats so that the littic vessels may see in navigating the weirs. They arrange their houses so that evil spirits may bc trapped and killed as soon as they enter. These curious facts were disclosed by Professor Currie Nlartia in the second lecture on "The Great Religions of the World," delivered under ' the auspices of the Warrington Sub-Union of Adult Schools, in the Academy-street School on , Monday night. The subject was the "Religion of China." Mr. W. Twiss presided over a large attendance.</p>
        <p>The lecturer said that the religion of China was a composite one, made up of four different elements, namely, Animism, Confucianism, Buddhism, and Tao-ism. In the °reline* Chinaman the inclusion of these divers elementf, produces a very wide catholicity of spirit, and enabled him, without qualms of conscience, ti worship in either a ' Confucian, Buddhish, oi Taoist temple, although so different in then doctrines. In the south and south-west of China, Animism is the. predominant phase of religion.</p>
        <p>, The Two Eyes. A traveller sees evidences of this wherever he goes. The tiny shoes of the children have two eyes on the toes—so that the tiny feet can see the way on land; the boats on the rivers have two eyes on the bow—so that they can see their way on the water. The spirits of wind and water are the two chief spirits of water. Not the wind and water of our physical and meteoro- logical ideas. But the Chinaman hears the wind and sees the waters rising in their devas- tating floods, and he puts it down to their evil spirits. Consequently, when he wishes to build a house, he consults his priest as to the most suitable site, a site where the wind and water spirits are least likely to interfere. Even inside the house provision is made to keep the spirit out. As a spirit is always said to move in a straight line, the doors are I arranged so that if the spirit enters he dashes against a wall and is killed. Animism underlies the whole thought of the community. One day,in his travels in Central China, he saw a man with a crowd round him, and on going to satisfy his curiosity as to the cause, he found the man was selling pieces of tiger, and the vendor telling his audience that tiger flesh dried, ground to powder and swal- lowed, transferred to the eater the qualities of strength possessed by the royal beast, and the eating of a tiger's hair in the same way gave the swallower another tiger quality—that of courage.</p>
        <p>Teacher and Reformer. Confucius, the great teacher, was really a great reformer. He went back to the far past and tried to give new life to an old religion. It had been asserted that Confucius was an Agnostic, because he was a pure moralist and thought about God and a future life. But this was only partly true, because so much of the Master's teaching was of a positive type. He does not, however, speak much of the unseen world and power. This was largely the reason why Confucianism was so great a failure in China. The Chinese had tried it for over 2,000 years, ftnd its power had failed. There had been a movement in recent years to raise Confucius into a deity, so that his personality might act as an inspiration.</p>
        <p>Christianity in China. The great inspiring idea which was creating the modern spirit in China was the personality not of Confucius, but of Christ. Buddhism; another great Eastern religion, was alien to, China, and had been imported from India. Many interesting and illuminating facts were, also given about Taoism and its great teacher, Lao Tzu. and -hiii doctrine of the Way. -Many beautiful parables and passages were read from the writings of Lao Tzu and Kwawyin. The discussion and questions which followed • replir</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0224" n="art0224">
        <head>NEW KINENIA HOUSE,</head>
        <p>NEW KINENIA HOUSE,</p>
        <p>emu ?en STREET,</p>
        <p>To BE OPENED NEXT MONDAY.</p>
        <p>The New Star Kinema House will be opened on Monday morning by the Mayor, and in the afternoon, at 2.30, the public performances will be commenced. The building is a handsome one, beautifully furnished, and decorated, and is capable of seating 700 Persons. The lonic style of architecture has been adopted, and the com- fort of patrons waiting for admission has been assured by the provision of glass verandahs to act as shelters from the rain and snow. On each side of the pay box, which is artistically ornamented, there are two glass-panelled entrance doors of white, the other entrance being in Greenall-street. The colour scheme is carried out in cream, white and mahogany. The interior of the theatre is exceedingly comfort- able, as well as beautiful. The best seats, which are wider than those ordinarily used, are on the tip-up principle, and upholstered in red plush, with brass studs, whilst in the fourpeallY and twopenny parts tip-up forms, in carriage repp, are used. The floor is on a gradient of "one in eight" from the front. The spacious auditorium is well ventilated. The complete</p>
        <p>safety of the public is assured by the provision of four emergency exits, and we understand the hall could be cleared in one minute, if. necessary. The operating chamber is built on the most modern lines, being absolutely fireproof. Two of the latest Pathe machines have been installed and the pictures will be perfectly "steady." Mr. Edward Connor is the proprietor, Mr. Barnes, late of the Hippodrome, is the manager, and Mr. L."-E. Beresford; 'late ef Cadishead Pictures, Ltd., will Operate. The architect is Mr. G. H. Kay (Manchester), and the builders are Messrs. Broadhurst and Sons (Warrington). Local firms have also supplied other requisites. There will be two performances nightly on Mondays and Saturdays, and on the remaining nights the performance will be continuous. At the matinees, which are on Mondays, Thurs- days and Saturdays, light refreshments will be provided.</p>
        <p>_ At the Police Court, yesterday, the music licence for the building was granted by the Bench, on the a Polication of Mr. E. A. Connor. solicitor, Warrington.</p>
