      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0225" n="art0225">
        <head>PANTOMIME AT THE ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</head>
        <p>PANTOMIME AT THE ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</p>
        <p>"Dick Whittington " is a Good Draw</p>
        <p>Another of Mr. Richard Nightingale's popular Pantomimes, "Dick Whittington," has been Presented at the Royal Court Theatre this week. The cast is practically the same as last year, and the scenic effects, as well as the artistic groupings of the ballet, are carried out on a lavish scale. The production goes with swing from start to finish, special features being excellent songs, sentimental and ragtime, and a host of rich and wholesome comedy mer- chants. Dolly Ross is as popular as ever as the principal boy.</p>
        <p>"ROBINSON CRUSOE " NEXT. A Strong, First-class Company. Miss Edith St. Clare 'will present next week, at the Royal Court Theatre, her successful pantomime revue, "Robinson Crusoe." This highly diverting production will be submitted 'by an exceptionally strong east of favourites.</p>
        <p>J. W. SALL As "The Bold, Bad Man."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0226" n="art0226">
        <p>mime is that seasoned comedian, J. W. Hall, who is an established favourite everywhere. Two other local comedians are J. Rich, of Sam Hague and Palladium Minstrel fame, who is the "Friday," also Mark Pratice as Hoppy, a comedian with a peculiar style. The special- ities are of the highest grade, headed by the well-known variety act, Miss Viviana's " Toy- shop " scoria. Included in her company is Mark Lupino, a member of that well-known family of pantomimists, who have now been before the public for 150 years. Those who remember his "Tommy, the Salesman, of the Toyshop," will know of his mirth-making qualities. With mag- nificent scenery, pretty frocks, and dainty girls, "Robinson Crusoe" should make a bold appeal to Court playgoers. There will be matinees on Monday and Saturday. The !pantomime plays twice nightly, and the box office is now open.</p>
        <p>BIG GAME HUNTING AT PICTUREDROME. A striking programme has been arranged for next week at the Picturedrome. Perhaps the ,greatest •interest will centre round. the film depicting "Seven Months' Big Game Hunting in Central Africa," which will show the sport afforded the adventurous African hunter. Lovers of thrilling drama will be catered for on Mon- day, Tuesday and Wednesday, when "Ashes" will be shown. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0227" n="art0227">
        <head>HIPPODROME PANTOMIME</head>
        <p>HIPPODROME PANTOMIME</p>
        <p>Successful Spectacular Product ion.</p>
        <p>The success of the Hippodrome management in securing only the best of variety turns is com- mon knowledge, and it is gratifying to note that when a diversion in the way of a pantomime appears, again only the best and smartest artistes are included in the combination. The gorgeous, spectacular and laughter provoking pantomime presented by E. C. Jazon and M. Montgomery this week has attracted large houses and satisfied an exacting public. Catchy music, pleasing songs, pomedy numbers, and a host of funny interludes, along with the mag- nificent and artistically arranged ballet and the beautiful scenic effects, go towards the suc- cess of the production. Miss Mand Beaumont is a vivacious and captivating principal boy, and the part of Robin Hood f's quite safe in her capable hands, her songs and mannerisms being exceptionally attractive. Miss Raie Walters makes a capital Maid Marion, and she was loudly applauded for her excellent rendering of popular songs. Chief amongst the mirth- makers is Billy Curzon, who appears as ,Mary Ellen, the maid-of-all-work. His drollery and "changes" were greeted With tumultuous ap- plause. The part of "the bold, bad baron" is played by Mr. Fred Dunstone, who is so success- ful in some of the "rowdy" humour. Mr. Clarence Tyner, as the gentleman-in-waiting, is a clever patter comedian, who has an abundance of witty gags and stories. Messrs. Goodall and Knox are excellent knockabout comedians, and have ample scope for their energies as Bubble and Squeak, who, as amateur burglars. cause roars of laughter. Mr. Dudley Hoard plays the part of the policeman in a comical manner, and, Mr. Bert Rose is also successful as Friar Tuck. The Sisters St. 'Julien, Miss Minnie Saunders and Miss Vi Saunders also acquit themselves admirably. The special features introduced by the Dandy Six, Bunny and Bogie, and the Eight Sunstars are specially attractive. An augmented orchestra supplies 'excellent musical items.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0228" n="art0228">
        <head>gut.- ; out man 7</head>
        <p>gut.- ; out man 7</p>
        <p>trestL</p>
        <p>_ des from the professor. The meeting next Monday is at Stockton Heath School, and the subject "Islam."</p>
        <p>NEW PICTURE HOUSE'S STRONG CARD.</p>
        <p>The management of tho New Picture House deserve congratulations for their enterprise in procuring the film "The Vicar of Wakefield," adapted from Oliver Goldsmith's book. This picture will be shown during the latter part of the week, and also figuring prominently will be Parts 3 and 4 of the series of "Our Navy."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0229" n="art0229">
        <head>"TESS OF THE D'URBERVILLES" AT</head>
        <p>"TESS OF THE D'URBERVILLES" AT</p>
        <p>ANDREWS'.</p>
        <p>"Tess of the D'Urbervilles " Thomas Hardy's masterpiece, is now brought into the moving picture world, and next week will be shown at Andrews' Picture Palace, the home of first-class cinema drama. If it was a masterpiece as a novel, it is a masterpiece as a picture. It grips and fires the imagination, and holds one's attention rivetted right to the finish. The murder scene, in which Tess figures, is a thril- ling incident, but there, our. readers must go and see the great play, as it will be admirably presented at Andrews popular house.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0230" n="art0230">
        <head>SOUTH BUCKS BY-ELECTION.</head>
        <p>SOUTH BUCKS BY-ELECTION.</p>
        <p>A Strong Liberal Candidate Chosen. Events in connection with the, South Bucks by-election have taken an unexpected turn. At a meeting of the South Bucks Liberal Associa- tion this week at High Wycombe, Colonel Hobaxt, the prospective Liberal candidate, with- drew from the contest on the ground that dur- ing the short time he had been before the elec- tors he had had but little opportunity acquaintance with the constituency. Mr. Tonman Mosley, a prominent South Bucks public man, was; instead chosen and consentea to contest, the seat. AS both Mr. Mosley and Mr. Du Pre, the Unionist candidate, are regarded as strong can- didates, a stiff fight is expected. Mr. Tonman Mosley only comparatively recently announced his adherence to the Liberal party, having been for many years a leading member of the South Bucks Conservative party, though always op- posed to Tariff "Reform."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0231" n="art0231">
        <head>REUNION OF SEPARATED FAMILIES 1N NEW ZEALAND.</head>
        <p>REUNION OF SEPARATED FAMILIES 1N NEW ZEALAND.</p>
        <p>As proof of the prosperity of New Zealand, it is worth recording that among the 210 re- duced fare emigrants who sailed on the 9th inst. by the Ss. Rangatira from London, there were 135 persons who had been nominated by their relatives in the Dominion. A large pro- portion of this number was made up of house- wives with families; in one instance a mother was taking as many as ten children with her to join their father in New Zealand. In many eases the father goes out first to "spy out the land," and, when he finds the conditions favourable, sends home for his wife and chil- dren to join him, for whom lie is able to arrange reduced rate passages with the immigration authorities in New Zealand.</p>
      </div>
