      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0233" n="art0233">
        <head>PEN K ErrH•</head>
        <p>PEN K ErrH•</p>
        <p>/11•••</p>
        <p>PARISI' COUNCIL MEETING.</p>
        <p>-- The Dangerous Level Crossing, at Fiddler's Ferry Station.</p>
        <p>--- A nleeting of the Parish Council took place Stocks-lane on Monday evening in the Council Schools, Murray (ca et Those present were Dr. irman), Miss TinsleY, Messrs. James James R. , Penkh. Ditchfield, an), J. G. Withinshaw, County Councillor Forshaw sent a telegram to the meeting, statino, Woodward, and Mr. E. Foster (clerk). be present. 0 ___ ___ _ that he was unable to</p>
        <p>that it is proposed to extend the The Clerk read a letter from the District Surveyor of the Liverpool Post Office stating holiday of the Sub-postelaster of I'enketh Sub- p.m. on Thursdays. Post Office by finally closing the office at one opens for public business from weekly half- . As you are no doubt aware," continued the letter, "the office re- 7 p.m. to 8 p.m., but as Thursday is the general half-holiday the amount of business transacted is comparatively small. The acceptance and delivery of tele- grams will continue as at-present, and no alter- ation will be made in arrival or despatching of mails. Before bringing I should be Council." glad to Put the matter before the the change into effect</p>
        <p>The members of the Council said they had no objection to the proposal, and the Clerk was instructed to write accordingly. A DANGEROUS CROSSING. Mr. Routledge proposed that the Council point out to the London and North-Western Railway Company the necessity for the erection of a footbridge at Fiddle's Ferry Station. It was a very dangerous crossing. The Chairman seconded, and it was unani- mously carried.</p>
        <p>The Clerk was instructed to write to the Railway Company on tke matter. _VINE ROUSE FOOTPATH.</p>
        <p>Mr. Withinshatv co-m—pl'ained of the dirty state of this path, and it was decided that Messrs. J. Ditchfield and R. Routledge form a sub-committee to see what the charge would be to cinder the path, with power to act.</p>
        <p>Mission Guild Concert.—An enjoyable evening Was spent at the Penketh Infants, bchoel on ther occasion of the 21st annual party of St. Mary's Mission Guild. There were about seventy at the tables, the number being augmented for the concert, when the Rev. J. R. Jones presided. The programme was given. by the following artistes: Misses Murray, Hilda Hall, Moston, Gladys Jones, Marjorie Murray, Mrs. Dwerry- house, Messrs. W. 11. Ireland, 11. M. Ashton, together with Masters Dyson Whitham and Frank Dowling. The Girls' Friendly Society was responsible for "Mrs. Jarley's Waxworks" and "Old Folks a Home." whilst the children from the Council School pleased with " The Suff- ragettes" and "The Tramps." The accompanists were Ntrs. John Dodds, Miss Newbold and Mr. Middlehurst. Thanks are due to Mrs. Owen, I who arranged the concert.</p>
        <p>Old Folk's Treat.—For the 27th time the aged people of Penketh and Sankey parishes were given the annual treat on Friday. Invitations were sent out to 44 men and 78 .women from Penketh, and 12 men and 20 women from Sankey. Those responding to the invitations numbered 107-72 women and 35 men. A letter regretting his unavoidable absence was received from the Rev. J. R. Jones (vicar). Those respon- sible for the tables were: Miss Tinsley, Mes- dames Tames Dodds, F. W. Gilbert, William Houghton and J. Routledge, and they were assisted by a band of willing helwr_a. The usual interesting entertainment vas given, and the "Stella,' Company of Warrington, were the I entertainers. Those taking part ere 3:essrs J. Willett (tenor), G. Sherlock (humorist), A. 'Grounds (ventriloquist), and Miss G. Hale (pianist). The President (Dr. Murray) said that Mr. J. Lewis, of Bootle (late If Penketh) had very kindly sent the men 460 z. of tobacco, and he trusted that after 'their conversations they would return to their homes with pleasant memories. On the proposition of Mr. J. T. Ambler (chairman of committee), seconded by Mr. D. R. Griffiths, the best thanks were given Ito all who had rendered assistance. A number of the players of the Penketh Tannery Prize IBrass Band kindly gave quartet selections, and, ' the entertainment over, a substantial supper was served. As the guests were leaving the men received tobacco and pipes, and the women ,parcels of groceries.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0234" n="art0234">
        <p>VICAR'S LAST WISH.</p>
        <p>INOMOPIIMI</p>
