      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0235" n="art0235">
        <p>Thelwall Parson Wanted to be Carried to Grave in Handcart.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0236" n="art0236">
        <head>, CHURCHYARD BIER USED.</head>
        <p>, CHURCHYARD BIER USED.</p>
        <p>A gloom was cast over the quaint village of fhelwall on Sunday afternoon, when the news of the'/ley. T. Pym Williamson's death was received. For a considerable time Mr. William- son, who was 71 years of age, had been in a pre- carious state of health. In February, last he underwent an operation, and recovered -suffi- ciently to be able to officiate at church. Quite recently, ,however, another operation was neces- sary, and lie had been weaker_ ever since.</p>
        <p>A.:native of the Cleveland district- of or k - shire, Mr. Williamson, who came of a long line of ministers, was the son of the Rev. Thomas Pym Williamson, vicar of Little Brickhill. Bucks, and was born on September 2nd, 1842. .lis grandfather was vicar of Guisboro', York- shire, and the parish church there contains many monuments of the family. Having re- ceived his early education at Rossall School and Marlborough College, he proceeded to Jesus College,. Cambridge, where he took the degree of B.A. in 1864. He was ordained deacon in 1867 by the Bishop of Chester, and priest' in 1868 by the Bishop of Lichfield. Having held cura- Ciefi at Sutton (Derbyshire) and Tamworth, Mr. . Williamson was presented to the living of All Saints', Thelwall, in 1869, by Mr. James Nichol- son, of Thelwall Hall, who was then the patron. The church, which had previously been in turn a manorial"chapel and a parochial chapel, became a parish church by Order in Council in 1870.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0237" n="art0237">
        <head>LOVED THE BEAUTIFUL.</head>
        <p>LOVED THE BEAUTIFUL.</p>
        <p>The artistic readily appealed to him, and he took a great pride in the beauty of his church. During his incumbency a north aisle vestry and porch were built, through the munificence of the late Mr. Henry Stanton, of Greenfield. Thelwall, and Mr. Williamson's guidance proved invaluable in the carrying out of the-, scheme. It was also largely due to his personal encouragement that the services of the church reached such a high level, for, while appropri- ately simple in character, their musical side is superior to what is found in the majority of small village churches. The enrichment of the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0238" n="art0238">
        <p>church, which is one of the most tastefully appointed in the county, always gave Mr. Wil- liamson great satisfaction. Trough his exer- tions the churchyard was enlarged by Admiral Jones-Parry. Mr. Williamson had the present schools built soon after he came to,Thelwap and he spared no pains to maintain their el ciency. By his death teachers and children have lost a true friend. He was an eloquent preacher, and, in his younger days, was in great request at the churches of Warrington and dis- trict, particularly at the harvest thanksgiving services. In the days when evensong at Thel- wall was held in the afternoon, Mr. Williamson was rarely without an invitation to preach at the evening service at some other church.</p>
        <p>I schools</p>
        <p>came to.Viehylall mantain their 'll</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0239" n="art0239">
        <head>• ARTIST. GOLFER, ANGLER.</head>
        <p>• ARTIST. GOLFER, ANGLER.</p>
        <p>One of Mr. Williamson's favourite recreations was sketching; he was remarkably clever with his pencil, lie had a natural bent for sports and games, and was, in fact, one of the best all-round sportsmen in the district—a fine shot, an enthusiastic golfer and fisherman. As a young man he played for his county at cricket. He was a member of the Royal Liverpool, West Lancashire, and Warrington Golf Clubs, and was captain of the Bowdon Golf Club 1904-5, and also captain of the Lymm Golf Club 1911_ 1912. He was most generous, and gave and did far more than could have been expected of him. He was blessed with tact—which those who were happy enough to be closely associated with him were best able to appreciate. He was broad- minded and whole-hearted in hie profession, a charming companio, a good sportsman, and deservedly popular with all who knew him. n Mr. Williamson leaves a widow, six sons and one daughter. in ostentation, Mr. Williamson left explicit dg directions that his funeral should be marked by the utmost simplicity. Tie wished for nothing more, he said, than was accorded to any of his parishioners. In a letter of in- struction he wrote: "Perhaps a hearse may be found a convenience, but a handcart covered by a pall is better, to my way of thinking."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-17_WidnesExaminer_art0240" n="art0240">
        <head>FUNERAL OF REVERENCE:</head>
        <p>FUNERAL OF REVERENCE:</p>
        <p>- —..—..--. A deep note of reverence was touched at the funeral on Thursday afternoon.. The coffin, a solid plain oak one, had been removed on a two- wheeled bier from the Vicarage the previous night, and had lain in the church all the night, so that a funeral cortege was dispensed with. When the time of the funeral approached there was -a large lumber of sympathising friends present. The Rev. W. J. Stanton officiated at the service in the churoh, and also at the grave- side.</p>
        <p>The chief mourners were: Messrs. Thomas, Frank, George Royds, Gerald, Charles and Schobel Williamson (sons), Mr. Thomas Parry (son-in-law), Mr. John Lloyd, Hatfield (brother-in-law), and Dr. Sydney Moberley. Amongst those present were the High Sheriff of Cheshire, Mr. W. Peter Rylands, Mr. Aylmer Frost, Rev. G. B. Thurston, Rev. E. C. E. Carleton, Mr. Henry Greenall, Mr. Willidm Houghton, Mr. James Houghton, Mr. P. J. Frith, Mr. T. J. Forester Smith, Mr. G. Hutch- ings, Mr. 11. Holden, Rev. H. V. Pigot, Mr. John Coxon, Mr. T. Ridgway, Mr. William Tinsley, Mr. E. W. Reynolds, Rev. G. Warburton, Mr. A. H. Hatton, Mr. 11. Norris, Mr. A. S. Bleckly, Dr. Burrowes, Mr. W. B. Cook, head master of Thelwall School. Mr. John Ditchfield, the old- est resident of the district, who remembered two previous vicars, was also present. The church was represented by Mr. T. J. Parry, vicar's warden; Mr. E. Hewitt, cople's warden, Mr. H. Shenton and Mr. T. Frampton. The bearers were Messrs. E. Hewitt, 11. %.'o- ton, H. Wright, A. Runt, A. Roebuck and A. Hampton._</p>
        <p>• Messrs. Hampton Bros., Thelwall, had charge of the funeral arrangements.</p>
      </div>
