        <p>(J, R. GIANT, Proprietoo. Pm"' LIVERPOOL.PLACE, Early Closing Day Saturday, I o'clocl</p>
        <p>WYCLIFFE P.S,A.</p>
        <p>The Rev. T.-IT.-,faTinWp-re-s-ided over a good meeting of the Wycliffe P.S.A. on Sunday. Mr. E. Lloyd Jones was the speaker, and his theme was: " What a man thinketh in his heart so is he."</p>
        <p>Thought, said the speaker, was the ruler of man, and the great ideals of life sprang from thought. So unfortunately did the evils of life. The creation of the world came from the great thought of God; the redemption of man was another great thought of God; and all the pro- visions for our highly developed natures and our being sprang from the great thought of God. Therefore they should see that their thoughts were good. If they thought righteously they would themselves be made better, their homes made better and their fellows made better. Every good thought sprang from God. Mr. McArthur sang in an impressive manner, " Nearer, my God, to Thee." Mr. Payton was the organist, and Mr. C. Cotton also took part in the meeting. A vote of sympathy was sent to two afflicted brethren, Messrs. Bond and Joynson. It was agreed that a letter should be sent to Mrs. G. J. Brown expressing the deepest sym- pathy of the P.S.A. in the great loss she and her family had sustained through the death of Mr. Brown, who - -ker at the P.S.A. for able and help-</p>
        <p>was a WOri___ _ who often gave</p>
        <p>ful addrees to the meetings.</p>
